up the carrying.\n\nLet me do this digit by digit:.\n\nStarting from the right (units):.\n\n1st digit (units): 0 + 2 = 2. So units place is 2. No carry.\n\n2nd digit (tens): 4 + 7 = 11. So write 1, carryover 1.\n\n3rd digit (hundreds): 9 + 5 = 14, plus carryover 1 = 15. So write 5, carryover 1.\n\n4th digit (thousands): 9 + 1 = 10, plus 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n5th digit (ten thousands): 2 + 0 = 2, plus 1 = 3. Write 3, no carry.\n\n6th digit (hundred thousands): 5 + 9 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\n7th digit (millions): 1 + 1 = 2, plus 1 = 3. Write 3, no carry.\n\n8th digit (ten millions): 3 + 3 = 6. Write 6, no carry.\n\n9th digit (hundred millions): 6 + 8 = 14. So here, since it's the highest digit, we just write 14. So the total would be 14 followed by the other digits.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the highest digit:.\n\n14 (hundred millions place), then 6 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands?), wait no, let's see:.\n\nWait, actually, the digits are:.\n\nThe places are from right to left as units up to hundred millions. So after adding, the digits from right to left are:.\n\nUnits: 2.\n\nTens:1.\n\nHundreds:5.\n\nThousands:1.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nHundred thousands:4.\n\nMillions:3.\n\nTen millions:6.\n\nHundred millions:14.\n\nWait, but 14 in the hundred millions place is actually 1 hundred million and 4 ten millions? Wait no, that's not right. Wait, when you have 14 in the hundred millions place, that's actually 14 hundred millions, which is 1,400,000,000. But since we can't have a digit higher than 9 in a single place, we need to carry over to the next higher place. Wait, but there is no higher place than hundred millions here unless we go to billions. Wait, so actually, the sum might be a 10-digit number. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
