ried over 1 is 7. Write 7, no carryover.\n\nTen millions place: 2 + 9 = 11. Write 1 and carry over 1.\n\nHundred millions place: 5 + 7 = 12, plus the carried over 1 makes 13. So write 3 and carry over 1. Wait, but there's no higher place, so we need to add that 1 as a new digit at the beginning.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right:.\n\nOnes:6, tens:8, hundreds:0 (with carryover), thousands:3, ten thousands:0 (with carryover), hundred thousands:5 (with carryover), millions:7, ten millions:1 (with carryover), hundred millions:3 (with carryover), and then the final carryover 1 in the billions place. .\n\nWait, let me recount the digits step by step to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Let me go through each digit again carefully.\n\nStarting over:.\n\nAligning the numbers:.\n\n```.\n        522,624,440.\n      +794,878,646.\n      -------------.\n```.\n\nLet me write them without commas for easier alignment:.\n\n522624440  .\n+794878646  .\n------------.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n1. Ones place: 0 + 6 = 6.  .\n2. Tens: 4 + 4 = 8.  .\n3. Hundreds: 4 + 6 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.  .\n4. Thousands: 4 + 8 = 12 + 1 (carryover) = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.  .\n5. Ten thousands: 2 + 7 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.  .\n6. Hundred thousands: 6 + 8 = 14 + 1 = 15. Write 5, carryover 1.  .\n7. Millions: 2 + 4 = 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7, no carryover.  .\n8. Ten millions: 2 + 9 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.  .\n9. Hundred millions: 5 + 7 = 12 + 1 (carryover) = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.  .\n10. Since there's a carryover 1 after the hundred millions place, we add a new digit to the left: 1.  .\n\nSo putting all the digits together from right to left:.\n\nThe digits we wrote are (starting from the rightmost):.\n\n6 (ones), 8 (tens), 0 (hundreds), 3 (thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 5 (hundred thousands), 7 (millions), 1 (ten millions), 3 (hundred millions), and then the final carryover 1 in the billions place.\n\nSo the total number would be 1 (billions), 3 (hundred