hundred millions place is actually two digits (1 and 6). So the total would be 16 followed by the other digits.\n\nSo the total number is 1,6 (hundred millions), then 9 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nWait, let me structure this properly:.\n\nBreaking down each digit position:.\n\n- The hundred millions place: 16. So that's 1 in the hundred millions and 6 in the ten millions? Wait, no, that's not right. Wait, actually, when adding the hundred millions place digits (8 +8 =16), that's the first digit(s) of the total. So since 16 is two digits, we need to carry over? Wait, hold on, maybe I messed up.\n\nWait, actually, when adding the hundred millions digits, which are the leftmost digits, each number has 8 in the hundred millions place. So 8 +8=16. Since there are no higher digits, that's simply 16 in the hundred millions place? But that can't be, because each digit place can only hold a single digit. Wait, so actually, when adding the hundred millions place, you have 8 +8=16, so the 6 stays in the hundred millions place, and the 1 is carried over to the next higher place, which would be billions. Since both numbers are 9 digits, their sum can be up to 17 digits (if they were both 999,999,999), but actually, adding two 9-digit numbers can give a 10-digit number at most.\n\nWait, so perhaps I made a mistake in the order of processing the digits. Let me try again, but this time write it out step by step with all columns properly.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically aligned:.\n\n          8 0 1 2 2 5 3 4 4.\n        + 8 8 9 2 9 3 0 5 3.\n        ---------------------.\nStarting from the right (units digit):.\n\nFirst digit (units): 4 + 3 = 7. Write down 7.\n\nSecond digit (tens): 4 +5 =9. Write down 9.\n\nThird digit (hundreds):3 +0 =3. Write 3.\n\nFourth digit (thousands):5 +3 =8. Write 8.\n\nFifth digit (ten thousands):2 +9 =11. Write down 1, carryover 1.\n\nSixth digit (hund