messages:  [['Please solve the math problem.\nQuestion: What is the sum of 405113796 and 730909864?', "<think>Okay, let's see, I need to add these two big numbers: 405,113,796 and 730,909,864. Hmm, adding large numbers can be tricky if I'm not careful. Let me write them down one on top of the other to align the digits properly.\n\nStarting with the rightmost digits first. The units place: 6 + 4 is 10. I'll write down 0 and carry over 1 to the tens place.\n\nNext, the tens place: 9 + 6 is 15, plus the carried over 1 makes 16. So I write 6 and carry over 1 again.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 7 + 8 is 15, plus the 1 gives me 16. Write 6, carry over 1.\n\nThousands place: 3 + 9 is 12, plus the 1 equals 13. Write 3, carry over 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 1 + 0 is 1, plus the carried 1 makes 2. So just 2 there, no carryover.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 1 + 9 is 10. Hmm, wait, let me check the digits again. Wait, maybe I misaligned the numbers. Let me confirm each digit's place.\n\nWait, maybe I should write it out step by step more carefully. Let me list each number with their digits separated by commas to make sure alignment is correct.\n\nFirst number: 405,113,796. Breaking it down:.\n\n- Billions place: 4.\n- Hundred millions: 0.\n- Ten millions: 5.\n- Millions: 1.\n- Hundred thousands: 1.\n- Ten thousands: 3.\n- Thousands: 7.\n- Hundreds: 9.\n- Tens: 6.\n- Units: 4? Wait, no, wait. Wait, commas are placed every three digits from the right. So 405,113,796 is actually:.\n\nBreaking it down:.\n\n4 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions?), Wait, maybe I need to parse the number properly. Let's see: 405,113,796. The first comma after three digits from the right? Wait, no, in standard notation, commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. So 405,113,796 is 405 (million), 113 (thousand), 796. So the digits are:.\n\n4 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (units).