no, 864 would be the last three digits. Wait, 730,909,864:.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\n7 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 4 (units). So each digit:.\n\nPosition (from right, starting at 1):.\n\n1: units: 4.\n\n2: tens: 6.\n\n3: hundreds: 8.\n\n4: thousands: 9.\n\n5: ten thousands: 0.\n\n6: hundred thousands: 9.\n\n7: millions: 0.\n\n8: ten millions: 3.\n\n9: hundred millions:7.\n\nSo aligning both numbers:.\n\nFirst number (405,113,796):.\n\nPosition 1 (units):6.\n\n2 (tens):9.\n\n3 (hundreds):7.\n\n4 (thousands):3.\n\n5 (ten thousands):1.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):1.\n\n7 (millions):5.\n\n8 (ten millions):0.\n\n9 (hundred millions):4.\n\nSecond number (730,909,864):.\n\nPosition 1 (units):4.\n\n2 (tens):6.\n\n3 (hundreds):8.\n\n4 (thousands):9.\n\n5 (ten thousands):0.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):9.\n\n7 (millions):0.\n\n8 (ten millions):3.\n\n9 (hundred millions):7.\n\nSo now, adding each column from right to left:.\n\nStarting at position 1 (units):.\n\n6 + 4 = 10. Write down 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\n9 + 6 = 15 + 1 (carryover) = 16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):.\n\n7 + 8 = 15 + 1 = 16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):.\n\n3 + 9 = 12 + 1 =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):.\n\n1 + 0 =1 +1 (carryover) =2. Write 2, no carryover.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n1 +9 =10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):.\n\n5 +0 =5 +1 (carryover) =6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):.\n\n0 +3 =3. Write 3.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):.\n\n4 +7 =11. Write 11.\n\nPutting all together, starting from the right:.\n\nPositions 1-9:.\n\nPosition 1:0.\n\n2:6.\n\n3:6.\n\n4:3.\n\n5:2.\n\n6:0.\n\n7:6.\n\n8:3.\n\n9:11.\n\nWait, but the hundred millions place is the 9th digit, so when we get 11 there, that would actually be 1 in the hundred millions place and 1 carried over to a new billion place? Wait, hold on. Wait, the original numbers are both 9-digit numbers, so adding them could result in a 10-digit number. Let me see.\n\nWait, in position 9 (hundred millions place), adding 4 +7 =11, so that's 1 in the hundred millions and carryover 1 to the billions place (which is the 10th digit). So we have to include that.\n\nSo let me track the digits properly:.\n\nStarting from position 1 (units):.\n\nAfter each step:.\n\nPosition 1: 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 2: 6, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 3:6, carryover1.\n\nPosition4:3, carryover1.\n\nPosition5:2, no carryover.\n\nPosition6:0, carryover1.\n\nPosition7:6, no carryover.\n\nPosition8:3, no carryover.\n\nPosition9:1 (from 4+7=11, plus no carryover here? Wait wait, let's retrace.\n\nWait, when adding position 9 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is 4, second is7. So 4+7=11. But before that, was there a carryover from position8?.\n\nWait, position8 is ten millions place. The digits there were 0 and3, plus any carryover?.\n\nLet me go step by step again carefully:.\n\nStarting over:.\n\nPosition1 (units):.\n\n6 +4 =10. Write 0, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):.\n\n9+6=15 +1=16. Write6, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\n7 +8=15 +1=16. Write6, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\n3 +9=12 +1=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\n1 +0=1 +1=2. Write2, no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n1 +9=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\n5 +0=5 +1=6. Write6, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\n0 +3=3 +0=3. Write3, no carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\n4 +7=11. Since there's no carryover from position8, so 11. So here, we write down 1 and carryover1 to the next (10th) digit, which is billions place.\n\nBut since both original numbers are 9-digit, the sum can be 10 digits. So the total would be 1 (billion) plus the rest.\n\nSo the digits from position9 to 1 are:.\n\nPosition10 (billions):1 (carryover).\n\nPosition9:1 (from 4+7=11).\n\nPosition8:3.\n\nPosition7:6.\n\nPosition6:0.\n\nPosition5:2.\n\nPosition4:3.\n\nPosition3:6.\n\nPosition2:6.\n\nPosition1:0.\n\nSo writing them all together:.\n\nBillions place:1,.\n\nHundred millions:1,.\n\nTen millions:3,.\n\nMillions:6,.\n\nHundred thousands:0,.\n\nTen thousands:2,.\n\nThousands:3,.\n\nHundreds:6,.\n\nTens:6,.\n\nUnits:0. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
