4.\n\nPosition5:9.\n\nPosition6:6.\n\nPosition7:3.\n\nPosition8:3.\n\nPosition9:4.\n\nWait, so the total number would be:.\n\nStarting from Position9 to Position1: 4 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),4 (hundreds),5 (tens),0 (ones).\n\nWait, that seems a bit confusing. Let me write it out step by step:.\n\nThe result digits from each position:.\n\nAfter adding, the digits from right to left (positions 1 to 9):.\n\n0,5,4,4,9,6,3,3,4.\n\nTherefore, when written as a number, starting from the leftmost digit (position9) to the rightmost (position1):.\n\n4 (hundred millions place), then 3 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),4 (hundreds),5 (tens),0 (ones).\n\nSo putting those together: 433,694,450?.\n\nWait, let me check that again.\n\nWait, let me list each digit's place:.\n\nPosition9: 4 (hundred millions).\n\nPosition8:3 (ten millions).\n\nPosition7:3 (millions).\n\nPosition6:6 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition5:9 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition4:4 (thousands).\n\nPosition3:4 (hundreds).\n\nPosition2:5 (tens).\n\nPosition1:0 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 4 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),4 (hundreds),5 (tens),0 (ones). So that would be 433,694,450.\n\nWait, but let me verify that. Let me add the numbers again step by step, maybe I made a mistake in the positions.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I should use another method, like breaking the numbers into parts.\n\nAlternatively, maybe adding them as whole numbers:.\n\n246,666,704.\n\n+180,727,746.\n\nLet me add the millions first. 246,666,704 is 246 million plus 666 thousand 704. The second number is 180 million plus 727 thousand 746.\n\nAdding the millions: 246,000,000 + 180,000,000 = 426,000,000.\n\nThen the thousands and the rest:.\n\n666,704 + 727,746.\n\nAdding those two: 666,704 + 700,000 is 1,366,704, but then adding the remaining 27