hen the carried-over 1 at the beginning. Wait, let me check the digits again step by step to make sure I didn't mix up any places.\n\nWait, maybe I should write it down digit by digit properly:.\n\nLet me line them up:.\n\n       8 6 7 5 3 6 9 1 0.\n     + 8 1 7 0 2 1 9 6 3.\n     --------------------.\n\nStarting from the right (units place):.\n\n0 + 3 = 3  .\n1 (tens) + 6 = 7  .\n9 (hundreds) + 9 = 18 → write 8, carryover 1  .\n6 (thousands) + 1 (from the next number's thousands place) + 1 (carryover) = 8  .\n3 (ten thousands) + 2 = 5  .\n5 (hundred thousands) + 0 = 5  .\n7 (millions) + 7 (millions place of second number) = 14 → write 4, carryover 1  .\n6 (ten millions) + 1 (ten millions of second number) + 1 (carryover) = 8  .\n8 (hundred millions) + 8 (hundred millions of second number) = 16 → write 6, carryover 1  .\n\nThen the final carryover 1 becomes the next digit (billions place). So the total digits would be 1 followed by 6, then 8, 4, 5, 5, 8, 8, 7, 3. Wait, maybe I need to list them in order.\n\nWait, let me do each digit step by step again carefully:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (position 1):.\n\nPosition 1 (units): 0 + 3 = 3  .\nPosition 2 (tens): 1 + 6 = 7  .\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 9 + 9 = 18 → write 8, carryover 1  .\nPosition 4 (thousands): 6 + 1 + 1 (carryover) = 8  .\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 3 + 2 = 5  .\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 5 + 0 = 5  .\nPosition 7 (millions): 7 + 7 = 14 → write 4, carryover 1  .\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 6 + 1 + 1 (carryover) = 8  .\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 8 + 8 = 16 → write 6, carryover 1  .\nPosition 10 (billions): 1 (carryover). Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
