.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\nFirst number: 8 6 2 6 1 8 7 4 7\n\nSecond number:2 7 3 5 4 5 8 3 0.\n\nAligning them:\n\nBillions: 8 vs 2 \u2192 but actually, they are both 9-digit numbers, so the first digit is hundred millions place. So when adding, the highest digit is hundred millions, but the sum can lead to a 10-digit number.\n\nWait let's go step by step again:\n\nStarting from the right (units):\n\n1. Units:7 + 0 =7. So units digit is 7.\n\n2. Tens:4 +3=7. Tens digit 7.\n\n3. Hundreds:7 (from first) +8 (from second) =15. So hundreds digit is 5, carryover 1.\n\n4. Thousands:8 (first) +5 (second) =13 +1 (carryover) =14 \u2192 thousands digit 4, carryover1.\n\n5. Ten thousands:1 (first) +4 (second) =5 +1 (carryover) =6. Ten thousands digit is 6, no carryover.\n\n6. Hundred thousands:6 (first) +5 (second)=11 \u2192 hundred thousands digit is1, carryover1.\n\n7. Millions:2 (first) +3 (second)=5 +1 (carryover)=6 \u2192 millions digit 6, no carryover.\n\n8. Ten millions:6 (first) +7 (second)=13 \u2192 ten millions digit 3, carryover1.\n\n9. Hundred millions:8 (first) +2 (second)=10 +1 (carryover)=11 \u2192 hundred millions digit 1, carryover1.\n\n10. Billions place: carryover1 \u2192 so billions digit is1.\n\nSo combining all digits from billions down:\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:1.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nHundred thousands:1.\n\nTen thousands:6.\n\nThousands:4.\n\nHundreds:5.\n\nTens:7.\n\nUnits:7.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, in position 6 (hundred thousands place), the digit was 1. Then position7 (millions) is 6, position8 (ten millions) 3, position9 (hundred millions)1, and then the carryover.\n\nWait, so putting all together:\n\nBillions (position10):1.\n\nHundred millions (position9):1.\n\nTen millions (position8):3.\n\nMillions (position7):6.\n\nHundred thousands (position6):1.\n\nTen thousands (position5):6.\n\nThousands (position4):4.\n\nHundreds (position3):5.\n\nTens (position2):7.\n\nUnits (position1):7.\n\nSo the number is 1,136,164,577? Wait,