digit is 4, no carryover here.\n\nHundred thousands place: 9 (from first number) + 0 (from second number) = 9. So that's 9.\n\nMillions place: 0 (from first number) + 3 (from second number) = 3.\n\nTen millions place: 0 (from first number) + 3 (from second number) = 3.\n\nHundred millions place: 1 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) = 3.\n\nPutting all the digits together from right to left, the sum should be 3 3 3 9 4 4 7 6 1. Wait, hold on, maybe I got the order wrong when writing the digits. Let me reconstruct the number properly.\n\nWait, the digits we wrote down, starting from the ones place:.\n\nOnes: 1.\n\nTens: 6.\n\nHundreds:4.\n\nThousands:4 (with a carryover from the thousands place).\n\nTen thousands:4.\n\nHundred thousands:9.\n\nMillions:3.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nHundred millions:3.\n\nWait, perhaps I should list them in order from highest to lowest place value. Let me see.\n\nStarting from the left (the highest place):.\n\nHundred millions: 3.\n\nTen millions: 3.\n\nMillions: 3.\n\nHundred thousands: 9.\n\nTen thousands:4.\n\nThousands:4.\n\nHundreds:4.\n\nTens:6.\n\nOnes:1.\n\nWait, that might not be correct. Let me think again. The positions are as follows:.\n\nEach number has 9 digits. Let me write the positions:.\n\nFirst number: 1 0 0 9 0 7 0 1 1.\n\nPositions (from left to right):.\n\n1: Hundred millions.\n\n0: Ten millions.\n\n0: Millions.\n\n9: Hundred thousands.\n\n0: Ten thousands.\n\n7: Thousands.\n\n0: Hundreds.\n\n1: Tens.\n\n1: Ones.\n\nSecond number: 2 3 3 0 3 7 4 5 0.\n\nPositions:.\n\n2: Hundred millions.\n\n3: Ten millions.\n\n3: Millions.\n\n0: Hundred thousands.\n\n3: Ten thousands.\n\n7: Thousands.\n\n4: Hundreds.\n\n5: Tens.\n\n0: Ones.\n\nAdding digit by digit:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones):.\n\nOnes: 1 + 0 =1.\n\nTens:1 +5=6.\n\nHundreds:0 +4=4.\n\nThousands:7 +7=14 (write 4, carryover 1).\n\nTen thousands:0 +3 +1(carry)=4.\n\nHundred thousands:9 +0=9.\n\nMillions:0 +3=3.\n\nTen millions:0 +3=3.\n\nHundred millions:1 +2=3.\n\nSo the digits from right to left (starting from ones) are 1,6,4,4,4,9,3,3,3. Wait, but when constructing the number, we need to arrange them in the correct order from highest to lowest place. So the highest is the hundred millions place (3), then ten millions (3), millions (3), hundred thousands (9), ten thousands (4), thousands (4), hundreds (4), tens (6), ones (1). .\n\nSo the number is 3 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 1 (ones). Therefore, my final answer is: "]] 19%|#9        | 96/500 [2:56:18<13:10:22, 117.38s/it]
