\nDigit6: hundred thousands (position6)\n\nDigit7: millions (position7)\n\nDigit8: ten millions (position8)\n\nDigit9: hundred millions (position9)\n\nDigit10: billions (position10) but only if there is a 10th digit.\n\nSo for 531,562,313:\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):5\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):3\n\nDigit7 (millions):1\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):5\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):6\n\nDigit4 (thousands):2\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):3\n\nDigit2 (tens):1\n\nDigit1 (ones):3\n\nWait that can't be, because 531,562,313 written out is:\n\n5 (hundred millions),\n\n3 (ten millions),\n\n1 (millions),\n\n5 (hundred thousands),\n\n6 (ten thousands),\n\n2 (thousands),\n\n3 (hundreds),\n\n1 (tens),\n\n3 (ones).\n\nWait that seems inconsistent. Wait let me think:\n\nThe number is 531,562,313.\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n- The first comma after three digits from the right: so 531,562,313 is split as 531 (million), 562 (thousand), 313 (units).\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The first part, 531, is the millions: 531,000,000.\n\nWithin that, 5 is the hundred millions (500,000,000),\n\n3 is the ten millions (30,000,000),\n\n1 is the millions (1,000,000).\n\nThen the next comma separates the thousands: 562,000.\n\n5 is the hundred thousands (500,000),\n\n6 is the ten thousands (60,000),\n\n2 is the thousands (2,000).\n\nThe last part is 313: 3 hundreds, 1 ten, 3 ones.\n\nSo the full breakdown:\n\nDigit positions from left (billions place) to right (ones):\n\n1. 5 (hundred millions place)\n\n2. 3 (ten millions place)\n\n3. 1 (millions place)\n\n4. 5 (hundred thousands place)\n\n5. 6 (ten thousands place)\n\n6. 2 (thousands place)\n\n7. 3 (hundreds place)\n\n8. 1 (tens place)\n\n9. 3 (ones place)\n\nWait that makes sense now. So each digit's place is:\n\nPosition1 (leftmost): hundred millions (5),\n\nPosition2: ten millions (3),\n\nPosition3: millions (1),\n\nPosition4: hundred thousands (5),\n\nPosition5: ten thousands (6),\n\nPosition6: thousands (2),\n\nPosition7: hundreds (3),\n\nPosition8: tens (1),\n\nPositi