rryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst:1, second:2 \u21921+2=3 +1=4. Write4, carryover0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst:3, second:8 \u21923+8=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst:5, second:7 \u21925+7=12 +1=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\nNow, we have a carryover of1 from the hundred millions addition, which goes into a new Position10 (billions place).\n\nThus, the total digits from Position1 to Position10 are:\n\nPosition10:1,\n\nPosition9:3,\n\nPosition8:1,\n\nPosition7:4,\n\nPosition6:4,\n\nPosition5:3,\n\nPosition4:2,\n\nPosition3:5,\n\nPosition2:6,\n\nPosition1:0.\n\nArranged from highest to lowest (Position10 to Position1), that is:\n\n1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,314,432,560.\n\nWait, let me confirm:\n\nBreaking down the result:\n\nBillions digit:1,\n\nHundred millions:3,\n\nTen millions:1,\n\nMillions:4,\n\nHundred thousands:4,\n\nTen thousands:3,\n\nThousands:2,\n\nHundreds:5,\n\nTens:6,\n\nOnes:0.\n\nTherefore, the number is 1,314,432,560.\n\nAh! So my first calculation was incorrect because I messed up the positions when reconstructing, but the second method using splitting gave me 1,314,432,560, and the corrected step-by-step addition also gives that. So the correct answer should be 1,314,432,560.\n\nWait let me verify using another method, perhaps calculator-like.\n\nAdding 531,562,313 and 782,870,247.\n\nLet me add the numbers step by step:\n\n531,562,313\n\n+782,870,247\n\nStart with 531,562,313 + 700,000,000 = 1,231,562,313.\n\nThen add 82,870,247.\n\nAdding 1,231,562,313 + 80,000,000 = 1,311,562,313.\n\nThen add 2,870,247:\n\n1,311,562,313 + 2,000,000 = 1,313,562,313.\n\nThen +870,247:\n\n1,313,562,313 + 800,000 = 1,314,362,313,\n\nThen +70,247: 1,314,362,313 +70,000 =1,314,432,313,\n\nThen +247: 1,314,432,313 +247 =1,314,432,560.\n\nYes, that matches the previous 