lions), 2 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 7 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nAdding column by column from right to left:.\n\nOnes: 7 + 3 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 6 + 7 = 13 + 1 =14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 4 +7 =11 +1=12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nThousands:6 +8=14 +1=15. Write 5, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:3 +0=3 +1=4. Write4, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands:3 +1=4. Write4.\n\nMillions:2 +6=8. Write8.\n\nTen millions:1 +7=8. Write8.\n\nHundred millions:4 +7=11. Write1 and carryover1. Since there are no more digits, the carryover1 becomes a new digit at the front.\n\nSo putting all together, starting from the right:.\n\nThe digits are 0 (ones),4 (tens),2 (hundreds),5 (thousands),4 (ten thousands),4 (hundred thousands),8 (millions),8 (ten millions),1 (hundred millions), and the carried over 1 in the billions place. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
