 3 3 2.\n\nWait, actually, let me count the digits properly. Let's see:.\n\n386,392,332 – that's 9 digits. Let me separate them:.\n\nDigits (from left to right):.\n\n3 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 2 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 491,799,586 – also 9 digits:.\n\n4 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nSo when adding, let's write them vertically:.\n\n\u2003\u20033 8 6 3 9 2 3 3 2.\n\n+\xa04 9 1 7 9 9 5 8 6.\n\n----------------------------.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 2 + 6 = 8. Write 8.\n\nTens: 3 + 8 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 3 + 5 = 8, plus carryover 1 = 9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nThousands: 2 + 9 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands: 9 + 9 = 18, plus 1 = 19. Write 9, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands: 3 + 7 = 10, plus 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nMillions: 6 + 1 = 7, plus 1 = 8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nTen millions: 8 + 9 = 17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions: 3 + 4 = 7, plus 1 = 8. Write 8. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
