nLet me list the digits with their positions:.\n\nFirst number: 7 (100,000,000), 8 (10,000,000), 6 (1,000,000), 2 (100,000), 4 (10,000), 0 (1,000), 0 (100), 4 (10), 4 (1).\n\nSecond number:8 (100,000,000),8 (10,000,000),5 (1,000,000),3 (100,000),5 (10,000),8 (1,000),3 (100),3 (10),9 (1).\n\nWait, maybe it's better to write each digit's place:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nPosition 1 (units): 4 +9 =13.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):4 +3 =7, plus carryover 1 makes 8?.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to make a table.\n\nLet me write the digits vertically:.\n\nFirst number: 7 8 6 2 4 0 0 4 4.\n\nSecond number:8 8 5 3 5 8 3 3 9.\n\nAligning each digit:.\n\nBillions place (position 9): 7 +8.\n\nHundred millions (position8):8 +8.\n\nTen millions (position7):6 +5.\n\nMillions (position6):2 +3.\n\nHundred thousands (position5):4 +5.\n\nTen thousands (position4):0 +8.\n\nThousands (position3):0 +3.\n\nHundreds (position2):0 +3.\n\nTens (position1):4 +9.\n\nWait, actually, the positions are counted from right to left, but the numbers are written left to right. Hmm, perhaps I'm mixing up.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's easier to write each digit in columns:.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically with each digit's place:.\n\nLet me think of each digit as columns from right (units) to left (billions):.\n\nSo the rightmost digit is units (position 1), then tens (2), hundreds (3), thousands (4), ten thousands (5), hundred thousands (6), millions (7), ten millions (8), hundred millions (9), billions (10). Wait, but since both numbers are 9 digits, the leftmost digit is hundred millions (position9) for each, so when adding, the total might go up to 10 digits.\n\nWait, this is getting confusing. Let me try again step by step.\n\nLet me write the two numbers:.\n\nFirst number: 786,240,044.\n\nSecond number: 885,358,339.\n\nLet me write them with spaces to separate the digits:.\n\nFirst number: 7 8 6 2 4 0 0 4 4.\n\nSecond number:8 8 5 3 5 8 3 3 9.\n\nNow, aligning each digit:.\n\nStarting from the ri