ns), 8 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the places when adding earlier. Let me try again step by step, writing each digit's place properly.\n\nLet me line them up properly:.\n\nFirst number: 625,804,415.\nBreakdown:.\n- Billions: 0 (since it's less than a billion).\n- Hundred millions: 6.\n- Ten millions: 2.\n- Millions: 5.\n- Hundred thousands: 8.\n- Ten thousands: 0.\n- Thousands: 4.\n- Hundreds: 4.\n- Tens: 1.\n- Ones: 5.\n\nSecond number: 886,801,296.\nBreakdown:.\n- Billions: 0 (still less than a billion? Wait, 886 million is 0.886 billion, so yes).\n- Hundred millions: 8.\n- Ten millions: 8.\n- Millions: 6.\n- Hundred thousands: 8.\n- Ten thousands: 0.\n- Thousands: 1.\n- Hundreds: 2.\n- Tens: 9.\n- Ones: 6.\n\nAdding each column from right to left:.\n\nOnes place: 5 + 6 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTens place: 1 + 9 = 10, plus 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place: 4 + 2 = 6, plus 1 = 7. Write 7, no carryover.\n\nThousands place: 4 + 1 = 5. Write 5.\n\nTen thousands place: 0 + 0 = 0. Write 0.\n\nHundred thousands place: 8 + 8 = 16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nMillions place: 5 + 6 = 11, plus 1 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nTen millions place: 2 + 8 = 10, plus 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions place: 6 + 8 = 14, plus 1 = 15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\nBillions place: 0 + 0 = 0, plus 1 = 1. So write 1.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the billions place down:.\n\n1 (billion), 5 (hundred million), 1 (ten million), 2 (million), 6 (hundred thousand), 0 (ten thousand), 5 (thousand), 7 (hundred), 1 (ten), 1 (one). Wait, let me check the order again.\n\nWait, the digits we wrote are:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, after adding each column, the digits are:.\n\nOnes: 1.\n\nTens: 1.\n\nHundreds:7.\n\nThousands:5.\n\nTen thousands:0.\n\nHundred thousands:6.\n\nMillions:2.\n\nTen millions:1.\n\nHundred millions:5.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nSo arranging from highest to lowest place value, it's:.\n\n1 (billion), 5 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 1 (ones). .\n\nWait, let me structure this properly. The total number would be:. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
