e millions digit is the 7th from right, which is 6.\n\nTherefore adding millions digits:5 (from first) +6 (from second)=11, plus the carryover1 from the hundred thousands place? Wait, wait in digit-wise addition earlier:\n\nWait, when I was doing the digit-wise addition, after adding the hundred thousands place (position 6):\n\n8 (first number) +8 (second number) =16, so we write 6 and carryover1 to the millions place.\n\nThen the millions place (position7):\n\n5 +6 =11, plus the carryover1 gives 12 \u2192 so 2 with carryover1.\n\nTherefore, the millions digit is 2, and the total so far up to that place is 12, but that's correct.\n\nThen ten millions place (position8): 2 (first number) +8 (second) =10, plus carryover1=11 \u21921 with carryover1.\n\nHundred millions place (position9):6 +8=14 +1=15 \u21925 and carryover1.\n\nBillions: 0+0+1=1.\n\nSo the total is 1 (billion), 5 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),7 (hundreds),1 (tens),1 (ones).\n\nSo that is 1,512,606,711.\n\nWait, but in the other method, it's 1,512,605,711. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait in the breakdown method, the smaller parts added to 1,605,711, which is 1,605,711. When adding to 1,511,000,000, that would be 1,511,000,000 +1,605,711=1,512,605,711.\n\nWait, but according to digit-wise, it should be 1,512,606,711. Hmm, so difference of 1,000.\n\nWait, perhaps there's an error in the breakdown. Let me check:\n\nWait, if first number is 625,804,415, that is 625 million +804 thousand 415.\n\nSecond number is 886,801,296 which is 886 million plus 801 thousand 296.\n\nWait, 804,415 is 804 thousand 415, and 801,296 is 801 thousand 296. Adding those two:\n\n804,000 +801,000 =1,605,000.\n\n415+296 =711.\n\nSo total is 1,605,000 +711=1,605,711. Correct.\n\nAdding to 625+886=1,511 million gives total 1,511,000,000 +1,605,711 =1,512,605,711.\n\nHmm. So where is the conflict? The digit-wise addition must be wrong then. Let me recompute th