ncy is in the breakdown method. Let me check where that went wrong.\n\nWait when I broke it into 625 million and 886 million, adding to 1,511 million, then adding the remaining parts 804,415 +801,296 =1,605,711.\n\nWait but 1,511,000,000 +1,605,711 =1,512,605,711.\n\nBut the digit-wise gives 1,512,606,711. So there is a difference of 1,000.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in splitting the numbers?\n\nWait first number is 625,804,415. Let's see:\n\n625,804,415 = 625,000,000 + 804,415. Correct.\n\nSecond number:886,801,296=886,000,000+801,296. Correct.\n\nAdding the millions:\n\n625,000,000 +886,000,000= 1,511,000,000. Correct.\n\nAdding the smaller parts:\n\n804,415 +801,296.\n\nWait, 804,415 is 804 thousand 415, and 801,296 is 801 thousand 296.\n\nAdding those:\n\n804,000 +801,000=1,605,000.\n\n415+296=711.\n\nTotal:1,605,000+711=1,605,711. Correct.\n\nSo 1,511,000,000 +1,605,711=1,512,605,711.\n\nHmm, so why is the digit-wise addition different? There must be an error in the digit-wise calculation.\n\nWait, let me do the digit-wise addition again, this time writing down each digit's place value.\n\nLet me write the numbers:\n\nFirst number (625,804,415):\n\n- Billions:0\n\n- Hundred millions:6\n\n- Ten millions:2\n\n- Millions:5\n\n- Hundred thousands:8\n\n- Ten thousands:0\n\n- Thousands:4\n\n- Hundreds:4\n\n- Tens:1\n\n- Ones:5\n\nSecond number (886,801,296):\n\n- Billions:0\n\n- Hundred millions:8\n\n- Ten millions:8\n\n- Millions:6\n\n- Hundred thousands:8\n\n- Ten thousands:0\n\n- Thousands:1\n\n- Hundreds:2\n\n- Tens:9\n\n- Ones:6\n\nAdding each column:\n\nOnes:5+6=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nTens:1+9=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:4+2=6+1=7 \u21927, carryover0.\n\nThousands:4+1=5 \u21925, carryover0.\n\nTen thousands:0+0=0 \u21920.\n\nHundred thousands:8+8=16 \u21926, carryover1.\n\nMillions:5+6=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:2+8=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:6+8=14 +1=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nBillions:0+0 +1=