ite 2, carryover 0.\n\nHundred millions place:3 (first) +4 (second)=7. Write 7, no carryover.\n\nSo putting all the digits together:.\n\nStarting from the left (hundred millions):.\n\n7 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 4 (millions), then 9 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 4 (ones). Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, the digits from each step:.\n\nHundred millions place: 3 +4 =7, no carryover beyond that.\n\nTen millions place: 1 +0 +1 (carryover from millions?) Wait, the ten millions place calculation was 1 +0 +1 (carryover from millions place addition which was 14, so carried over 1). So 1+0+1=2.\n\nMillions place: 9 +4 +1 (carryover from hundred thousands?) Wait, the hundred thousands place had a carryover of 1, so the millions place addition was 9 (first number's millions) +4 (second number's millions) plus the carryover 1 from the hundred thousands place, which gave 14. So that's where the 4 and carryover 1 to ten millions.\n\nSo the digits from left to right:.\n\nHundred millions:7, ten millions:2, millions:4, hundred thousands:9, ten thousands:1, thousands:9, hundreds:2, tens:8, ones:4.\n\nWait, let me reconstruct the number step by step:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:.\n\nOnes:4.\n\nTens:8.\n\nHundreds:2.\n\nThousands:9.\n\nTen thousands:1.\n\nHundred thousands:9.\n\nMillions:4.\n\nTen millions:2.\n\nHundred millions:7. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
