Since there are no billions digits in the original numbers, the total would be 1 (billion) 1 (hundred million).. Wait, so actually, the total is 1,160, something?.\n\nWait, let me retrace step by step:.\n\nLet me do the addition step by step carefully:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:.\n\nOnes place:.\n0 + 7 = 7. Write down 7.\n\nTens place:.\n9 +4 =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place:.\n1 (from first number) +2 (from second) +1 (carryover) =4. Write 4, no carryover.\n\nThousands place:.\n7 (first) +3 (second) =10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place:.\n4 (first) +8 (second) =12 +1 (carryover) =13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands place:.\n4 (first) +5 (second) =9 +1 (carryover) =10. Write 0, carryover1.\n\nMillions place:.\n0 (first) +0 (second) +1 (carryover) =1. Write 1, no carryover.\n\nTen millions place:.\n4 (first) +2 (second) =6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nHundred millions place:.\n8 (first) +3 (second) =11. Write 11, but since we can't have two digits here, that means we need to carryover 1 to the billions place. So the hundred millions place becomes 1, and we carryover 1 to the billions.\n\nSo the total is 1 (billion) 1 (hundred millions), then the rest of the digits.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nHundred millions:1.\n\nTen millions:6.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nHundred thousands:0.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nThousands:0.\n\nHundreds:4.\n\nTens:3.\n\nOnes:7.\n\nSo the number is 1,160, (wait, let's see):.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:1 → so 1,1..\n\nThen ten millions:6 → 1,160..\n\nMillions:1 → 1,161..\n\nHundred thousands:0 → 1,161,0..\n\nTen thousands:3 → 1,161,03..\n\nThousands:0 → 1,161,030..\n\nHundreds:4 → 1,161,0304?.\n\nWait, maybe I need to structure the number properly. Let's see:.\n\nThe digits from left to right after adding are:.\n\nBillions digit:1.\n\nHundred millions:1.\n\nTen millions:6.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nHundred thousands:0.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nThousands:0.\n\nHundreds:4.