ver0.\n\nHundred millions:4+8=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\nBillions:0+0=0 +1=1. Write1.\n\nThus combining all digits from billions down:1 (billions),2 (hundred millions),9 (ten millions),7 (millions),4 (hundred thousands),0 (ten thousands),0 (thousands),2 (hundreds),0 (tens),7 (units).\n\nWait, so that's:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:2,\n\nTen millions:9,\n\nMillions:7,\n\nHundred thousands:4,\n\nTen thousands:0,\n\nThousands:0,\n\nHundreds:2,\n\nTens:0,\n\nUnits:7.\n\nTherefore, the number is 1,297,040,207.\n\nBut in the second method, I got 1,297,400,207. The difference is in the hundred thousands place: 4 vs 4? Wait, no. Wait, in the second method, when adding the thousands parts, the 344,240 +55,967 is 399,207. That is 399 thousand 207. So 399 thousand is 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands). Therefore, adding that to the millions:\n\nTotal millions:1,297,000,000 + 399,000 =1,297,399,000, plus 207 gives 1,297,399,207? Wait no, 1,297,000,000 + 399,000 is 1,297,399,000. Then adding 207: 1,297,399,207. Wait, but earlier I thought it was 1,297,400,207. Maybe I miscalculated that.\n\nWait, let me do 344,240 +55,967 again:\n\nStarting with 344,240 +55,967.\n\nAdding units digits:\n\n0 +7 =7.\n\nTens:4 +6 =10 \u2192 0 with carryover1.\n\nHundreds:2 +9 =11 +1 =12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nThousands:4 +5 =9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:4 (from 344,240's ten thousands digit is 4) plus 5 (from 55,967's ten thousands digit is 5) \u21924+5=9 plus carryover1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:3 (from 344,240) plus 0 (since 55,967 has 0 in hundred thousands?) Wait, 55,967 is 0 hundred thousands, 5 ten thousands, 5 thousands, etc.\n\nWait, 55,967 is:\n\nDigit breakdown:.\n\n5 (ten thousands),\n\n5 (thousands),\n\n9 (hundreds),\n\n6 (tens),\n\n7 (units).\n\nWait, so in the hundred thousands place, it's 0.\n\nSo the hundred thousands digit for the second number in the 344,240 +55,967 sum is 3 (from first number) +0 (from second) plus carryover1 (from ten thousands column which had 4+5+1=10) \u21923+0+1=4.\n\nWait, that's confusing. Let me do it step by step.\n\nAdding 344,240 and 55,967:\n\n344,240\n\n+ 55,967\n\n------------\n\nLet's write them aligned:\n\n344,240\n\n+\u00a055,967\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits:0+7=7.\n\nTens:4+6=10, write 0, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:2+9=11 +1=12, write2, carryover1.\n\nThousands:4 (from 344,240's thousands place) +5 (from 55,967's thousands place) =9 +1=10, write0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:4 (from 344,240's ten thousands) +5 (from 55,967's ten thousands) =9 +1=10, write0, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:3 (from 344,240's hundred thousands place) +0 (since 55,967 has no hundred thousands) =3 +1 (carryover) =4.\n\nSo the result is 399,207.\n\nWait, so 344,240 +55,967 = 399,207. So that is 399 thousand 207.\n\nTherefore, adding to the millions: 417,000,000 +880,000,000 =1,297,000,000.\n\nThen adding the thousands and units parts: 399,207.\n\nSo total is 1,297,000,000 + 399,207 =1,297,399,207.\n\nWait, so that is 1,297,399,207.\n\nBut the first method gave me 1,297,040,207. These are different.\n\nHmm, so clearly an error in one of the methods. Let's see where.\n\nFirst, in the first method, when adding the hundred thousands place:\n\nFirst number:3 (hundred thousands digit).\n\nSecond number:0 (from the second number's hundred thousands digit).\n\nPlus carryover1 from the ten thousands column (which had 4+5 + previous carryover=10).\n\nSo 3+0 +1=4, so that's correct. So the hundred thousands digit is4.\n\nIn the second method, the result is 399,207 added to 1,297,000,000 gives 1,297,399,207. So the breakdown is:\n\n1,297,000,000\n\n+\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0399,207\n\n=1,297,399,207.\n\nBut in the digit-by-digit method, the total is 1,297,040,207. These two results are different.\n\nWait, let me check the digit-by-digit again.\n\nLet me write the digits as per the first method:\n\nThe digits from billions dow