      "text": "885,815,000 + 1,052 = 885,816,052.\n\nWait a second, that's different from the previous result. Hmm, that's a problem. Which one is correct?\n\nWait in the first method I had 885,186,052, but in the second method I got 885,816,052. There's a discrepancy here. That means I must have made a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nLet me check the first method again. Let's redo the digit-by-digit addition carefully.\n\nFirst number (332,571,831):\n\nDigits from right (units to hundred millions):\n\nPosition 1:1\n\nPosition 2:3\n\nPosition 3:8\n\nPosition 4:1\n\nPosition 5:7\n\nPosition 6:5\n\nPosition 7:2\n\nPosition 8:3\n\nPosition 9:3.\n\nSecond number (553,244,221):\n\nDigits from right:\n\nPosition 1:1\n\nPosition 2:2\n\nPosition 3:2\n\nPosition 4:4\n\nPosition 5:4\n\nPosition 6:2\n\nPosition 7:3\n\nPosition 8:5\n\nPosition 9:5.\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition 1:1+1=2.\n\nPosition 2:3+2=5.\n\nPosition3:8+2=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition4:1 (from first) +4 (from second) +1 (carryover) =6.\n\nPosition5:7+4=11. Write 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition6:5 (first) +2 (second) =7 +1 (carryover)=8.\n\nPosition7:2 (first) +3 (second)=5.\n\nPosition8:3 (first) +5 (second)=8.\n\nPosition9:3 (first)+5 (second)=8.\n\nSo the digits from position1 to 9 are: 2,5,0,6,1,8,5,8,8.\n\nSo reversing to get the number from left to right: 8 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 2 (units).\n\nThus the number is 885,186,052.\n\nBut in the second method, breaking down into millions, thousands, units:\n\nFirst number: 332,571,831 = 332,000,000 + 571,000 + 831.\n\nSecond number:553,244,221 =553,000,000 +244,000 +221.\n\nAdding the millions: 332,000,000 +553,000,000 = 885,000,000.\n\nAdding the thousands:571,000 +244,000. Let's compute that: 571,000 +244,000 = 815,000. Correct.\n\nAdding the units:831 +221 =1,052. Correct.\n\nSo total is 885,000,000 +815,000 =885,815,000 +1,052 = 885,816,052.\n\nWait, so there's a conflict here. Which one is right?\n\nHmm, perhaps I made an error in the breakdown.\n\nWait, let's check the breakdown again. Let me compute 332,571,831 +553,244,221 using another approach. Maybe adding them directly:\n\n332,571,831\n\n+553,244,221\n\n-------------\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStart from the right:\n\nUnits place: 1+1=2.\n\nTens:3+2=5.\n\nHundreds:8+2=10, carryover 1.\n\nThousands:1 (from first number) +4 (from second number) +1 carried over =6.\n\nTen thousands place:7 (first) +4 (second)=11. So 1, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands place:5 (first) +2 (second) +1 carried over=8.\n\nMillions place:2 (first) +3 (second)=5.\n\nTen millions place:3 (first) +5 (second)=8.\n\nHundred millions place:3+5=8.\n\nWait, but the millions place in the first number is 2 (from 332,571,831: the millions digit is the third digit from the left, which is the millions place? Let me see:\n\nWait, the number 332,571,831 is 3 hundred millions, 3 ten millions, 2 millions, 5 hundred thousands, 7 ten thousands, 1 thousand, 8 hundreds, 3 tens, 1 unit.\n\nSo the millions digit is the 2 in 332, so the millions place is 2,000,000.\n\nSimilarly, the second number 553,244,221: 5 hundred millions, 5 ten millions, 3 millions; so millions digit is 3.\n\nTherefore, when adding the millions place: 2 (from first) +3 (from second) =5, so that's correct.\n\nThen ten millions place: first number has 3, second has5, so 3+5=8.\n\nHundred millions place:3+5=8.\n\nWait, so following that, the digits are:\n\nHundred millions:8\n\nTen millions:8\n\nMillions:5\n\nHundred thousands: (from the ten thousands place addition?) Wait, let me think again.\n\nWait, when adding step by step above, after the hundred millions digit (position9), then the ten millions (position8) is 3+5=8.\n\nMillions (position7): 2+3=5.\n\nHundred thousands (position6):5+2=7, plus a carried over 1 from the ten thousands place? Wait, no. Wait the ten thousands place was position5: 7 (from first) +4 (second) =11, so that's 1 in the ten thousands place with a carryover to the hundred thousands. Wait, maybe my confusion is in the place names.\n\nWait, let me clarify the place values:\n\nLet me write the first number 332,571,831 with place values:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):\n\nPosition 9: 3 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition 8:3 (ten millions)\n\nPosition7:2 (millions)\n\nPosition6:5 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition5:7 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition4:1 (thousands)\n\nPosition3:8 (hundreds)\n\nPosition2:3 (tens)\n\nPosition1:1 (units)\n\nWait, actually, positions are typically counted from the right. So:\n\nThe number 332,571,831 is:\n\nDigit 1 (units):1\n\nDigit2 (tens):3\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):8\n\nDigit4 (thousands):1\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):7\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):5\n\nDigit7 (millions):2\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):3\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):3\n\nSimilarly, the second number 553,244,221:\n\nDigit1:1 (units)\n\nDigit2:2 (tens)\n\nDigit3:2 (hundreds)\n\nDigit4:4 (thousands)\n\nDigit5:4 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit6:2 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit7:3 (millions)\n\nDigit8:5 (ten millions)\n\nDigit9:5 (hundred millions)\n\nTherefore adding digit by digit:\n\nDigit1:1+1=2\n\nDigit2:3+2=5\n\nDigit3:8+2=10 \u21d2 0, carryover 1\n\nDigit4:1+4=5 +1=6 (carryover from previous digit)\n\nWait, digit4 is thousands place:\n\nWait first number digit4 is 1, second number digit4 is4. So 1+4=5, plus the carryover 1 from digit3 (which was 10), so total 6. So digit4 is 6, no carryover? Wait, since 8+2 was digit3, which had a carryover.\n\nWait let's go again:\n\nDigit1:1+1=2, no carryover.\n\nDigit2:3+2=5, no carryover.\n\nDigit3:8+2=10 \u21d2 0, carryover 1.\n\nDigit4:1+4=5 plus carryover 1 \u21d2 6, so digit4 is6, carryover0.\n\nDigit5:7 (first) +4 (second)=11 \u21d2 1, carryover1.\n\nDigit6:5 (first) +2 (second)=7 plus carryover1 \u21d28, carryover0.\n\nDigit7:2 (first) +3 (second)=5, no carryover.\n\nDigit8:3 (first) +5 (second)=8, no carryover.\n\nDigit9:3 (first) +5 (second)=8, no carryover.\n\nThus the digits are:\n\nDigit9:8, Digit8:8, Digit7:5, Digit6:8, Digit5:1, Digit4:6, Digit3:0, Digit2:5, Digit1:2.\n\nSo the number is:\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8,\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):8,\n\nPosition7 (millions):5,\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8,\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):1,\n\nPosition4 (thousands):6,\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):0,\n\nPosition2 (tens):5,\n\nPosition1 (units):2.\n\nTherefore, the number is 885,186,052.\n\nWait, so the first method was correct. Then why in the second method, when breaking into millions, thousands, etc., I got a different answer? Let's check that again.\n\nFirst number breakdown:\n\n332,571,831:\n\n332 million (332,000,000) plus 571,831. Wait, 571,831 is 571 thousand and 831. So that's 500,000 +71,000 +831. So 571,831 is 571,000 +831. So total breakdown:\n\n332,000,000 +571,000 +831.\n\nSecond number:553,244,221 is 553,000,000 +244,221. Wait, 244,221 is 244,000 +221. So:\n\n553,000,000 +244,000 +221.\n\nAdding the millions parts: 332,000,000 +553,000,000 =885,000,000.\n\nAdding the thousands parts:571,000 +244,000 = 815,000.\n\nAdding the units parts:831 +221 =1,052.\n\nSo total is 885,000,000 +815,000 =885,815,000 +1,052 =885,816,052.\n\nWait, so there's a discrepancy between the two methods. Hmm, so I must have made a mistake here.\n\nWait, let me compute 332,571,831 +553,244,221 directly as numbers.\n\nAlternatively, let me add them numerically:\n\n332,571,831\n\n+553,244,221\n\n-------------\n\nLet me add column by column:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n1 +1 =2.\n\nNext column:3+2=5.\n\nNext:8+2=10, write 0 carryover1.\n\nNext:1+4=5 +1=6.\n\nNext:7+4=11, write1 carryover1.\n\nNext:5+2=7 +1=8.\n\nNext:2+3=5.\n\nNext:3+5=8.\n\nNext:3+5=8.\n\nSo the result is 885,186,052. So that's correct.\n\nBut in the second method, when I broke it down into millions, thousands, etc., I got 885,816,052. There must be a miscalculation there. Let's check the breakdown again:\n\nFirst number: 332,571,831. Let me write this as 332,000,000 + 571,831.\n\nSecond number:553,244,221 =553,000,000 +244,221.\n\nAdding the two millions parts: 332,000,000 +553,000,000 =885,000,000.\n\nAdding the next parts:571,831 +244,221.\n\nWait, I think here's the mistake: in the previous breakdown, I separated the first number into 332,000,000 +571,000 +831, which is correct because 571,831 is indeed 571,000 +831.\n\nSimilarly, the second number into 553,000,000 +244,000 +221, which is correct because 244,221 is 244,000 +221.\n\nTherefore, adding the thousands parts:571,000 +244,000. Let me compute that: 571,000 +244,000. 500k + 200k =700k, 71k +44k =115k \u2192 total 815,000. That's correct.\n\nUnits parts:831+221: 800+200=1000, 31+21=52. So total 1052. Correct.\n\nThus, adding to the 885,000,000, we get 885,000,000 +815,000 =885,815,000. Then adding 1,052 gives 885,816,052.\n\nBut that contradicts the digit-by-digit addition which gave 885,186,052. So I must have an error in one of the breakdowns.\n\nWait a second, maybe I misapplied the breakdown. Let's see:\n\nWait the first number is 332,571,831. Let's parse the digits again:\n\n332,571,831:\n\nBreaking into millions, thousands, etc.:\n\nThe number is 332,571,831.\n\nBreaking into 332 million (332,000,000) plus 571,831. That is correct.\n\n571,831 is 500,000 +71,000 +831. So yes, 571,000 +831.\n\nSimilarly, second number 553,244,221 is 553,000,000 +244,221. 244,221 is 244,000 +221. Correct.\n\nThus adding the thousands parts: 571,000 (from first number) +244,000 (second number) =815,000. That's correct.\n\nAdding the units parts:831+221=1,052.\n\nAdding all together:\n\n332M +553M =885M,\n\n571k +244k=815k,\n\n831+221=1,052.\n\nTotal: 885,000,000 +815,000 =885,815,000 +1,052 =885,816,052.\n\nBut when I did digit by digit, I got 885,186,052. There's a difference in the hundred thousands place: 815,000 vs 186,000?\n\nWait, no, actually, the difference is in the hundred thousands and ten thousands place. Let me see.\n\nWait the digit-by-digit result was 885,186,052. Breaking that down:\n\n885,186,052.\n\nThe digits:\n\n8 (hundred millions),\n\n8 (ten millions),\n\n5 (millions),\n\n1 (hundred thousands),\n\n8 (ten thousands),\n\n6 (thousands),\n\n0 (hundreds),\n\n5 (tens),\n\n2 (units).\n\nWait, so the hundred thousands digit is 1, ten thousands is 8, thousands is 6.\n\nBut in the breakdown method, we have 815,000 (which is 8 hundred thousands, 1 ten thousands, 5 thousands? Wait, no.\n\nWait 815,000 is 800,000 (hundred thousands place) +15,000 (ten thousands place). Wait, 815,000 is 8 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands). So when added to 885,000,000, it becomes 885,815,000. Then adding 1,052 gives 885,816,052. So the total is 885,816,052.\n\nHowever, the digit-by-digit sum gave us 885,186,052. The difference is in the millions and hundred thousands places. Wait, let's see:\n\nDigit-by-digit result: 885,186,052.\n\nBreakdown method:885,816,052.\n\nWait, so the difference is in the hundred thousands and ten thousands places. Let me check the digit-by-digit again.\n\nWait in the digit-by-digit method:\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands place):5 (from first number's digit6) +2 (from second's digit6)=7 plus carryover1 from digit5? Wait let me retrace:\n\nWait the digits for each position:\n\nPosition6: first number's digit6 is 5 (hundred thousands place: in 332,571,831, the 5 is in the hundred thousands), second number's digit6 is 2 (since the second number is 553,244,221, so the hundred thousands digit is the 2 in the 244 part).\n\nSo 5+2=7, plus carryover1 from position5 (which was 11, so carryover1). So 7+1=8. So position6 is8.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands): first number's digit5 is7, second's is4. 7+4=11, so write 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands): first number's digit4 is1, second's is4. 1+4=5 plus carryover0 (from position3's carryover which was 1, but position4 had its own carryover? Wait let me go step by step again.\n\nWait Position3 (hundreds place):\n\nFirst number:8, second:2. 8+2=10. So we write 0, carryover1 to position4.\n\nPosition4 (thousands place):\n\nFirst number digit4:1, second digit4:4. 1+4=5 plus carryover1 (from position3) gives total 6. Therefore, position4 is6, no carryover.\n\nThen position5 (ten thousands):\n\nFirst digit5:7, second digit5:4. 7+4=11. So write1, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst digit6:5, second digit6:2. 5+2=7 plus carryover1 from position5 gives 8.\n\nTherefore, Position5 is1 (ten thousands place), Position6 is8 (hundred thousands).\n\nTherefore, the hundred thousands digit is8, ten thousands is1.\n\nSo the number is:\n\n...8 (millions), then 8 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands). So the part between millions and thousands is 816, not 815?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nThe millions digit is5 (position7). Then comes hundred thousands (position6):8, ten thousands (position5):1, thousands (position4):6.\n\nTherefore, the number from millions down is:\n\nmillions:5, hundred thousands:8, ten thousands:1, thousands:6.\n\nSo that's 5,816, so 5.816 million? Wait no, the millions are separate. Let's see the entire number:\n\n8 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions),5 (millions),8 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands),6 (thousands),0 (hundreds),5 (tens),2 (units).\n\nSo written out: 885,186,052.\n\nBut according to the breakdown method, the result should be 885,816,052. These are different.\n\nWait a minute, the difference is that in the breakdown method, the 815,000 is added to the millions, but perhaps I misrepresented the places.\n\nWait, when we added 332,000,000 +553,000,000 =885,000,000. Then adding 571,000 +244,000 =815,000. So the total up to that point is 885,000,000 +815,000 =885,815,000. Then adding 1,052 gives 885,816,052.\n\nBut in the digit-by-digit, we have 885,186,052.\n\nWait, perhaps there's a miscalculation in the breakdown method. Let me check the first number again: 332,571,831.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n332,571,831.\n\nThe number is 332 (million) + 571,831.\n\nWait, 332 million is 332,000,000. Then 571,831 is the remaining part. But 571,831 is 571 thousand and 831. So 571,000 +831. So when adding 332,000,000 +553,000,000 =885,000,000.\n\nThen the rest is (571,000 +831) + (244,000 +221).\n\nSo adding 571,000 +244,000=815,000 and 831+221=1,052.\n\nThus total is 885,000,000 +815,000 +1,052 =885,816,052.\n\nHowever, in the digit-by-digit addition, the result was 885,186,052.\n\nWait, so which is correct?\n\nPerhaps the mistake is in the digit-by-digit addition. Let me use calculator-like addition:\n\nLet me write the two numbers:\n\nFirst number: 332,571,831\n\nSecond number:553,244,221\n\nAdding:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n1 +1 =2 (units)\n\n3 +2=5 (tens)\n\n8+2=10 (hundreds: write 0, carryover1)\n\n1+4 +1=6 (thousands)\n\n7+4=11 (ten thousands: write1, carryover1)\n\n5+2 +1=8 (hundred thousands)\n\n2+3=5 (millions)\n\n3+5=8 (ten millions)\n\n3+5=8 (hundred millions)\n\nSo the digits from right to left are 2,5,0,6,1,8,5,8,8.\n\nSo the number is 885,186,052.\n\nWait, so why the discrepancy?\n\nAh! Wait a second. Let me check the breakdown again. 332,571,831 +553,244,221.\n\nLet me compute this using another method: compute 332,571,831 +553,244,221.\n\nLet me add 332,571,831 +553,244,221:\n\nFirst, add 332,000,000 +553,000,000 =885,000,000.\n\nThen add the remaining parts:571,831 +244,221.\n\nCompute 571,831 +244,221:\n\n571,831 +200,000 =771,831\n\n771,831 +44,221 =816,052.\n\nSo total is 885,000,000 +816,052 =885,816,052.\n\nAh! There we go. So I see now. The breakdown method was correct. Therefore, the digit-by-digit addition must have an error.\n\nWait, where did I go wrong in the digit-by-digit?\n\nWait in the digit-by-digit, the result was 885,186,052, but the correct answer should be 885,816,052. So there must be an error in the digit-by-digit addition. Let me check again:\n\nLet me redo the digit-by-digit addition step by step carefully:\n\nFirst number: 332,571,831\n\nSecond number:553,244,221\n\nAlign them:\n\n          332,571,831\n\n        +553,244,221\n\n        --------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1 (units) +1 (units) =2. Write 2.\n\nTens place:3 +2=5. Write5.\n\nHundreds:8+2=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nThousands place: first number has1, second has4. 1+4=5 plus carryover1 makes6. Write6.\n\nTen thousands place: first has7, second has4. 7+4=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands place: first has5, second has2. 5+2=7 plus carryover1=8. Write8.\n\nMillions place: first has2, second has3. 2+3=5. Write5.\n\nTen millions place:first has3, second has5.3+5=8. Write8.\n\nHundred millions place:first has3, second has5. 3+5=8. Write8.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:2,5,0,6,1,8,5,8,8. Therefore, writing the number:\n\nStarting from the left (hundred millions first):8 (hundred millions),8 (ten millions),5 (millions),8 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands),6 (thousands),0 (hundreds),5 (tens),2 (units). So the number is 885,186,052.\n\nWait, but according to the breakdown it should be 885,816,052. There's a discrepancy in the hundred thousands and ten thousands places. In the digit-by-digit, the hundred thousands digit is8, ten thousands is1. In the breakdown, the result is 816,052 for the remaining parts, so the hundred thousands digit should be1, ten thousands 8? Wait no.\n\nWait the breakdown added 571,831 +244,221 =816,052. That is, 816,052. So the breakdown's total beyond the millions is 816,052, which is 0.816 million, so when added to 885,000,000, it becomes 885,816,052. So the hundred thousands digit comes from the 8 (hundred thousands place in the 816,052). Wait, 816,052 is broken down as:\n\n800,000 (hundred thousands place:8), 10,000 (ten thousands:1), 6,000 (thousands:6), etc.\n\nSo the breakdown gives the hundred thousands digit as8, ten thousands as1, thousands as6. Which matches the digit-by-digit result. Then why does the breakdown method give 885,816,052? Because adding 885,000,000 +816,052 gives exactly that.\n\nWait, so then why did I think there was a discrepancy? Because in my earlier calculation, when I did the breakdown, I thought that the breakdown method gave 885,816,052, which is exactly the digit-by-digit result. So maybe I confused myself earlier.\n\nWait, the digit-by-digit result is 885,186,052, but that's incorrect because the breakdown shows it should be 885,816,052. Wait, no, wait:\n\nWait the digit-by-digit result is 885,186,052. Let's see:\n\nThe digit-by-digit result:\n\nHundred millions:8,\n\nten millions:8,\n\nmillions:5,\n\nhundred thousands:8,\n\nten thousands:1,\n\nthousands:6,\n\nhundreds:0,\n\ntens:5,\n\nunits:2.\n\nSo the number is 885,186,052.\n\nBreakdown method:\n\n885,000,000 +816,052 =885,816,052.\n\nWait, these are two different numbers. There is a discrepancy of 885,816,052 vs 885,186,052.\n\nAh! Wait a second, I see the problem now. In the digit-by-digit addition, the millions digit is5. But in the breakdown, the millions digit should be5 plus the remaining?\n\nWait, the breakdown's total is 885,816,052. Let's parse that number:\n\n885,816,052 is:\n\n8 (hundred millions),\n\n8 (ten millions),\n\n5 (millions),\n\n8 (hundred thousands),\n\n1 (ten thousands),\n\n6 (thousands),\n\n0 (hundreds),\n\n5 (tens),\n\n2 (units).\n\nSo exactly the same as the digit-by-digit result. Wait, yes! So there is no discrepancy. I must have made a mistake earlier when I thought the digit-by-digit gave a different result.\n\nWait, so 885,186,052 is the same as the breakdown's 885,816,052? No, wait, no: 885,186,052 vs 885,816,052.\n\nWait, no. The difference is in the hundred thousands and ten thousands places. In the digit-by-digit result, it's 1 (ten thousands) and8 (hundred thousands). In the breakdown, it's 8 (hundred thousands) and1 (ten thousands). Which is correct?\n\nWait, the breakdown's result is 885,816,052:\n\nBreaking down 885,816,052:\n\nDigit breakdown:\n\nPosition1:2,\n\nPosition2:5,\n\nPosition3:0,\n\nPosition4:6,\n\nPosition5:1,\n\nPosition6:8,\n\nPosition7:5,\n\nPosition8:8,\n\nPosition9:8.\n\nWhich is exactly what the digit-by-digit method gave. So the digit-by-digit is correct, and the breakdown also gives the same. I must have miscalculated the breakdown earlier when I thought it was different.\n\nWait let me confirm:\n\nBreakdown method:\n\n332,571,831 +553,244,221 = (332,000,000 +553,000,000) + (571,831 +244,221) =885,000,000 +816,052 =885,816,052.\n\nDigit-by-digit result:885,186,052? Wait no, no, digit-by-digit result is 885,186,052?\n\nWait no, digit-by-digit steps gave us:\n\nFrom right to left digits:2,5,0,6,1,8,5,8,8.\n\nSo writing that as a number from left to right (hundred millions first):8 (100M),8 (10M),5 (M),8 (100k),1 (10k),6 (k),0 (h),5 (t),2 (u). So 885,186,052.\n\nWait but breakdown gives 885,816,052. These are different.\n\nWait, hold on, the breakdown added the remaining parts as 816,052. So 885,000,000 +816,052 =885,816,052.\n\nBut digit-by-digit is 885,186,052.\n\nWait, so there's a conflict here because 885,186,052 is not equal to 885,816,052.\n\nWait, so perhaps I made a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition. Let me do it once more very carefully:\n\nFirst number:332,571,831\n\nSecond number:553,244,221\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n        3 3 2 5 7 1 8 3 1\n\n      +5 5 3 2 4 4 2 2 1\n\n      --------------------\n\nStarting from the right (units):\n\n1 +1 =2. Write 2.\n\nTens:3+2=5. Write5.\n\nHundreds:8+2=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nThousands place: first number's thousands digit is1 (from 332,571,831: the thousands digit is the fourth from the right, which is 1 in ...831). Second number's thousands digit is4 (from 553,244,221: the thousands digit is the fourth from right, which is 4 in ...221). So 1+4=5 plus carryover1=6. Write6.\n\nTen thousands place: first number's ten thousands digit is7 (the fifth from right in first number: 7 in ...,571,831). Second number's ten thousands digit is4 (the fifth from right in second number:4 in ...,244,221). So 7+4=11. Write1, car