are placed every three digits. So 990,061,514 is broken down as:.\n\n- 9 (hundred millions place).\n- 9 (ten millions).\n- 0 (millions).\n- 0 (hundred thousands).\n- 6 (ten thousands).\n- 1 (thousands).\n- 5 (hundreds).\n- 1 (tens).\n- 4 (ones).\n\nWait, that might not be correct. Let me count the digits properly. Let's see:.\n\nThe number is 990,061,514. Let's remove the commas to see the digits:.\n\nDigits: 9 9 0 0 6 1 5 1 4.\n\nSo positions (from right, starting at 1):.\n\nPosition 9: 9 (hundred millions).\nPosition 8: 9 (ten millions).\nPosition 7: 0 (millions).\nPosition 6: 0 (hundred thousands).\nPosition 5: 6 (ten thousands).\nPosition 4: 1 (thousands).\nPosition 3: 5 (hundreds).\nPosition 2: 1 (tens).\nPosition 1: 4 (ones).\n\nWait, actually, when reading numbers, the rightmost digit is the first position (ones). So let's list each digit with its place:.\n\nDigit positions (from right):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 4.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 1.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):5.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):1.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):6.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):0.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):0.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):9.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):9.\n\nOkay, so that's correct.\n\nNow the second number is 397,334,501. Let's break that down similarly:.\n\nDigits without commas: 3 9 7 3 3 4 5 0 1.\n\nPositions from the right (ones place):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):0.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):5.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):4.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):3.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):3.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):7.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):9.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):3.\n\nWait, let me confirm again:.\n\nOriginal number: 397,334,501.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nDigits: 3 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (ones). .\n\nYes, that's correct. So in positions from the right:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):0.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):5.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):4.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):3.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):3.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):7.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):9.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):3.\n\nAlright, so now to add them digit by digit starting from the right, keeping track of carries.\n\nLet me write them aligned properly:.\n\n          9 9 0 0 6 1 5 1 4.\n\n        + 3 9 7 3 3 4 5 0 1.\n\n        ---------------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (position 1):.\n\nPosition 1: 4 + 1 = 5. Write down 5. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition 2: 1 (from first number) + 0 (from second) = 1. Write 1. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition 3: 5 + 5 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 4: 1 + 4 = 5, plus carryover 1 makes 6. Write 6, carryover 0.\n\nPosition 5: 6 + 3 = 9. Write 9, carryover 0.\n\nPosition 6: 0 + 3 = 3. Write 3, carryover 0.\n\nPosition 7: 0 + 7 = 7. Write 7, carryover 0.\n\nPosition 8: 9 + 9 = 18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 9: 9 + 3 = 12, plus carryover 1 makes 13. Wait, but since these are the highest digits, we need to handle the carryover properly. Let me think step by step:.\n\nWait, actually, when adding two 9-digit numbers, the result can have up to 10 digits, so we need to account for that.\n\nWait, actually, the first number is 9 digits: 990,061,514 (since 990 million is 9 digits). The second is also 9 digits: 397,334,501. Adding two 9-digit numbers can result in a 10-digit number. So when adding the leftmost digits (positions 9 and 9), which are 9 and 3, plus any carryover from the previous position.\n\nWait, let me go through each position step by step carefully.\n\nLet me list each digit from both numbers in each position:.\n\nPositions from right (1 to 9):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):.\n\nFirst number: 4.\n\nSecond number: 1.\n\nSum: 4 +1 =5. Write 5, carryover 0.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\nFirst:1.\n\nSecond:0.\n\nSum:1+0=1. Write 1, carryover 0.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):.\n\nFirst:5.\n\nSecond:5.\n\nSum:5+5=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):.\n\nFirst:1.\n\nSecond:4.\n\nSum:1+4=5 + carryover 1 =6. Write 6, carryover 0.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):.\n\nFirst:6.\n\nSecond:3.\n\nSum:6+3=9. Write 9, carryover 0.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):.\n\nFirst:0.\n\nSecond:3.\n\nSum:0+3=3. Write 3, carryover 0.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):.\n\nFirst:0.\n\nSecond:7.\n\nSum:0+7=7. Write 7, carryover 0.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst:9.\n\nSecond:9.\n\nSum:9+9=18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst:9.\n\nSecond:3.\n\nSum:9 +3=12, plus carryover 1 is 13. .\n\nWait, here's the problem. Since we're at the highest digit (position 9), adding 9 + 3 gives 12, plus the carryover 1 makes 13. So we need to write 3 in the hundred millions place and carryover 1 to a new tenth digit (billions place). .\n\nTherefore, the total sum would be:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits we have written so far (from position 1 to 9) are:.\n\nPosition 1:5.\n\n2:1.\n\n3:0.\n\n4:6.\n\n5:9.\n\n6:3.\n\n7:7.\n\n8:8.\n\n9:3 (with a carryover 1 to the next digit).\n\nBut since there's a carryover 1 from position 9, we need to add a new digit at the tenth position (billions place), which is 1.\n\nSo the total digits are now 10 digits:.\n\n1 (billions) followed by the digits from position 9 (now 3), and so on.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nHundred millions:3.\n\nTen millions:8.\n\nMillions:7.\n\nHundred thousands:3.\n\nTen thousands:9.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nHundreds:0.\n\nTens:1.\n\nOnes:5.\n\nSo the number would be 1,3 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 5 (ones). .\n\nWait, let me write the digits in order from highest to lowest:.\n\nBillions digit:1.\n\nHundred millions:3.\n\nTen millions:8.\n\nMillions:7.\n\nHundred thousands:3.\n\nTen thousands:9.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nHundreds:0.\n\nTens:1.\n\nOnes:5. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
