ber:8 8 5 0 4 8 7 5 2.\n\nHundred millions:8.\n\nTen millions:8.\n\nMillions:5.\n\nHundred thousands:0.\n\nTen thousands:4.\n\nThousands:8.\n\nHundreds:7.\n\nTens:5.\n\nOnes:2.\n\nWait, perhaps aligning them:.\n\nLet me write both numbers digit by digit in the same places:.\n\nHundred millions Ten millions Millions Hundred thousands Ten thousands Thousands Hundreds Tens Ones.\n\nFirst number:       9           4        0           8          3         3        5      9    8.\n\nSecond number:      8           8        5           0          4         8        7      5    2.\n\nAdding each column:.\n\nStarting from the Ones:.\n\nOnes: 8 +2 =10 → 0, carryover 1.\n\nTens:9 +5 =14 +1=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:5 +7=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nThousands:3 +8=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:3 +4=7 +1=8 →8.\n\nHundred thousands:8 +0=8 →8.\n\nMillions:0 +5=5 →5.\n\nTen millions:4 +8=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:9 +8=17 +1 (carryover)=18 →8, carryover1.\n\nWait, but the hundred millions place addition gives 17 plus the carryover 1 from ten millions, making 18. So that would be 8 in the hundred millions and a carryover of 1 to a new billion place.\n\nTherefore, the total sum would be 1 (from the carryover) followed by 8 in the hundred millions place, then the other digits.\n\nWait, so putting all together:.\n\nThe total digits would be:.\n\nBillions place: 1 (carryover).\n\nHundred millions:8.\n\nTen millions:2.\n\nMillions:5.\n\nHundred thousands:8.\n\nTen thousands:8.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nTens:5.\n\nOnes:0.\n\nWait, let me reconstruct the number step by step:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (Ones):.\n\nOnes:0.\n\nTens:5.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nTen thousands:8.\n\nHundred thousands:8.\n\nMillions:5.\n\nTen millions:2.\n\nHundred millions:8.\n\nPlus the carryover 1 in the billions place. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
