,000,000 (hundred millions:8).\n- 90,000,000 (ten millions:9).\n- 0,000,000 (millions:0).\n- 700,000 (hundred thousands:7).\n- 20,000 (ten thousands:2).\n- 2,000 (thousands:2).\n- 900 (hundreds:9).\n- 30 (tens:3).\n- 5 (units:5).\n\nSo digits from left to right: 8,9,0,7,2,2,9,3,5.\n\nTherefore, when we add them:.\n\nPosition 1 (units): 5 +5 =10 → 0 carry 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 3+3=6 +1=7.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):3 +9=12 →2 carry1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):9+2=11 +1=12 →2 carry1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):8 +2=10? Wait, wait, the first number's ten thousands place is 8 (from breakdown above: 8 in ten thousands?), and second number's ten thousands place is 2? Wait let me check again:.\n\nWait the first number's ten thousands place is 8 (from 852,989,335: the digits are 8 (hundred millions),5 (ten millions),2 (millions),9 (hundred thousands),8 (ten thousands),9 (thousands), etc. So yes, ten thousands is 8. Second number's ten thousands place is 2 (from 890,722,935: breakdown shows ten thousands is 2). So 8 +2 =10, plus the carryover 1 makes 11. Wait, hold on, in my previous calculation I had written 8+7? That was a mistake. Wait I think I messed up in step 5.\n\nWait let me re-express all positions properly.\n\nLet me create a table for each digit position:.\n\nNumber1: 8 5 2 9 8 9 3 3 5.\n\nNumber2:8 9 0 7 2 2 9 3 5.\n\nPositions from left to right (hundred millions to units):.\n\nPosition 1 (hundred millions): 8 vs 8.\n\nPosition 2 (ten millions):5 vs9.\n\nPosition3 (millions):2 vs0.\n\nPosition4 (hundred thousands):9 vs7.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):8 vs2.\n\nPosition6 (thousands):9 vs2.\n\nPosition7 (hundreds):3 vs9.\n\nPosition8 (tens):3 vs3.\n\nPosition9 (units):5 vs5.\n\nWait, actually, perhaps the positions are better numbered from the right. Let me instead number positions from right (units) as position1 to position9 (hundred millions):.\n\nSo:.\n\nNumber1 (852,989,335):.\n\nPosition9 (leftmost): 8.\n\nPosition8:5.\n\nPosition7:2.\n\nPosition6:9.\n\nPosition5:8.\n\nPosition4:9.\n\nPosition3:3.\n\nPosition2:3.\n\nPosition1:5.\n\nWait, this might be confusing. Alternatively, perhaps better to just write them aligned:.\n\nLet me write them vertically with each digit's place:.\n\nLet me write each number with their digits separated:.\n\nFirst number (852,989,335):.\n\nDigit 1 (Billions place? Wait, actually, 852 million is less than a billion. So 852,989,335 is 8 hundred millions, 5 ten millions, 2 millions, etc. So the places are:.\n\nDigit 1 (Hundred Millions): 8.\n\nDigit 2 (Ten Millions):5.\n\nDigit3 (Millions):2.\n\nDigit4 (Hundred Thousands):9.\n\nDigit5 (Ten Thousands):8.\n\nDigit6 (Thousands):9.\n\nDigit7 (Hundreds):3.\n\nDigit8 (Tens):3.\n\nDigit9 (Units):5.\n\nSecond number (890,722,935):.\n\nDigit1 (Hundred Millions):8.\n\nDigit2 (Ten Millions):9.\n\nDigit3 (Millions):0.\n\nDigit4 (Hundred Thousands):7.\n\nDigit5 (Ten Thousands):2.\n\nDigit6 (Thousands):2.\n\nDigit7 (Hundreds):9.\n\nDigit8 (Tens):3.\n\nDigit9 (Units):5.\n\nTherefore, when adding digit by digit from right to left (starting at units):.\n\nPosition9 (Units): 5 +5 =10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition8 (Tens):3 +3 =6 +1=7. Write7, no carry.\n\nPosition7 (Hundreds):3 +9=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (Thousands):9 +2=11 +1=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (Ten thousands):8 +2=10 +1=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nWait, hold on, previously I thought it was 8 +7? Wait, no. Position5 is Ten thousands place. For the first number, that digit is 8 (as per breakdown above: Digit5 is Ten thousands:8). The second number's Ten thousands digit is 2 (Digit5:2). So 8+2=10, plus carryover1=11. So write 1, carryover1. Okay.\n\nPosition4 (Hundred thousands): first number's digit is9, second is7. 9+7=16, plus carryover1=17. So write7, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (Millions): first number's digit is2, second is0. 2+0=2 +1=3. Write3, no carry.\n\nPosition2 (Ten millions): first is5, second is9. 5+9=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nPosition1 (Hundred millions): first is8, second is8. 8+8=16 +1 (carryover)=17. So write7 and carryover1.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, the carried over1 becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nSo putting all the digits together from the right:.\n\nStarting from the units (position9) to the left:.\n\nPosition9:0.\n\nPosition8:7.\n\nPosition7:2.\n\nPosition6:2.\n\nPosition5:1.\n\nPosition4:7.\n\nPosition3:3.\n\nPosition2:4.\n\nPosition1:7.\n\nPlus the final carryover1 at the beginning.\n\nSo the digits from left to right are:.\n\n1 (from the final carryover),.\n\nthen Position1's digit:7,.\n\nPosition2's digit:4,.\n\nPosition3's digit:3,.\n\nPosition4's digit:7,.\n\nPosition5's digit:1,.\n\nPosition6's digit:2,.\n\nPosition7's digit:2,.\n\nPosition8's digit:7,.\n\nPosition9's digit:0.\n\nWait, hold on, this is getting confusing. Let me write the result step by step as we go through each position:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):.\n\nAfter processing each position, the digits written are:.\n\nPosition9 (Units):0.\n\nPosition8 (Tens):7.\n\nPosition7 (Hundreds):2.\n\nPosition6 (Thousands):2.\n\nPosition5 (Ten thousands):1.\n\nPosition4 (Hundred thousands):7 (because 9+7+1=17, so write7 carry1?).\n\nWait, wait, let me redo the steps with correct positions:.\n\nLet me go through each position again carefully:.\n\nStarting from the right (units):.\n\n1. Units (position9):.\n\n5 +5 =10 → 0, carryover1.\n\nResult digit1:0.\n\n2. Tens (position8):.\n\n3 +3 =6 +1 =7 →7, carryover0.\n\nResult digit2:7.\n\n3. Hundreds (position7):.\n\n3 +9 =12 →2, carryover1.\n\nResult digit3:2.\n\n4. Thousands (position6):.\n\n9 (from first number) +2 (from second) =11 +1 (carryover) =12 →2, carryover1.\n\nResult digit4:2.\n\n5. Ten thousands (position5):.\n\n8 +2 =10 +1 =11 →1, carryover1.\n\nResult digit5:1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands (position4):.\n\n9 (first) +7 (second) =16 +1 (carryover) =17 →7, carryover1.\n\nResult digit6:7.\n\n7. Millions (position3):.\n\n2 (first) +0 (second) =2 +1 (carryover) =3 →3, carryover0.\n\nResult digit7:3.\n\n8. Ten millions (position2):.\n\n5 (first) +9 (second) =14 →4, carryover1.\n\nResult digit8:4.\n\n9. Hundred millions (position1):.\n\n8 (first) +8 (second) =16 +1 (carryover) =17 →7, carryover1.\n\nResult digit9:7.\n\nNow, the carryover1 from the hundred millions place needs to be added as a new digit to the left, making it a 10-digit number.\n\nSo the total result digits from right to left (units to hundred millions plus the new digit):.\n\nDigits (from right to left):.\n\n0 (units),7 (tens),2 (hundreds),2 (thousands),1 (ten thousands),7 (hundred thousands),3 (millions),4 (ten millions),7 (hundred millions), and the final carryover1 (billions place).\n\nTherefore, writing the number from left to right (billions place first):.\n\n1 (billions),7 (hundred millions),4 (ten millions),3 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands),2 (thousands),2 (hundreds),7 (tens),0 (units).\n\nWait, that seems off. Let me list all the result digits in order from highest to lowest place:.\n\nThe final carryover is 1, which is the billions place (since the original numbers were up to hundred millions). Then the next digit is the hundred millions place (7), then ten millions (4), millions (3), hundred thousands (7), ten thousands (1), thousands (2), hundreds (2), tens (7), units (0).\n\nSo the full number is:. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
