lions place):.\n\nFirst number:4.\n\nSecond number:2.\n\nSum:4+2=6 → write6, carryover0.\n\nDigit 9 (hundred millions place):.\n\nFirst number:6.\n\nSecond number:8.\n\nSum:6+8=14 → write4, carryover1.\n\nWait, but there are no more digits beyond the hundred millions place, so that carryover 1 needs to be added as a new digit at the beginning.\n\nSo the total sum would be 1 followed by the digits we have so far.\n\nWait, let me check that again.\n\nWait, when adding the hundred millions place (digit 9):.\n\n6 +8 =14. So that's 4 in the hundred millions place and carryover 1 to the billions place. Since neither number has a digit beyond the hundred millions, the carryover becomes the next digit (billions place). Therefore, the total sum would be 1 (billions) 4 (hundred millions) followed by the rest of the digits.\n\nPutting all together from left to right:.\n\nStarting with the carryover 1 (billions place), then 4 (hundred millions), then the digits from previous steps:.\n\nWait, let's reconstruct the entire sum step by step.\n\nStarting from the right, after each digit's addition, we have the following digits and carryover:.\n\nPositions (from right):.\n\n1. Units: 2, carryover 1.\n\n2. Tens:4, carryover0.\n\n3. Hundreds:5, carryover0.\n\n4. Thousands:2, carryover1.\n\n5. Ten thousands:3, carryover0.\n\n6. Hundred thousands:3, carryover0.\n\n7. Millions:7, carryover0.\n\n8. Ten millions:6, carryover0.\n\n9. Hundred millions:4, carryover1.\n\nThen, the carryover 1 from the hundred millions place addition becomes the next digit (billions place).\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:.\n\n2 (units), 4 (tens), 5 (hundreds), 2 (thousands),3 (ten thousands),3 (hundred thousands),7 (millions),6 (ten millions),4 (hundred millions), and then 1 (billions).\n\nSo when written normally from left to right, it's 1,4 followed by the others. Let me list them properly:.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nHundred millions:4.\n\nTen millions:6.\n\nMillions:7.\n\nHundred thousands:3.\n\nTen th