ions place, carryover1 to ten millions.\n\nWait, so the millions digit is 5, but in the second method, when adding 405 million plus 259 million, that gives 664 million, but the digit-by-digit approach has 5 in millions place. Wait, but in the first number, the millions digit is 5 (from 405,781,934: the millions digit is the 5 in the millions place). The second number's millions digit is 9 (from259,354,502: the millions digit is the9 in the millions place). So adding those two:5+9=14, plus the carryover1 from the hundred thousands place (which was 13, so carryover1), so total 15. So the millions digit is5, and carryover1 to ten millions.\n\nTherefore, the total millions part is 5 million plus the carryover to ten millions. So in the total sum, the millions digit is 5, but in the second method, the millions are accounted for in the 405+259=664 million, but adding the remaining 1,136,436, which is under a million, so the millions digit would be 6 (from 664 million plus the 1,136,436's 1.1 million). Wait, perhaps there is confusion between the millions place in the digit-by-digit and the total millions in the overall number.\n\nWait in the digit-by-digit result of 665,316,436, the structure is:\n\n- 6 (hundred millions)\n\n- 6 (ten millions)\n\n-5 (millions)\n\n-3 (hundred thousands)\n\n-1 (ten thousands)\n\n-6 (thousands)\n\n-4 (hundreds)\n\n-3 (tens)\n\n-6 (units).\n\nThus, the millions digit is 5, and the ten millions digit is6.\n\nHowever, in the second method, adding 405 million +259 million gives664 million, and adding the 1.136 million gives665.136436 million. So that's 665,136,436, which would be:\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n\n6 (ten millions)\n\n5 (millions) ?\n\nWait, let me write 665,136,436 as:\n\n600,000,000 +60,000,000 +5,000,000 +136,436.\n\nWait that's 665,136,436. So the millions place is 5? Let's see:\n\nBreaking down 665,136,436:\n\n- 600,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n-60,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n-5,000,000 (millions)\n\n-136,436 (remaining).\n\nWait, but 600+60+5 =665 million. The remaining 136,436 is the rest. So the millions digit is 5, yes, because the 5 is in the millions place. The ten millions digit is 6, and the hundred millions is6.\n\nWait then why does the second method give this? Because 665,136,436 is the same as the digit-by-digit result of 665,316,436? Wait no. Wait wait 665,136,436 versus 665,316,436.\n\nAh! There is a difference in the hundred thousands place. In the digit-by-digit, the hundred thousands digit is 3, but in the second method's result, the hundred thousands digit is1? Wait let me parse both numbers:\n\nFirst result (digit-by-digit): 665,316,436.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n\n6 (ten millions)\n\n5 (millions)\n\n3 (hundred thousands)\n\n1 (ten thousands)\n\n6 (thousands)\n\n4 (hundreds)\n\n3 (tens)\n\n6 (units).\n\nSecond result (second method):665,136,436.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n\n6 (ten millions)\n\n5 (millions)\n\n1 (hundred thousands)\n\n3 (ten thousands)\n\n6 (thousands)\n\n4 (hundreds)\n\n3 (tens)\n\n6 (units).\n\nAh! Here's the mistake: in the second method, when adding the remaining parts (781,934 +354,502=1,136,436), the 1,136,436 has:\n\n-1,000,000 (millions)\n\n-136,436 (the rest).\n\nBut in the total sum, when adding to 664,000,000, it becomes 665,136,436. So the breakdown is:\n\n- 6 hundred millions,\n\n-6 ten millions,\n\n-5 millions,\n\n-1 hundred thousands,\n\n-3 ten thousands,\n\n-6 thousands,\n\n-4 hundreds,\n\n-3 tens,\n\n-6 units.\n\nWait but why is the hundred thousands digit 1 instead of 3?\n\nBecause in the digit-by-digit, the hundred thousands place was 3. Let's see:\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\nFirst number's hundred thousands digit is7,\n\nSecond number's hundred thousands digit is5,\n\nplus a carryover of1 from the ten thousands place.\n\nSo 7+5+1=13 \u2192 writes 3, carryover1 to millions place.\n\nTherefore, the hundred thousands digit is3, so that must mean the second method is wrong. Which is it?\n\nWait in the second method