 (10^1).\n- 0: ones (10^0).\n\nNow, adding each corresponding place:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 8 + 0 = 8.\n\nTens: 7 + 5 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 4 + 1 = 5, plus the carried 1 makes 6.\n\nThousands: 0 + 6 = 6.\n\nTen thousands: 6 + 1 = 7.\n\nHundred thousands: 3 + 5 = 8.\n\nMillions: 9 + 2 = 11. Hmm, so here we have 11 in the millions place. Wait, that's a two-digit number, so we need to carry over. So write down 1 and carryover 1 to the next higher place.\n\nTen millions: 4 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) = 6, plus the carried over 1 makes 7.\n\nHundred millions: 6 + 9 = 15. Again, two digits. So write down 5 and carry over 1. Since there are no more digits beyond hundred millions, the carried over 1 becomes a 1 in the billions place.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the ones and moving left:.\n\nBillions place: 1 (carried over).\n\nHundred millions: 5.\n\nTen millions: 7.\n\nMillions: 1.\n\nHundred thousands: 8.\n\nTen thousands: 7.\n\nThousands: 6.\n\nHundreds: 6.\n\nTens: 2.\n\nOnes: 8.\n\nSo writing the number out: 1,5 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nWait, let me structure this properly. Let me list each digit step by step:.\n\nStarting from the highest place:.\n\nBillions: 1 (from the carryover of hundred millions addition).\n\nHundred millions: 5 (since 6+9=15, so 5 here).\n\nTen millions: 7 (4+2+1 carryover from millions place).\n\nWait, let's retrace the steps again carefully:.\n\nLet me go step by step for each digit place:.\n\n1. Ones place: 8 + 0 = 8. Result digit: 8.\n\n2. Tens place: 7 + 5 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\n3. Hundreds place: 4 + 1 = 5 + 1 (carryover) = 6. Result digit:6, no carryover.\n\n4. Thousands place: 0 + 6 = 6. Result:6.\n\n5. Ten thousands place: 6 (from first number) +1 (from second number) =7. Result:7.\n\n6. Hundred thousands place: 3 +5=8. Result:8.\n\n7. Millions place: 9 (first number) +2 (second number)=11. So write down 1 in millions place and carryover 1 to the next (ten millions).\n\n8. Ten millions place: 4 (first number) +2 (second number) +1 (carryover)=7. So 7 here, no new carryover.\n\n9. Hundred millions place: 6 (first number) +9 (second number)=15. So write down 5 in hundred millions and carryover 1 to the billions place.\n\n10. Billions place: The carryover 1 from the hundred millions, so we have 1 in the billions place.\n\nNow, compiling all the digits from highest to lowest:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:5.\n\nTen millions:7.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nHundred thousands:8.\n\nTen thousands:7.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nHundreds:6.\n\nTens:2.\n\nOnes:8.\n\nSo putting all together, the number is:.\n\n1,5 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 8 (ones). Therefore, my final answer is: "]] 46%|####6     | 230/500 [6:43:18<4:53:52, 65.30s/it]
