sition4:7+3=10. Write0, Carry1.\n\nPosition5:4+0=4 +1=5. Carry0.\n\nPosition6:0+1=1. Carry0.\n\nPosition7:6+5=11. Write1, Carry1.\n\nPosition8:6+7=13 +1=14. Write4, Carry1.\n\nPosition9:8+9=17 +1=18. Write8, Carry1.\n\nAfter that, the carryover1 becomes the next digit (billions place).\n\nThus the result is:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n8 (hundred millions),\n\n4 (ten millions),\n\n1 (millions),\n\n5 (hundred thousands),\n\n0 (ten thousands),\n\n1 (thousands),\n\n4 (hundreds),\n\n2 (tens),\n\n6 (ones).\n\nWhich is 1,841,501,426.\n\nWait, but according to the second method it's 1,841,150,426.\n\nSo there is a discrepancy in the millions and hundred thousands places.\n\nAh! Let me check where the error is.\n\nWait in the thousands place addition:\n\nPosition4 (thousands place):\n\nFirst number has 7 (thousands digit),\n\nSecond number has 3 (thousands digit).\n\n7+3=10. So we write 0 and carry 1 to the next column (ten thousands).\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\nFirst number:4, Second number:0. 4+0+1(carry)=5. So that's correct.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst number:0, Second number:1. 0+1=1. So that's correct.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst number:6, Second number:5. 6+5=11. Write1, carry1.\n\nSo the millions digit is 1, and carryover1 to the next.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number:6, Second number:7. 6+7=13 plus carryover1=14. So ten millions digit is 4, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst number:8, Second number:9. 8+9=17 plus carryover1=18. So hundred millions digit is8, carryover1.\n\nThus the millions place is 1, hundred thousands is 1? Wait no. Wait the millions digit is at position7. The digits are:\n\nPosition9: hundred millions,\n\nPosition8: ten millions,\n\nPosition7: millions,\n\nPosition6: hundred thousands,\n\nPosition5: ten thousands,\n\nPosition4: thousands,\n\nSo when we get to Position7 (millions place):\n\nThe sum was 11, so we wrote 1 (millions digit) and carried over 1.\n\nThus, in the millions place, it's 1, and the hundred thousands place is 0+1 (from the second number) plus no carry? Wait position6:\n\nPosition6 is hundred thousands:\n\nFirst number's hundred thousands digit is 0,\n\nSecond number's hundred thousands digit is1,\n\nSo 0+1=1, no carry. So that's correct. So the hundred thousands digit is1.\n\nWait, so the digits from position9 to 1 are:\n\nPosition9:8,\n\nPosition8:4,\n\nPosition7:1,\n\nPosition6:1,\n\nPosition5:5,\n\nPosition4:0,\n\nPosition3:4,\n\nPosition2:2,\n\nPosition1:6.\n\nWait, that gives 8 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions),1 (millions),1 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),0 (thousands), etc.\n\nWait so the number would be:\n\nStarting from billions digit (1), then 8 (hundred millions),4 (ten millions),1 (millions),1 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),0 (thousands),1 (hundreds?), Wait no, Position3 is hundreds.\n\nWait let's reorganize:\n\nThe digits from left to right (after adding the carryover):\n\nBillions place:1,\n\nHundred millions:8,\n\nTen millions:4,\n\nMillions:1,\n\nHundred thousands:1,\n\nTen thousands:5,\n\nThousands:0,\n\nHundreds:4,\n\nTens:2,\n\nOnes:6.\n\nTherefore the number is 1,841,105,426?\n\nWait no, let me write it out with commas properly:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:8,\n\nTen millions:4,\n\nMillions:1,\n\nHundred thousands:1,\n\nTen thousands:5,\n\nThousands:0,\n\nHundreds:4,\n\nTens:2,\n\nOnes:6.\n\nSo, breaking into groups:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nMillions: 841 (million),\n\nThousands:105 (thousand),\n\nand 426.\n\nWait no, maybe:\n\nWait 1 (billion),\n\n841 (million),\n\n105 (thousand),\n\n426.\n\nWait that would be 1,841,105,426.\n\nWait but in the digit-wise breakdown, the digits after the first (billions) are 8 (hundred millions),4 (ten millions),1 (millions),1 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),0 (thousands),4 (hundreds),2 (tens),6 (ones).\n\nSo the number is:\n\n1,841,105,426? Wait, let's see:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1 (billion),\n\n841 (million),\n\nthen the next three digits: 105 (thousands),\n\nand 