,0,4,2,6.\n\nWait, so the digits from left to right are:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n8 (hundred millions),\n\n4 (ten millions),\n\n1 (millions),\n\n1 (hundred thousands),\n\n5 (ten thousands),\n\n0 (thousands),\n\n4 (hundreds),\n\n2 (tens),\n\n6 (ones).\n\nSo that is 1,841,105,426.\n\nWait so why the discrepancy? The second method says 1,841,150,426.\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error in the second method's breakdown.\n\nWait the two numbers are:\n\nFirst number:866,047,140.\n\nSecond number:975,103,286.\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me compute 866,047,140 +975,103,286 the straightforward way.\n\nStarting with 866,047,140,\n\nAdd 975,103,286:\n\nLet me write it as:\n\n866,047,140\n\n+975,103,286\n\n-------------\n\nLet me add digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 +6 =6.\n\n4 +8=12, carry 1.\n\n1 +2 +1=4, carry0.\n\n7 +3=10, carry1.\n\n4 +0 +1=5, carry0.\n\n0 +1=1, carry0.\n\n6 +5=11, carry1.\n\n6 +7 +1=14, carry1.\n\n8 +9 +1=18, carry1.\n\nAnd the final carryover 1.\n\nThus the result is 1,841,150,426? Wait wait, wait, now I'm confused.\n\nWait when adding the millions place:\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst number has 6 (millions place),\n\nSecond number has5 (millions place).\n\n6+5=11, so we write 1 in millions and carryover1 to the next (ten millions).\n\nThen Position8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number:6,\n\nSecond:7,\n\nplus carryover1: total 14, so write4, carry1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n8 +9 +1=18, write8, carry1.\n\nThus the digits are:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nhundred millions:8,\n\nten millions:4,\n\nmillions:1,\n\nthen next digit (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst number's hundred thousands digit is0,\n\nSecond number's hundred thousands digit is1,\n\nplus any carry? From the millions place addition, the carry was only to the ten millions.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst number digit:0,\n\nSecond number digit:1,\n\nso 0+1=1, no carry.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\nFirst:4,\n\nSecond:0,\n\nso 4+0=4.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n7+3=10, wrote0, carried1 to ten thousands which became 5.\n\nWait, so the digits:\n\nAfter the millions place (position7:1),\n\nthe next is hundred thousands (position6:1),\n\nthen ten thousands (position5:5),\n\nthousands (position4:0),\n\nhundreds (position3:4),\n\ntens (position2:2),\n\nones (position1:6).\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\nStarting from the highest digit:\n\nBillions (1),\n\nhundred millions (8),\n\nten millions (4),\n\nmillions (1),\n\nhundred thousands (1),\n\nten thousands (5),\n\nthousands (0),\n\nhundreds (4),\n\ntens (2),\n\nones (6).\n\nSo that is 1,841,105,426.\n\nBut in the second method, I had 1,841,150,426. So why the difference?\n\nAh! In the second method, I incorrectly added 866,047 + 975,103 as the thousands components.\n\nWait let's recalculate that addition:\n\n866,047 +975,103:\n\nLet me compute it properly:\n\n866,047\n\n+975,103\n\n= ?\n\nStarting from right:\n\n7 +3=10,\n\ncarryover.\n\nWait let's do it step by step.\n\nUnits place:7 +3=10, write 0, carryover1.\n\nTens:4+0+1=5.\n\nHundreds:0+1=1.\n\nThousands:6 +5=11, write1, carry1.\n\nTen thousands:6+7+1=14, write4, carry1.\n\nHundred thousands:8 +9 +1=18, so write8, carry1.\n\nMillions: carryover1.\n\nWait wait, this is getting confusing. Let me do it properly:\n\n866,047 + 975,103:\n\nBreak into parts:\n\n800,000 + 60,000 +6,000 + 400 +7,\n\nplus\n\n900,000 +70,000 +5,000 +100 +3.\n\nAdding:\n\nMillions: 800k +900k =1,700,000.\n\nHundred thousands:60k +70k=130k.\n\nTen thousands:6k +5k=11k.\n\nThousands:400 +100=500.\n\nUnits:7+3=10.\n\nWait that doesn't help. Let me do it digit by digit.\n\n866,047\n\n+975,103\n\n-------------\n\nAdding units digit:7 +3=10, write0, carry1.\n\nTens:4+0=4 +1=5.\n\nHundreds:0+1=1.\n\nThousands:6 +5=11, write1, carry1.\n\nTen thousands:6 +7=13 +1=14, write4, carry1.\n\nHundred thousands:8 +9=17 +1=18, write8, carry1.\n\nMillions: carry1.\n\nSo total is 1, (8), (4), (1), (4), (1), (5), 0.\n\nWait, no, let me count the digits:\n\nResult of 866,047 +975,103:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition1:0,\n\nPosition2:5,\n\nPosition3:1,\n\nPosition4:1 (with carryover),\n\nPosition5:4 (from 6+7+1),\n\nPosition6:8 (from 8+9+1),\n\nPosition7:1 (carryover).\n\nWait this is getting too tangled. Alternatively, let me compute 866,047 + 975,103 numerically.\n\n866,047 + 975,103:\n\nAdding 866,047 + 900,000 =1,766,047,\n\nthen add 75,103: 1,766,047 +75,103 = 1,841,150.\n\nAh! So that's correct. Therefore the thousands part is 1,841,150,000. Then adding 426 gives 1,841,150,426.\n\nBut the digit-wise addition gives 1,841,105,426.\n\nThere is a discrepancy of 45,000 between the two results.\n\nHmm, so which is correct?\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made an error in digit positions.\n\nWait let's use another method: approximate the numbers.\n\nFirst number: approximately 866 million,\n\nSecond number: approximately 975 million,\n\nTotal approximately 1,841 million, which is 1,841,000,000. The exact total should be a bit more because the other parts are 47,140 and 103,286.\n\nAdding those gives roughly 47k +103k =150,000. So total approx 1,841,150,000 +426=1,841,150,426, which matches the second method.\n\nTherefore, the digit-wise addition must have an error.\n\nLet me do the digit-wise addition again:\n\nFirst number: 866,047,140.\n\nDigits from right (position1 to9):\n\n0,4,1,7,4,0,6,6,8.\n\nSecond number:975,103,286.\n\nDigits from right (position1 to9):\n\n6,8,2,3,0,1,5,7,9.\n\nAdding each position:\n\nPosition1:0+6=6.\n\nPosition2:4+8=12. Write2, carry1.\n\nPosition3:1+2=3 +1=4. Carry0.\n\nPosition4:7+3=10. Write0, carry1.\n\nPosition5:4+0=4 +1=5. Carry0.\n\nPosition6:0+1=1. Carry0.\n\nPosition7:6+5=11. Write1, carry1.\n\nPosition8:6+7=13 +1=14. Write4, carry1.\n\nPosition9:8+9=17 +1=18. Write8, carry1.\n\nSo the digits after adding are:\n\nCarryover1, then 8 (position9),4 (position8),1 (position7),1 (position6),5 (position5),0 (position4),4 (position3),2 (position2),6 (position1).\n\nWait, the position7 is millions place, which was 6 (first number) +5 (second) =11, so that's 1 in millions and carryover. So millions digit is1, and carryover to ten millions.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions): first number's digit is6 + second's7 + carryover1=14, so ten millions digit is4 with carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8 +9 +1=18, so 8 with carryover1.\n\nThus the digits from left (billions) to right are:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n8 (hundred millions),\n\n4 (ten millions),\n\n1 (millions),\n\n1 (hundred thousands),\n\n5 (ten thousands),\n\n0 (thousands),\n\n4 (hundreds),\n\n2 (tens),\n\n6 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,841,105,426.\n\nWait, but that contradicts the other method. Where is the mistake?\n\nWait, perhaps the position numbering is different.\n\nWait if I consider the first digit (hundred millions place) as position9, then when building the final number, the thousands digit is position4, which in the digit-wise result is 0. However in the second method, the thousands digit is 0, so that's consistent.\n\nWait let me write out both results:\n\nDigit-wise result: 1,841,105,426.\n\nSecond method:1,841,150,426.\n\nThe difference is in the ten thousands and hundred thousands places.\n\nDigit-wise has 1 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands).\n\nSecond method has 5 (hundred thousands?), wait no.\n\nWait in the second method, the thousands part was 1,841,150,000. The 1,841,150,000 breaks down as:\n\n1,841,150,000 is equal to 1 billion, 841 million, 150 thousand. Wait, so the ten thousands digit would be 5, hundred thousands 1, millions 4?\n\nWait let's parse 1,841,150,000:\n\n- 1, (billion),\n\n- 841 (million),\n\n- 150 (thousand),\n\n- and 000.\n\nSo breaking into digits:\n\n1 (billions digit),\n\n8 (hundred millions),\n\n4 (ten millions),\n\n1 (millions),\n\n1 (hundred thousands),\n\n5 (ten thousands),\n\n0 (thousands),\n\n0 (hundreds),\n\n0 (tens),\n\n0 (ones).\n\nSo the millions place is1, hundred thousands is1, ten thousands is5, thousands is0.\n\nWhich matches the digit-wise result: 1 (millions),1 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),0 (thousands).\n\nTherefore, the total should be 1,841,105,426.\n\nWait but why does the second method say 1,841,150,426?\n\nAh! Because I think I confused the thousands part. When I added the thousands parts (866,047 and 975,103) to get 1,841,150, that is 1,841,150 thousands, which is 1,841,150,000. Breaking that down:\n\n1,841,150,000 is:\n\n1, (billion),\n\n841, (million),\n\n150, (thousand),\n\nso:\n\n1,841,150,000 is indeed 1,841,150 thousands. But when adding the remaining 140 +286 =426, it should be 1,841,150,000 +426=1,841,150,426. But according to the digit-wise addition, it's 1,841,105,426. There's a 45,000 difference.\n\nWait, wait, perhaps I made an error in the second method's breakdown.\n\nWait the first number is 866,047,140.\n\nBreaking into 866,047 thousands (866,047 *1000 =866,047,000) plus 140.\n\nSecond number is975,103,286 is 975,103 thousands (975,103*1000=975,103,000) plus286.\n\nAdding the thousands parts:866,047 +975,103=1,841,150 => that's correct.\n\nSo total thousands part is1,841,150,000.\n\nAdding the remaining 140 +286=426 gives total of1,841,150,426.\n\nHowever, digit-wise addition gives1,841,105,426.\n\nThis is a contradiction. There must be an error in one of the methods.\n\nLet me try adding the two numbers using another method, breaking them into smaller parts.\n\nFirst number: 866,047,140.\n\nSecond number:975,103,286.\n\nLet's write them as:\n\n866,047,140\n\n+975,103,286\n\n-------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes place:0+6=6.\n\nTens:4+8=12. Write2, carry1.\n\nHundreds:1+2=3 +1=4.\n\nThousands:7+3=10. Write0, carry1.\n\nTen thousands:4+0=4 +1=5.\n\nHundred thousands:0+1=1.\n\nMillions:6+5=11. Write1, carry1.\n\nTen millions:6+7=13 +1=14. Write4, carry1.\n\nHundred millions:8+9=17 +1=18. Write8, carry1.\n\nBillions: carry1.\n\nSo writing it out, the result is 1,841,105,426.\n\nWait so this matches the digit-wise addition, but contradicts the second method.\n\nWait, so then where is the error in the second method?\n\nAh, I think in the second method, I added 866,047 thousands and 975,103 thousands to get 1,841,150 thousands, but actually:\n\nWait 866,047 thousands is 866,047 *1000 =866,047,000,\n\nplus 975,103 *1000 =975,103,000,\n\nTotal thousands part is866,047,000 +975,103,000 =1,841,150,000.\n\nThen adding the remaining 140 +286=426 gives1,841,150,426.\n\nWait but according to digit-wise addition, it's 1,841,105,426.\n\nWait there must be a miscalculation in the digit-wise addition somewhere.\n\nWait let me do the two numbers in calculator fashion.\n\nLet me compute 866,047,140 +975,103,286.\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStart with 866,047,140\n\nAdd 975,103,286:\n\nAdding the units:\n\n0 +6 =6.\n\nTens:4 +8=12. Write2 carry1.\n\nHundreds:1 +2=3 +1=4.\n\nThousands place:7 +3=10. Write0 carry1.\n\nTen thousands:4 +0=4 +1=5.\n\nHundred thousands place:0 +1=1.\n\nMillions place:6 (from first number's millions digit) +5 (second's millions digit)=11, so write1 carry1.\n\nTen millions:6 +7=13 +1=14, write4 carry1.\n\nHundred millions:8+9=17 +1=18, write8 carry1.\n\nBillions: carryover1.\n\nSo the total is1,841,105,426.\n\nWait so the second method must have an error.\n\nWait in the second method, I added 866,047 +975,103 =1,841,150, which when multiplied by 1000 gives1,841,150,000. But that's wrong?\n\nWait wait, 866,047 thousands +975,103 thousands = (866,047 +975,103) thousands.\n\nBut 866,047 +975,103 =?\n\nLet me compute 866,047 +975,103:\n\n866,047 +975,103.\n\nLet's compute 866,047 + 900,000 =1,766,047,\n\nthen add 75,103:\n\n1,766,047 +75,103 =1,841,150. Correct.\n\nTherefore the thousands part is indeed1,841,150,000.\n\nThen adding the remaining 426 gives1,841,150,426.\n\nWait but according to digit-wise addition it is1,841,105,426.\n\nThere is a discrepancy of 45,000.\n\nWait now I'm really confused. Let me try adding the two numbers using a calculator-like approach manually:\n\nStart with the first number:866,047,140.\n\nAdd