5 (last digit).\n\nPosition 2 (tens):5?.\n\nWait, let me write it out digit by digit:.\n\nSecond number: 7 9 7 4 4 4 8 5 5.\n\nWait, actually, 797,444,855 is:.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nDigits from left to right:.\n\n7 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 5 (units). Wait, that can't be right. Wait, commas are placed every three digits.\n\nWait, 797,444,855:.\n\nFirst comma after three digits: 7 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 7 (millions). Then comma after next three: 4 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands). Then comma after next three: 8 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 5 (units). Wait, no, actually, commas are placed as follows:.\n\nThe number is written as 797,444,855. So starting from the right, every three digits are separated by commas. Let's count:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nThe last three digits are 855 (units, tens, hundreds).\n\nThen the next three: 444 (thousands, ten thousands, hundred thousands).\n\nThen the next three: 797 (millions, ten millions, hundred millions). Wait, actually, the first comma separates thousands, then millions, etc.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to parse it as:.\n\n797,444,855:.\n\n- 797 (million).\n\n- 444 (thousand).\n\n- 855 (units).\n\nSo the digits are:.\n\n7 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 5 (units).\n\nTherefore, when written out:.\n\nPosition 1 (units): 5.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):5?.\n\nWait, no. Let's list all digits from right to left with their positions:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):.\n\nPosition 1: 5 (units place).\n\nPosition 2: 5 (tens place).\n\nWait, hold on, the second number is 797,444,855. Let's write all digits in order:.\n\nDigits of second number from left to right:.\n\n7, 9, 7, 4, 4, 4, 8, 5, 5.\n\nWait, that's 9 digits. So the rightmost digit is the 9th digit from the left, which is 5 (units place). The second from the right is the 8th digit: 5 (tens place). The third from the right is the 7th digit: 8 (hundreds place). Fourth from right: 4 (thousands place). Fifth from right: 4 (ten thousands). Sixth from right:4 (hundred thousands). Seventh from right:7 (millions). Eighth from right:9 (ten millions). Ninth from right:7 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, that seems conflicting. Let me better write both numbers digit by digit, aligned properly.\n\nFirst number: 127,322,286.\n\nDigits from left to right:.\n\n1 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 6 (units).\n\nSecond number: 797,444,855.\n\nDigits from left to right:.\n\n7 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 5 (units).\n\nNow, aligning both numbers by their place values:.\n\nHundred millions: 1 vs 7.\n\nTen millions: 2 vs 9.\n\nMillions:7 vs7.\n\nHundred thousands:3 vs4.\n\nTen thousands:2 vs4.\n\nThousands:2 vs4.\n\nHundreds:2 vs8.\n\nTens:8 vs5.\n\nUnits:6 vs5.\n\nWait, but when adding, we need to start from the units and move left. Let me structure the addition vertically:.\n\nLet me write both numbers vertically with each digit's place:.\n\n          1  2  7  3  2  2  2  8  6.\n        + 7  9  7  4  4  4  8  5  5.\n        ---------------------------.\nStarting from the right (units):.\n\nPosition 1 (units): 6 +5 =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):8 +5=13 +1=14. Write 4, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2 +8=10 +1=11. Write 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):2 +4=6 +1=7. Write7, no carryover.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):2 +4=6. Write6, no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):3 +4=7. Write7, no carryover.\n\nPosition7 (millions):7 +7=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):2 +9=11 +1(carryover)=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):1 +7=8 +1(carryover)=9. Write9.\n\nSo putting it all together, the sum would be:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:1, then 4, 1,7,6,7,4,2,9. Wait, but we have to consider the positions. Let's write the digits in order from highest to lowest place:.\n\nThe result digits from position9 to position1:.\n\nPosition9:9.\n\nPosition8:2.\n\nPosition7:4.\n\nPosition6:7.\n\nPosition5:6.\n\nPosition4:7.\n\nPosition3:1.\n\nPosition2:4.\n\nPosition1:1.\n\nTherefore, the number is 9 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions),4 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),6 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),1 (hundreds),4 (tens),1 (units). So putting it all together:. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
