ion5:4 (ten thousands).\n\nYes, so 8 +4=12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst number:9 (hundred thousands).\n\nSecond number:4 (hundred thousands).\n\n9+4 +1 (carryover)=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst number:1 (millions).\n\nSecond number:1 (millions).\n\n1+1 +1 (carryover)=3. Write3, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number:1 (ten millions).\n\nSecond number:4 (ten millions).\n\n1+4=5. Write5.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n5 +9=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nAdding the carryover to a new billions place:1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:4,\n\nTen millions:5,\n\nMillions:3,\n\nHundred thousands:4,\n\nTen thousands:2,\n\nThousands:6,\n\nHundreds:9,\n\nTens:9,\n\nOnes:9.\n\nThus the total is 1,453,426,999.\n\nWhich matches the second method's result.\n\nTherefore, my initial mistake was incorrectly identifying the thousands digit of the second number as 4 instead of 3. After correcting that, both methods agree.\n\nSo the correct answer should be 1,453,426,999.\n\nWait, but let me confirm with another method: using calculator-like addition.\n\nLet me write both numbers:\n\n511,983,601\n\n+941,443,398\n\n------------\n\nAdding them:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1+8=9\n\n0+9=9\n\n6+3=9\n\n3+3=6\n\n8+4=12 (write 2 carryover1)\n\n9+4=13 +1=14 (write4 carryover1)\n\n1+1=2 +1=3\n\n1+4=5\n\n5+9=14 (write4 carryover1)\n\nThen the carried over 1 makes the next digit 1.\n\nSo the number is 1,453,426,999.\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nTherefore, the final answer should be 1,453,426,999.\n\nBut let me verify once more with another approach.\n\nLet me add them in parts:\n\nFirst, add 511,983,601 + 941,443,398.\n\nLet me split into 511,983,601 + 900,000,000 = 1,411,983,601.\n\nThen add the remaining 41,443,398.\n\n1,411,983,601 +41,443,398.\n\nAdding:\n\n1,411,983,601 +40,000,000 =1,451,983,601.\n\nThen add 1,443,398:\n\n1,451,983,601 +1,443,398 = ?\n\n1,451,983,601 +1,000,000 =1,452