messages:  [['Please solve the math problem.\nQuestion: What is the sum of 684357587 and 756476890?', "<think>Okay, so I need to add these two big numbers: 684,357,587 and 756,476,890. Hmm, let me think how to approach this. Maybe I should start by writing them down one on top of the other to make sure the digits line up properly. Let me visualize it:.\n\n\u2003684,357,587  .\n+756,476,890  .\n______________.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. Let's go step by step.\n\nFirst, the ones place: 7 + 0 = 7. That's straightforward.\n\nNext, the tens place: 8 + 9. Wait, 8 plus 9 is 17. Hmm, okay, so I write down 7 and carry over 1 to the hundreds place.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 5 + 6 is 11, plus the carried over 1 makes 12. So write 2 and carry over 1 again.\n\nThousands place: 7 + 8. That's 15, plus the carried 1 is 16. Write 6, carry over 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 5 + 4. Wait, 5 (from the first number) and 6 (from the second number?) Wait, maybe I need to check the digits again. Let me make sure I'm aligning them correctly.\n\nWait, let me write out the numbers without the commas to avoid confusion:.\n\n684357587  .\n756476890  .\nLet me align them properly:.\n\n\u2003684357587  .\n+756476890  .\n______________.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes place: 7 + 0 =7  .\nTens: 8 +9=17 (carry 1)  .\nHundreds:5 +6=11 plus 1 is 12 (carry1)  .\nThousands: 7 +8=15 plus 1=16 (carry1)  .\nTen-thousands place: 5 (from first number) and 6 (from second?) Wait, hold on, maybe I messed up the places.\n\nWait, let's count the digits properly. Let's see:.\n\nFirst number: 684,357,587. Breaking it down:.\n\nDigits: 6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 756,476,890.\n\nBreaking down: 7 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nLet's write each digit position:.\n\nHundred Millions: 6 +7  .\nTen Millions:8 +5  .\nMillions:4 +6  .\nHundred Thousands:3 +4  .\nTen Thousands:5 +7  .\nThousands:7 +6  .\nHundreds:5 +8  .\nTens:8 +9  .\nOnes:7 +0.\n\nWait, maybe this way is better. Let me do each place step by step:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 7 +0=7.\n\nTens:8 +9=17, write 7, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:5 +8=13 +1=14, so write 4 and carryover 1.\n\nThousands:7 +6=13 +1=14, write 4, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:5 +7=12 +1=13, write 3, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:3 +4=7 +1=8, no carryover? Wait, 3+4 is 7 plus the carried 1 makes 8, so write 8, no carryover.\n\nMillions:4 +6=10, write 0 and carryover1.\n\nTen millions:8 +5=13 +1=14, write 4 and carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:6 +7=13 +1=14, write 4 and carryover1.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, we have to add that carried over 1 as a new digit at the beginning.\n\nSo putting all together:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:.\n\nOnes:7.\n\nTens:7.\n\nHundreds:4.\n\nThousands:4.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nHundred thousands:8.\n\nMillions:0.\n\nTen millions:4.\n\nHundred millions:4.\n\nAnd the final carried over 1 in the billions place. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
