uld be a carryover to a new digit).\n\nWait, hold on, the hundred millions place: 5 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) = 11. Since both are in the hundred millions place, their sum is 11 hundred million, so that would be 1,100,000,000. So we need to write that as 1 in the billions place and 1 in the hundred millions place? Wait, actually, adding two numbers in the hundred millions can result in up to 1.1 billion, so yes, the sum would have a billion digit.\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:.\n\n3 (units), 5 (tens), 7 (hundreds), 8 (thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 3 (hundred thousands), 5 (millions), 6 (ten millions), and then the hundred millions sum is 11, so we have 1 in the hundred millions and carryover 1 to the billions place.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconstruct the total sum step by step.\n\nLet me write down each digit position with their sums:.\n\n1. Units: 0 + 3 = 3.\n2. Tens: 0 +5 =5.\n3. Hundreds: 8+9=17 (7 with carryover 1).\n4. Thousands: 1 +6 +1(carry)=8 (no carry).\n5. Ten thousands:9 +3 =12 (2 with carryover 1).\n6. Hundred thousands:2 +0 +1(carry)=3 (no carry).\n7. Millions:2 +3 =5.\n8. Ten millions:1 +5=6.\n9. Hundred millions:5 +6=11.\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to hundred millions) are:.\n\n3,5,7,8,2,3,5,6, and then the hundred millions place is 11. Wait, but 11 in the hundred millions place can't be, since each digit can only go up to 9. So 11 in the hundred millions place is actually 1 billion and 1 hundred million. So we need to carry over 1 to the billions place.\n\nTherefore, the total sum would be:.\n\nBillions place: 1 (from the carryover of 11 in hundred millions).\n\nHundred millions place: 1 (since 11 -10 =1).\n\nThen the rest of the digits as above.\n\nSo putting all together, the total sum is:.\n\n1 (billion) 1 (hundred million), then the remaining digits:.\n\n1 (billion) 1 (hundred million), 6 (ten million), 5 (million), 3 (hundred thousand), 2 (ten thousand), 8 (thou