ns:3.\n\nTen millions:2.\n\nHundred millions:12.\n\nBut since hundred millions can't be 12, that would actually be 1,2 in the hundred millions place, meaning we have to carry over again. Wait, did I miss that?.\n\nHold on! When adding the hundred millions place: 5 +7 =12. So that is 2 in the hundred millions place and carryover 1 to the billions place. Since there are no digits beyond hundred millions in either number, that 1 becomes a new digit in the billions place. .\n\nTherefore, the total should be 1 (billion), 2 (hundred millions), then the rest as above. Let me reconstruct the number step by step.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nDigits:.\n\nOnes:5.\n\nTens:8.\n\nHundreds:2.\n\nThousands:4.\n\nTen thousands:2.\n\nHundred thousands:9.\n\nMillions:3.\n\nTen millions:2.\n\nHundred millions:2 (from 12, but actually we have to carry over 1 to billions).\n\nWait, no. Let me retrace:.\n\nWhen adding the hundred millions digits (5 and7), that gives 12. So the hundred millions place is 2, and we carry over 1 to the billions place. Since neither number has a digit in the billions place, that carried-over 1 becomes the billions digit. So the total would be:.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nHundred millions place:2.\n\nThen ten millions:2.\n\nMillions:3.\n\nHundred thousands:9.\n\nTen thousands:2.\n\nThousands:4.\n\nHundreds:2.\n\nTens:8.\n\nOnes:5.\n\nPutting that all together:.\n\n1 (billion), 2 (hundred million), 2 (ten million), 3 (million), 9 (hundred thousand), 2 (ten thousand), 4 (thousand), 2 (hundred), 8 (ten), 5 (one). Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
