h digit from right):.\n\nFirst number: 9 (since it's 9**5**6..).\n\nSecond number: 5.\n\nWait, maybe breaking down each digit:.\n\nFirst number: 9 (billions), 5 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 2 (tens?), wait no, maybe I messed up the places.\n\nWait, standard place values:.\n\nFor a 9-digit number:.\n\nDigits from right (units):.\n\n1: units (ones).\n\n2: tens.\n\n3: hundreds.\n\n4: thousands.\n\n5: ten thousands.\n\n6: hundred thousands.\n\n7: millions.\n\n8: ten millions.\n\n9: hundred millions.\n\n10: billions, etc.\n\nWait, so 956,206,294 is 9 hundred millions, 5 ten millions, 6 millions, 2 hundred thousands, 0 ten thousands, 6 thousands, 2 hundreds, 9 tens, 4 ones.\n\nSimilarly, 534,101,039 is 5 hundred millions, 3 ten millions, 4 millions, 1 hundred thousands, 0 ten thousands, 1 thousand, 0 hundreds, 3 tens, 9 ones.\n\nSo when adding, let's go digit by digit from right to left:.\n\nOnes place: 4 + 9 = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 9 + 3 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 2 + 0 = 2 + 1 = 3. Write 3, no carryover.\n\nThousands: 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7.\n\nTen thousands: 0 + 0 = 0.\n\nHundred thousands: 2 + 4 = 6.\n\nMillions: 6 + 3 = 9.\n\nTen millions: 5 + 4 = 9.\n\nHundred millions: 9 + 5 = 14. So here, 14. Since this is the hundred millions place, writing 4 and carrying over 1 to the billions place.\n\nBut since there are no digits beyond the hundred millions except the carryover, that 1 becomes a new digit at the start.\n\nSo putting it all together, starting from the right:.\n\nOnes:3, tens:3, hundreds:3, thousands:7, ten thousands:0, hundred thousands:6, millions:9, ten millions:9, hundred millions:4, and then the carried over 1 in the billions place. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
