s), 8 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 9 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 8 + 0 = 8.\n\nTens: 5 + 5 = 10, write 0, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 4 + 7 = 11, plus 1 is 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 5 + 2 = 7, plus 1 is 8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands: 7 + 3 = 10. Wait, the first number's ten thousands place is 7, second's is 3? Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, first number is 251,975,458. Breaking down:.\n\n2 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 982,132,750: 9 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nSo, ten thousands place: first number has 7, second has 3. 7 + 3 = 10. So write 0, carryover 1. Wait, but in the previous step, the thousands place had a carryover? Wait, let's go step by step again carefully.\n\nStarting over:.\n\nOnes: 8 + 0 = 8. So ones place is 8.\n\nTens: 5 (from first number) + 5 (from second number) = 10. So write 0, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 4 + 7 = 11, plus carryover 1 = 12. So write 2, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 5 (first) + 2 (second) = 7, plus carryover 1 = 8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands: 7 (first) + 3 (second) = 10. So write 0, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands: 9 (first) + 1 (second) = 10, plus carryover 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nMillions: 1 (first) + 2 (second) = 3, plus carryover 1 = 4. Write 4, no carryover.\n\nTen millions: 5 (first) + 8 (second) = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions: 2 (first) + 9 (second) = 11, plus carryover 1 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1 to the next (billions) place.\n\nWait, but the first number is only 251 million, so the hundred millions place is 2, and the second is 9, so adding them gives 11, plus the carryover 1 makes 12. So we have to write 2 and carryover 1 to the billions place.\n\nSo the total would be 1 (from the carryover) followed by 2, then the rest. Wait, let me put all the digits together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes:8.\n\nTens:0.\n\nHundreds:2.\n\nThousands:8.\n\nTen thousands:0.\n\nHundred thousands:1.\n\nMillions:4.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nHundred millions:2.\n\nBillions:1 (carryover).\n\nSo putting them all together: 1 (billions), 2 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 8 (ones). Wait, that can't be right. Let me check again.\n\nWait, when we get to the hundred millions place:.\n\nHundred millions place: 2 + 9 = 11, plus the carryover from the ten millions place which was 1? Wait, let me retrace.\n\nWait, the ten millions place: first number has 5, second has 8. 5+8=13, so we write 3 and carryover 1. Then the hundred millions place: 2 +9 =11, plus the carryover 1 is 12. So that gives 2 in the hundred millions place and carryover 1 to the billions. So the billions place is 1.\n\nSo the total number is 1,2 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 8 (ones). Wait, but the order should be:.\n\nBillions digit first, then hundred millions, ten millions, etc. So putting it all together:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:2.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nMillions:4.\n\nHundred thousands:1.\n\nTen thousands:0.\n\nThousands:8.\n\nHundreds:2.\n\nTens:0.\n\nOnes:8. Therefore, my final answer is: "]] 65%|######4   | 323/500 [9:18:12<4:02:31, 82.21s/it]
