en millions place. So 9 (first) +4 (second) =13. So we need to put 3 here and carryover1?.\n\nWait, but the carryover from the previous step was already handled in the hundred thousands place. Wait, let me retrace:.\n\nWait, let's go step by step again, perhaps my earlier steps were confused.\n\nStarting over:.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically with their digits aligned properly:.\n\n          5 9 1 9 3 1 3 7 6.\n        + 7 4 4 0 8 9 6 0 1.\n        ----------------------.\n\nStarting from the right (position 1, ones):.\n\nPosition9 (ones): 6 +1 =7. Write down 7.\n\nPosition8 (tens):7 +0=7. Write 7.\n\nPosition7 (hundreds):3 +6=9. Write 9.\n\nPosition6 (thousands):1 +9=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3 +8 +1 (carryover)=12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (hundred thousands):9 +0 +1=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (millions):1 (from first number) +4 (from second number) +1 (carryover)=6. Write6, no carryover.\n\nPosition2 (ten millions):9 (first) +4 (second)=13. So write 3, carryover1.\n\nPosition1 (hundred millions):5 (first) +7 (second) +1 (carryover)=13. So write 3 and carryover1. But since there are no more digits, we have to add that 1 as a new digit at the beginning.\n\nWait, so the total digits would be 10 digits now. Let me check:.\n\nStarting from the highest place:.\n\nHundred millions place addition resulted in 13, so we had 5 +7=12 plus the carryover 1 from the ten millions place? Wait, no, let's re-express each step carefully.\n\nWait, Position2 is ten millions:.\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 9, second's is4. So 9 +4=13. So we write 3 in the ten millions place and carryover1 to the hundred millions place.\n\nThen Position1 (hundred millions): first number has5, second has7. 5+7 + the carryover1= 13. So we write 3 in the hundred millions place and carryover1 to the billions place.\n\nSince there are no more digits, that carryover1 becomes a new digit at the billions place.\n\nSo putting all together:.\n\nThe result is:.\n\n1 (billions place) 3 (hundred millions) 3 (ten millions) 6 (millions) 0 (hundred thousands) 2 (ten thousands) 0 (thousands?) Wait, let me go step by step.\n\nWait, let me list all the digits from highest to lowest:.\n\nStarting from the billions place (position10):.\n\nThe carryover from the hundred millions addition was 1, so that's the first digit:1.\n\nThen hundred millions place:3 (from 5+7+1 carryover? Wait, maybe I need to track each position again.\n\nWait perhaps I should write down each digit step by step:.\n\nAfter adding each column:.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1 (billions): carryover from hundred millions place addition? Let's see.\n\nWait, let's list all the digits from right to left:.\n\nAfter calculating each position:.\n\nStarting from the right (position9 to position1):.\n\nPosition9 (ones):7.\n\nPosition8 (tens):7.\n\nPosition7 (hundreds):9.\n\nPosition6 (thousands):0 (with carryover1).\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):2 (with carryover1).\n\nPosition4 (hundred thousands):0 (with carryover1).\n\nPosition3 (millions):6.\n\nPosition2 (ten millions):3 (with carryover1).\n\nPosition1 (hundred millions):3 (with carryover1 to billions).\n\nThen the final carryover1 for the billions place.\n\nSo the digits from left to right (highest to lowest):.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:3.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nHundred thousands:0.\n\nTen thousands:2.\n\nThousands:0.\n\nHundreds:9.\n\nTens:7.\n\nOnes:7. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
