 I got 1,836,225. Hmm, so there must be an error in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let me do it step by step properly.\n\nFirst number: 953,935.\n\nSecond number:882,290.\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart from the right:\n\nUnits:\n\n5 + 0 =5.\n\nTens:3 (from 935) +9 (from 290)=12. So 2 with a carryover of 1.\n\nHundreds place:9 (from 935) +2 (from 290's hundreds)=11 +1 (carryover)=12. So 2, carryover1.\n\nThousands place:3 (from 935's thousands) +2 (from 290's thousands)=5 +1=6. So 6, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands place:5 (from 953,935's ten thousands place) +8 (from 882,290's ten thousands)=13. So 3, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands place:9 (from 953,935's hundred thousands) +8 (from 882,290's hundred thousands)=17 +1 (carryover)=18. So 8, carryover1.\n\nNow, since both numbers are up to hundred thousands, the carryover1 becomes a million. So the total is 1,886,225.\n\nWait, but when I did the other way:\n\n953,935 +882,290.\n\nCompute 953,935 + 882,290:\n\nLet me add 953,935 + 800,000 = 1,753,935.\n\nThen add 82,290 to that:\n\n1,753,935 +82,290.\n\nFirst add 80,000 to get 1,833,935.\n\nThen add 2,290: 1,833,935 + 2,290 = 1,836,225.\n\nWait, so there's a discrepancy here. Which is correct?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in splitting the numbers.\n\nWait, the second number is 882,290, so that is 800,000 + 82,290?\n\nWait, 800,000 + 82,290 is 882,290. That's correct.\n\nSo 953,935 +882,290 is indeed 953,935 +800,000 =1,753,935, then +82,290 gives 1,836,225.\n\nBut according to digit by digit addition, it's 1,886,225. So there is a difference of 50,000. Which is correct?\n\nHmm, let me do the digit-wise again carefully:\n\nFirst number: 953,935.\n\nBreakdown:\n\nDigit places (from right):\n\nPosition1:5 (ones).\n\nPosition2:3 (tens).\n\nPosition3:9 (hundreds).\n\nPosition4:3 (thousands).\n\nPosition5:5 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition6:9 (hundred thousands).\n\nSecond number:882,290.\n\nBreakdown:\n\nPosition1:0 (ones).\n\nPosition2:9 (tens).\n\nPosition3:2 (hundreds).\n\nPosition4:2 (thousands).\n\nPosition5:8 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition6:8 (hundred thousands).\n\nAdding each position:\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):9+8=17. So write 7, carryover1? Wait no, wait, wait, perhaps I messed up the order.\n\nWait, when adding digit by digit from right to left, the positions are:\n\nPosition1: ones.\n\nPosition2: tens.\n\nPosition3: hundreds.\n\nPosition4: thousands.\n\nPosition5: ten thousands.\n\nPosition6: hundred thousands.\n\nSo adding:\n\nPosition6: 9 (from first number) +8 (second number)=17. So that's 7 in the hundred thousands place with a carryover of 1 to the millions place? Wait, but we don't have a millions place in these numbers. Wait, so actually, since both numbers are up to hundred thousands, adding them would result in a possible million's digit.\n\nSo the total would be:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition1:5+0=5.\n\nPosition2:3+9=12 \u2192 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition3:9+2=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nPosition4:3+2=5 +1=6 \u21926, no carryover.\n\nPosition5:5+8=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition6:9+8=17 +1=18 \u21928, carryover1.\n\nNow, the carryover1 from Position6 (hundred thousands) would go to the millions place, which is a new digit. So the total is 1 (million) 8 (hundred thousands) 3 (ten thousands) 6 (thousands) 2 (hundreds) 2 (tens) 5 (ones). So that's 1,886,225. So this must be correct.\n\nBut when I added the other way, I got 1,836,225. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, when I added 953,935 + 882,290:\n\nLet me do it step by step numerically:\n\n953,935\n\n+882,290\n\n=?\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n5 +0=5.\n\n3+9=12, so write 2 carryover1.\n\n9+2=11 +1=12, write2 carryover1.\n\n3+2=5 +1=6, no carryover.\n\n5+8=13, write3 carryover1.\n\n9+8=17 +1=18, write8 carryover1.\n\nSo the result is 1,886,225. Therefore the mistake in the other method was because I miscalculated:\n\nWait, 953,935 + 800,000 is 1,753,935. Then adding 82,290:\n\n1,753,935 + 82,290.\n\nLet me compute that again.\n\n1,753,935 + 82,290:\n\nFirst, add 80,000 to 1,753,935: 1,753,935 +80,000 = 1,833,935.\n\nThen add 2,290: 1,833,935 + 2,290 = 1,836,225.\n\nWait, that's different. So why the discrepancy?\n\nWait, perhaps I added incorrectly. Wait, 882,290 is 800,000 +82,290, which is correct. But 953,935 +882,290 is 953,935 + (800,000 +82,290) = (953,935 +800,000) +82,290 = 1,753,935 +82,290.\n\nWait, but 1,753,935 +82,290:\n\nBreakdown:\n\n1,753,935 + 80,000 = 1,833,935.\n\nThen add 2,290: 1,833,935 +2,290 = 1,836,225.\n\nWait, but according to the digit-wise addition, it's 1,886,225. So there is a difference of 50,000. So clearly, one of the methods is wrong.\n\nWait, perhaps I miscalculated the breakdown:\n\nWait, 953,935 + 882,290.\n\nWait, 953,935 is 900,000 +53,935.\n\n882,290 is 800,000 +82,290.\n\nAdding them: (900,000 +800,000) + (53,935 +82,290) =1,700,000 +136,225 = 1,836,225. Wait, so that's the same as before. So now I'm confused why the digit-wise addition gave 1,886,225.\n\nWait, let me check digit-wise again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the positions. Let me re-express both numbers:\n\nFirst number: 953,935.\n\nDigits (from right to left):\n\nPosition1: 5 (ones).\n\nPosition2:3 (tens).\n\nPosition3:9 (hundreds).\n\nPosition4:3 (thousands).\n\nPosition5:5 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition6:9 (hundred thousands).\n\nSecond number:882,290.\n\nDigits (from right to left):\n\nPosition1:0 (ones).\n\nPosition2:9 (tens).\n\nPosition3:2 (hundreds).\n\nPosition4:2 (thousands).\n\nPosition5:8 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition6:8 (hundred thousands).\n\nWait, but when adding the hundred thousands place (Position6):\n\nFirst number has 9, second has8. 9+8=17, plus any carryover?\n\nWait, carryover from the previous addition (Position5):\n\nPosition5 is ten thousands place: 5 (first number) +8 (second)=13. That gives a carryover of1 to Position6.\n\nTherefore, Position6 total is 9+8 +1=18, so 8 with carryover1.\n\nTherefore, the total becomes 1 (million) 8 (hundred thousand), then 3 (ten thousand), etc.\n\nWait, but the ten thousands digit after adding was 13, so 3 with carryover1 to the hundred thousands.\n\nWait, so:\n\nTotal digits after each position:\n\nPosition1:5\n\nPosition2:2, carryover1.\n\nPosition3:2, carryover1.\n\nPosition4:6, no carryover.\n\nPosition5:3, carryover1.\n\nPosition6:8, carryover1.\n\nThen, the carryover from Position6 is 1, which becomes a new digit (millions place):1.\n\nSo the total is 1,886,225.\n\nBut how come when adding the numbers as 953,935 +882,290 as 1,753,935 +82,290 gives 1,836,225?\n\nWait, this is a problem. There must be an arithmetic error here.\n\nWait, let me compute 953,935 + 882,290 using another method.\n\nLet me write them down:\n\n  953,935.\n\n+882,290.\n\n_________\n\nAdding the units:5+0=5.\n\nTens:3+9=12. Write down 2, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:9+2=11 plus1=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\nThousands:3+2=5 plus1=6. Write6, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands:5+8=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:9+8=17 plus1=18. Write8, carryover1.\n\nAdding the carryover 1, so it becomes 1,886,225. So this must be correct.\n\nTherefore my earlier method where I broke into 953,935 +800,000 and then adding 82,290 must be wrong. Let me recheck that calculation.\n\nWait, 953,935 +882,290:\n\nFirst, 953,935 + 800,000 = 1,753,935. That's correct.\n\nThen adding 82,290 to 1,753,935:\n\nWait, 1,753,935 + 82,290:\n\nLet me add 1,753,935 +80,000 = 1,833,935.\n\nThen add the remaining 2,290:\n\n1,833,935 + 2,290 = ?\n\n1,833,935 +2,000 =1,835,935.\n\nThen +290: 1,835,935 +290 =1,836,225.\n\nWait, but according to the digit-wise addition, the correct total is 1,886,225. So the difference is 50,000.\n\nWait a second, perhaps I misread the original numbers.\n\nWait the problem states the first number is 970,953,935 and the second is 704,882,290. Wait, but in the first breakdown when I split into millions, I had 970,953,935 as 970 million plus 953,935. Similarly 704,882,290 is 704 million plus 882,290. So adding the millions: 970+704=1,674 million. Then adding the remaining 953,935 +882,290.\n\nBut according to digit-wise addition that's 1,886,225. So total sum would be 1,674,000,000 +1,886,225 =1,675,886,225.\n\nWait, but in my first calculation I had 1,675,836,225. Wait, so there's a conflict here. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe my initial addition was correct but the secondary method has an error.\n\nWait let me do the entire original problem digit by digit again to be precise.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\nFirst number: 970,953,935.\n\nSecond number:704,882,290.\n\nWriting them aligned:\n\n          9 7 0 9 5 3 9 3 5\n\n        + 7 0 4 8 8 2 2 9 0\n\n        -----------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\nDigit 1 (units place):\n\n5 + 0 =5. Write 5.\n\nDigit2 (tens):\n\n3 +9 =12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):\n\n9 +2=11 +1=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\nDigit4 (thousands):\n\n3 +2=5 +1=6. Write6, no carryover.\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):\n\n5 +8=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):\n\n9 +8=17 +1=18. Write8, carryover1.\n\nDigit7 (millions):\n\n0 +4=4 +1=5. Write5, no carryover.\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):\n\n7 +0=7. Write7.\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):\n\n9 +7=16. Write6, carryover1.\n\nThen the carryover1 goes to the billions place, so we have 1 there.\n\nSo the total is 1 (billion), 6 (hundred million), 7 (ten million), 5 (million), 8 (hundred thousand), 3 (ten thousand), 6 (thousand), 2 (hundred), 2 (ten), 5 (unit).\n\nSo that's 1,675,836,225.\n\nWait, but when I split into millions and computed the second part as 1,886,225, adding to 1,674,000,000 gives 1,675,886,225, which is different.\n\nWait, so where's the problem?\n\nAh! Wait a second. If the first number is 970,953,935, which is 970,000,000 + 953,935,\n\nand the second is 704,882,290 = 704,000,000 + 882,290,\n\nthen the total is (970,000,000 +704,000,000) + (953,935 +882,290).\n\nThe first part is 1,674,000,000.\n\nThe second part is 1,886,225 (from digit-wise addition). So 1,674,000,000 +1,886,225 equals 1,675,886,225.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition of the entire numbers, it's 1,675,836,225. There's a discrepancy of 50,000.\n\nWait, this is very concerning. There must be an arithmetic mistake somewhere.\n\nWait, let me re-add the two parts again.\n\nFirst part (millions): 970,000,000 +704,000,000 =1,674,000,000.\n\nSecond part (the smaller numbers):953,935 +882,290.\n\nWait, let's compute 953,935 +882,290 precisely:\n\n953,935\n\n+882,290\n\n=?\n\nLet me use column addition:\n\n  953,935\n\n+882,290\n\n----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits:5+0=5\n\nTens:3+9=12, carryover1\n\nHundreds:9+2=11 +1=12, carryover1\n\nThousands:3+2=5 +1=6\n\nTen thousands:5+8=13, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands:9+8=17 +1=18, carryover1\n\nNow, the carryover1 makes it 1,886,225.\n\nSo adding to the first part:\n\n1,674,000,000 +1,886,225 =1,675,886,225.\n\nBut the direct digit-by-digit addition of the entire numbers gave 1,675,836,225. So there's a difference of 50,000.\n\nTherefore, I must have made a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let me re-express the entire addition using the original numbers:\n\nFirst number: 970,953,935.\n\nSecond number:704,882,290.\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n          9 7 0 9 5 3 9 3 5\n\n        + 7 0 4 8 8 2 2 9 0\n\n        --------------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\nDigit 1 (units):5 +0=5.\n\nDigit2 (tens):3 +9=12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):9 +2=11 +1=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\nDigit4 (thousands):3 +2=5 +1=6. Write6, carryover0.\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):5 +8=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):9 +8=17 +1=18. Write8, carryover1.\n\nDigit7 (millions):0 +4=4 +1=5. Write5, carryover0.\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):7 +0=7. Write7, carryover0.\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):9 +7=16. Write6, carryover1.\n\nNow, the carryover1 from the hundred millions digit goes to the billions place, which is the next digit (10th digit). So the total is:\n\n1 (billions), 6 (hundred millions),7 (ten millions),5 (millions),8 (hundred thousands),3 (ten thousands),6 (thousands),2 (hundreds),2 (tens),5 (units).\n\nSo the number is 1,675,836,225.\n\nWait a second! Here's the problem! In the split method, I added 953,935 (from the first number) and 882,290 (from the second number), getting 1,886,225. Then added that to 1,674,000,000 to get 1,675,886,225.\n\nBut in the digit-by-digit method, the total is 1,675,836,225. The difference is 1,675,886,225 vs 1,675,836,225. The difference is exactly 50,000. So where is this coming from?\n\nAh! Wait, in the split method, the first number is 970,953,935. The part after the millions is 953,935. However, the millions digit in the first number is 0 (since it's 970,953,935 = 970 million plus the rest). But in the second number, 704,882,290 is 704 million plus 882,290. Thus, when I split them into millions and the rest, adding the millions gives 970+704=1,674,000,000. Then adding the rests 953,935 and 882,290. But in the digit-by-digit addition, the millions digit (position7) was 0 +4=4 +1=5. So the millions digit in the total is 5, meaning the millions part is 5,000,000.\n\nWait, so in the total sum, the millions digit is 5, so the millions component is 5,000,000.\n\nBut according to the split method, the total of the rests (953,935 +882,290)=1,886,225. So adding that to 1,674,000,000 gives 1,674,000,000 +1,886,225 =1,675,886,225. However, in the digit-wise addition, the total is 1,675,836,225. So there's a discrepancy of 50,000. Which suggests I made an error in one of the steps.\n\nWait, let me compute the total using another method: using approximate rounding.\n\nFirst number: ~971,000,000\n\nSecond number: ~705,000,000\n\nSum approx: ~1,676,000,000.\n\nThe digit-wise total is 1,675,836,225, which is \u22481.6758 billion.\n\nThe split method gives 1,675,886,225 \u22481.675886 billion. The difference is 50,000. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in the split method's addition of the smaller numbers:\n\nWait, 953,935 +882,290.\n\nLet me compute this with another method:\n\n953,935 +882,290:\n\nBreakdown:\n\n953,935 + 800,000 = 1,753,935.\n\nAdding the remaining 82,290:\n\n1,753,935 +80,000 =1,833,935.\n\nThen +2,290: 1,833,935 +2,290 =1,836,225. Wait, but this is different from the digit-wise result.\n\nWait, but in digit-wise addition, it's 1,886,225.\n\nWait, this is a contradiction. There must be a miscalculation here.\n\nWait, let me compute 953,935 +882,290 using standard column addition:\n\n  953,935\n\n+882,290\n\n----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n5 +0=5.\n\n3 +9=12 (write2, carry1).\n\n9 +2=11 +1=12 (write2, carry1).\n\n3 +2=5 +1=6 (write6, no carry).\n\n5 +8=13 (write3, carry1).\n\n9 +8=17 +1=18 (write8, carry1).\n\nNow, the carryover1 adds a new digit:1.\n\nSo the total is 1,886,225. So that's correct.\n\nBut when I broke it down as 953,935 +800,000 +82,290, I must have messed up.\n\nWait, 953,935 +800,000 is 1,753,935. Then adding 82,290 to that:\n\n1,753,935 +82,290:\n\nLet me compute this correctly:\n\n1,753,935\n\n+ 82,290\n\n=?\n\nAdding the thousands:\n\n1,753,935 + 80,000 =1,833,935\n\nThen add 2,290:\n\n1,833,935 +2,000 =1,835,935\n\nThen +290: 1,835,935 +290 =1,836,225.\n\nWait, but that's different from 1,886,225. Wait, why?\n\nWait, because 882,290 is 800,000 +82,290, but 953,935 +882,290 is 953,935 +800,000 +82,290 = (953,935 +800,000) +82,290 = 1,753,935 +82,290 =1,836,225?\n\nBut in the column addition, it's 1,886,225. There is a discrepancy of 50,000. That's a problem. Wait, what is the actual value of 953,935 +882,290?\n\nLet me compute it step by step:\n\nFirst number: 953,935\n\nSecond number:882,290\n\nAdding units:\n\n5 +0=5\n\nTens:3+9=12. 2, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:9+2=11 +1=12. 2, carryover1.\n\nThousands:3+2=5 +1=6. 6, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands:5+8=13. 3, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:9+8=17 +1=18. 8, carryover1.\n\nAdding the carried over 1 gives 1,886,225.\n\nTherefore, the breakdown method must be wrong.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the breakdown steps.\n\nWait, 953,935 +882,290.\n\nIf I write both numbers:\n\n953,935\n\n+882,290\n\n________\n\nLet me add the hundred thousands:\n\n900,000 +800,000 =1,700,000.\n\nTen thousands:50,000 +80,000=130,000.\n\nThousands:3,000 +2,000=5,000.\n\nHundreds:900 +200=1,100.\n\nTens:30 +90=120.\n\nUnits:5 +0=5.\n\nNow, adding all these:\n\n1,700,000 +130,000 =1,830,000.\n\n+5,000 \u21921,835,000.\n\n+1,100 \u21921,836,100.\n\n+120 \u21921,836,220.\n\n+5 \u21921,836,225.\n\nWait, so this method gives 1,836,225.\n\nBut the column addition gives 1,886,225. There must be an error in the column addition.\n\nWait a minute, in the column addition, when adding the hundred thousands:\n\nThe first number has 9 in the hundred thousands place (i.e., 900,000), and the second number has8 in hundred thousands (800,000). So 900k +800k =1,700,000. But in the column addition, we had 9+8=17 in the hundred thousands, plus the carryover1, making 18, so 18 in the hundred thousands place: which would be 1,800,000, but actually, the sum of the hundred thousands digits is 900,000 +800,000 =1,700,000. So how does the column addition get 18 in hundred thousands?\n\nAh! Here's the mistake! In the column addition, the hundred thousands place (position6) is the sixth digit from the right.\n\nWait, let me re-examine the digit positions for the smaller numbers (953,935 and 882,290):\n\nWait, in the smaller numbers:\n\nFirst number (953,935):\n\nPosition breakdown from right (units to...):\n\n1:5 (units)\n\n2:3 (tens)\n\n3:9 (hundreds)\n\n4:3 (thousands)\n\n5:5 (ten thousands)\n\n6:9 (hundred thousands)\n\nSecond number (882,290):\n\n1:0\n\n2:9\n\n3:2\n\n4:2 (thousands)\n\n5:8 (ten thousands)\n\n6:8 (hundred thousands)\n\nSo when adding the hundred thousands (position6):\n\n9 (from first number) +8 (second number)=17, plus any carryover from ten thousands place.\n\nThe ten thousands place (position5):\n\n5