      "text": "1,348,012,385?\n\nWait, that doesn't align with my previous thought. Hmm, maybe I messed up the digit positions. Let me try again.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's easier to add the numbers digit by digit step by step, writing down each step.\n\nLet me write the numbers with commas for clarity:\n\nFirst number: 649,239,260\n\nSecond number: 698,773,125\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me write them aligned properly:\n\n        649,239,260\n\n      +698,773,125\n\n      ----------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nOnes place: 0 + 5 = 5\n\nTens place: 6 + 2 = 8\n\nHundreds place: 2 + 1 = 3\n\nThousands place: 9 + 3 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place: 3 + 7 = 10 + 1 (carryover) = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: 2 + 7 = 9 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nMillions place: 9 + 8 = 17 +1 = 18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nTen millions place: 4 + 9 = 13 + 1 =14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions place: 6 + 6 =12 +1=13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nBillions place: The carryover 1, since both numbers only have up to hundred millions place. So the billions place is 1.\n\nPutting it all together from left to right:\n\n1 (billion), 3 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,348,012,385.\n\nWait, but let me check that again because when I first thought I got 1,347,012,385, but now with this step-by-step, it's 1,348,012,385? Hmm, where is the discrepancy?\n\nWait, let's check the millions place again. The millions place in the first number is 9 (from 649,239,260: the millions digit is the 9 in the millions place?), let me parse the numbers properly.\n\nWait, perhaps the confusion is arising from how the commas are placed. Let me confirm the digit positions.\n\nIn the first number: 649,239,260.\n\nBreaking it down with commas as thousand separators:\n\n649,239,260 is 649 (million), 239 (thousand), 260.\n\n