t. Let me write both numbers with commas to see:.\n\nFirst number: 121,876,445.\nSo commas after every three digits starting from the right. So that's 121 (million), 876 (thousand), 445.\n\nSecond number: 967,866,178.\nSimilarly: 967 (million), 866 (thousand), 178.\n\nTherefore, when written out:.\n\nFirst number: 1 2 1 8 7 6 4 4 5.\nBut actually, in digits:.\n\n1 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nWait, maybe I need to align them properly. Let me write both numbers digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 1 (100,000,000), 2 (10,000,000), 1 (1,000,000), 8 (100,000), 7 (10,000), 6 (1,000), 4 (100), 4 (10), 5 (1).\n\nSecond number: 9 (100,000,000), 6 (10,000,000), 7 (1,000,000), 8 (100,000), 6 (10,000), 6 (1,000), 1 (100), 7 (10), 8 (1).\n\nSo aligning them:.\n\nHundred millions: 1 vs 9.\n\nTen millions: 2 vs 6.\n\nMillions: 1 vs 7.\n\nHundred thousands: 8 vs 8.\n\nTen thousands: 7 vs 6.\n\nThousands: 6 vs 6.\n\nHundreds: 4 vs 1.\n\nTens: 4 vs 7.\n\nOnes: 5 vs 8.\n\nWait, so when adding, we start from the rightmost digit (ones):.\n\nOnes place: 5 + 8 = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nTens place: 4 + 7 + 1 (carry) = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place: 4 + 1 + 1 = 6. Wait, 4 (from first number's hundreds place) plus 1 (from second's hundreds place) is 5, plus the carryover 1 makes 6. So write 6, no carryover.\n\nWait, hold on, let me check again. Hundreds place:.\n\nFirst number has 4, second has 1. 4 +1 =5, plus the carryover 1 from the previous step (which was 1 from the tens place addition). So 5 +1 =6. So yes, 6 with no carryover.\n\nMoving to thousands place: first number's thousands is 6, second's is 6. 6 +6 =12. Plus no carryover. So 12. So write 2, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place: first number has 7, second has 6. 7+6 =13. Plus the carryover 1 gives 14. So write 4, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: first has 8, second has 8. 8+8=16. Plus carryover 1 =17. So write 7, carryover 1.\n\nMillions place: first has 1, second has 7. 1+7=8, plus carryover 1 =9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nTen millions place: first has 2, second has 6. 2+6=8. No carryover. Write 8.\n\nHundred millions place: first has 1, second has 9. 1+9=10. Write 0 and carryover 1.\n\nWait, but since we're at the highest digit, we need to add that carryover as a new digit. So the total would be 1 followed by the rest.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the ones digit:.\n\nOnes: 3.\n\nTens:2.\n\nHundreds:6.\n\nThousands:2 (from the thousands place addition 12, so 2 with carryover 1).\n\nWait, let me retrace step by step to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Let's go through each digit carefully.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n1. Ones place:.\n   5 (from first) +8 (second) =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n2. Tens place:.\n   4 +7 =11, plus 1 carried =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\n3. Hundreds place:.\n   4 (first) +1 (second) =5, plus 1 carried =6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\n4. Thousands place:.\n   6 +6 =12, plus 0 carried =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\n5. Ten thousands place:.\n   7 (first) +6 (second) =13, plus 1 carried =14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands place:.\n   8 +8 =16, plus 1 carried =17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\n7. Millions place:.\n   1 (first) +7 (second) =8, plus 1 carried =9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\n8. Ten millions place:.\n   2 (first) +6 (second) =8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\n9. Hundred millions place:.\n   1 (first) +9 (second) =10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nNow, since there's a carryover after the hundred millions place, we need to add an additional digit at the beginning: 1.\n\nSo putting all the digits together from right to left, but we need to reverse the order. Let me list the digits in order from highest to lowest:.\n\nThe carryover 1 is the next digit beyond hundred millions, so that's the billions place. So the digits are:.\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nWait, let me structure it properly:.\n\nStarting from the highest digit:.\n\nBillions place: 1.\n\nHundred millions: 0 (from 1+9=10, so the 0 is here, and the 1 is the billion).\n\nTen millions: 8 (from 2+6=8).\n\nMillions: 9 (from 1+7+1=9?).\n\nWait, let's see:.\n\nWait, when we did the millions place: first number's millions digit is 1, second's is 7, plus the carryover from hundred thousands (which was 1). So 1+7+1=9. So yes, millions place is 9.\n\nHundred thousands place was 8+8+1 (carryover from ten thousands) which was 17, so 7 with carryover 1.\n\nWait, let me list each place step by step:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 3.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):2.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):6.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):2 (from 6+6+carryover? Wait, let's go step by step again.\n\nWait maybe I should write down each result step by step as I go through each digit:.\n\nLet me try again, more carefully.\n\nLet me write both numbers aligned:.\n\n          1 2 1 8 7 6 4 4 5.\n        + 9 6 7 8 6 6 1 7 8.\n        --------------------.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 5 +8 =13. Write down 3, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 4 +7 =11, plus 1 is 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 4 +1 =5, plus 1 is 6. Write 6, carryover 0.\n\nThousands: 6 +6 =12, plus 0 is 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:7 +6 =13, plus 1 is14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:8 +8 =16, plus1 is17. Write7, carryover1.\n\nMillions:1 +7 =8, plus1 is9. Write9, carryover0.\n\nTen millions:2 +6 =8. Write8, carryover0.\n\nHundred millions:1 +9 =10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nNow, the final carryover1 is added as the next digit (billions place). So the total is:.\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 3 (ones). Therefore, my final answer is: "]] 70%|#######   | 350/500 [9:58