ect places. Let me write them out:.\n\n878,962,317.\n\n143,379,661.\n\nBreaking them down digit by digit:.\n\nBillions place: 8 (from first) and 1 (from second).\n\nHundred millions:7 and4.\n\nTen millions:8 and3.\n\nMillions:9 and3.\n\nHundred thousands:6 and7.\n\nTen thousands:2 and9.\n\nThousands:3 and6.\n\nHundreds:1 and6.\n\nTens:3 and ?.\n\nWait, maybe I need to write them out with commas:.\n\nFirst number: 8,7,8,9,6,2,3,1,7.\n\nSecond number:1,4,3,3,7,9,6,6,1.\n\nWait, perhaps the confusion is because when written with commas, 878,962,317 is actually 8 (hundred millions?), no. Wait, actually, in the standard format, commas separate every three digits. So 878,962,317 is 878 million, 962 thousand, 317. So the digits are:.\n\n8 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nSimilarly, 143,379,661 is 1 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nSo aligning each digit:.\n\nBillions place: 8 (first number) and 1 (second number). Wait, actually, the first number is 878,962,317, which is less than a billion? Wait, 878 million is under a billion. The second number is 143 million, so both are under a billion. So the highest place is hundred millions.\n\nLet me list each digit from right to left (starting from ones):.\n\nFirst number: 7 (ones), 1 (tens), 3 (hundreds), 2 (thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 9 (hundred thousands), 8 (millions), 7 (ten millions), 8 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number:1 (ones),6 (tens),6 (hundreds),9 (thousands),7 (ten thousands),3 (hundred thousands),3 (millions),4 (ten millions),1 (hundred millions).\n\nSo adding digit by digit:.\n\nOnes place: 7 +1 =8.\n\nTens:1+6=7.\n\nHundreds:3+6=9.\n\nThousands:2+9=11, write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:6+7=13 +1=14, write 4, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands:9+3=12 +1=13, write 3, carryover1.\n\nMillions:8+3=11 +1=12, write 2, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:7+4=11 +1=12, write 2, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:8+1=9 +1=10, write 0, carryover1.\n\nNow, since there's a carryover 1 after the hundred millions, that becomes the billions place:1.\n\nSo putting all together from the highest to the lowest:.\n\n1 (billion), 0 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nWait, let me check the positions again:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes:8.\n\nTens:7.\n\nHundreds:9.\n\nThousands:1 (with carryover).\n\nWait, maybe I need to reconstruct the digits step by step.\n\nWait, the result digits from the addition steps:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):.\n\n1. Ones:8.\n\n2. Tens:7.\n\n3. Hundreds:9.\n\n4. Thousands:1 (with a carryover of 1).\n\n5. Ten thousands:4 (from 13 + carryover 1? Wait, let me retrace.\n\nWait, let me list each step again more carefully:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\n1. Ones place: 7 +1 =8. Write 8. No carryover.\n\n2. Tens place:1 +6 =7. Write 7. No carryover.\n\n3. Hundreds place:3 +6 =9. Write9. No carryover.\n\n4. Thousands place:2 +9 =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n5. Ten thousands place:6 +7 =13 +1 (carryover) =14. Write 4, carryover1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands place:9 +3 =12 +1 (carryover) =13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\n7. Millions place:8 +3 =11 +1 (carryover) =12. Write2, carryover1.\n\n8. Ten millions place:7 +4 =11 +1 (carryover) =12. Write2, carryover1.\n\n9. Hundred millions place:8 +1 =9 +1 (carryover) =10. Write0, carryover1.\n\n10. Now, since there are no more digits, we add the final carryover1 to the billions place. So the billions digit is 1.\n\nTherefore, the digits from left to right (billions to ones) are:.\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nSo putting it all together, the number i