messages:  [['Please solve the math problem.\nQuestion: What is the sum of 739909908 and 198831414?', "<think>Okay, so I need to add these two big numbers: 739,909,908 and 198,831,414. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. Since they are both whole numbers, I can just line them up and add each column, starting from the rightmost digit, right?.\n\nFirst, I'll write them down one under the other to make sure the digits are aligned properly. Let me see:.\n\n       739,909,908.\n     + 198,831,414.\n     --------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number has an 8 and the second has a 4. Adding those gives 12. So I write down 2 and carry over 1 to the next column.\n\nNext, the tens place: the first number has a 0, and the second has a 1. Adding those plus the carried over 1 gives 0 + 1 + 1 = 2. So I write 2 here. No carry-over.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 9 (from first number) and 4 (from second). 9 + 4 = 13. Write down 3 and carry over 1.\n\nNext, the thousands place: 9 (first) and 1 (second). 9 + 1 = 10, plus the carried over 1 makes 11. So write 1 and carry over 1 again.\n\nTen-thousands place: 0 (first) and 3 (second). 0 + 3 = 3, plus the carried 1 is 4. So write 4, no carry-over.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 9 (first) and 8 (second). 9 + 8 = 17. Write down 7 and carry over 1.\n\nMillions place: 3 (first number) and 8 (second). Wait, let me check again. Wait, hold on, maybe I miscounted the places. Let me verify the positions again because these numbers are big and it's easy to get confused.\n\nLet me break down each number digit by digit with their place values to be precise.\n\nFirst number: 739,909,908.\n\nBreaking it down:.\n\n- 7 (hundred millions place).\n- 3 (ten millions).\n- 9 (millions).\n- 9 (hundred thousands).\n- 0 (ten thousands).\n- 9 (thousands).\n- 9 (hundreds).\n- 0 (tens).\n- 8 (ones).\n\nWait, actually, let's parse it properly:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 8.\n\nTens: 0.\n\nHundreds: 9.\n\nThousands: 9.\n\nTen thousands: 0.\n\nHundred thousands: 9.\n\nMillions: 9.\n\nTen millions: 3.\n\nHundred millions: 7.\n\nSo the first number is 7 (hundred million), 3 (ten million), 9 (million), 9 (hundred thousand), 0 (ten thousand), 9 (thousand), 9 (hundred), 0 (ten), 8 (one).\n\nSecond number: 198,831,414.\n\nBreaking down similarly:.\n\nOnes: 4.\n\nTens: 1.\n\nHundreds: 4.\n\nThousands: 1.\n\nTen thousands: 3.\n\nHundred thousands: 8.\n\nMillions: 8.\n\nTen millions: 9.\n\nHundred millions: 1.\n\nWait, let me do this step by step:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 4.\n\nTens: 1.\n\nHundreds: 4.\n\nThousands: 1.\n\nTen thousands: 3.\n\nHundred thousands: 8.\n\nMillions: 8.\n\nTen millions: 9.\n\nHundred millions: 1.\n\nWait, actually, let me confirm:.\n\nThe second number is 198,831,414. Breaking into digits:.\n\nDigits: 1 (hundred million), 9 (ten million), 8 (million), 8 (hundred thousand), 3 (ten thousand), 1 (thousand), 4 (hundred), 1 (ten), 4 (one). Wait, maybe I messed up the breakdown here.\n\nWait, let's write it as:.\n\n1 9 8 8 3 1 4 1 4.\n\nSo positions from left to right:.\n\n1: hundred millions place.\n\n9: ten millions.\n\n8: millions.\n\n8: hundred thousands.\n\n3: ten thousands.\n\n1: thousands.\n\n4: hundreds.\n\n1: tens.\n\n4: ones.\n\nWait, that seems inconsistent. Let me count:.\n\nStarting from the left:.\n\nFirst digit: 1 (hundred million place).\n\nSecond digit: 9 (ten million).\n\nThird digit: 8 (million).\n\nFourth digit: 8 (hundred thousand).\n\nFifth digit: 3 (ten thousand).\n\nSixth digit: 1 (thousand).\n\nSeventh digit: 4 (hundred).\n\nEighth digit: 1 (ten).\n\nNinth digit: 4 (one). Okay, that's correct.\n\nSo when adding, aligning both numbers:.\n\nFirst number (739,909,908):.\n\nHundred millions:7.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nMillions:9.\n\nHundred thousands:9.\n\nTen thousands:0.\n\nThousands:9.\n\nHundreds:9.\n\nTens:0.\n\nOnes:8.\n\nSecond number (198,831,414):.\n\nHundred millions:1.\n\nTen millions:9.\n\nMillions:8.\n\nHundred thousands:8.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nThousands:1.\n\nHundreds:4.\n\nTens:1.\n\nOnes:4.\n\nNow, writing them vertically:.\n\n          7 3 9 9 0 9 9 0 8.\n\n        + 1 9 8 8 3 1 4 1 4.\n\n        --------------------.\n\nStarting from the ones place:.\n\nOnes: 8 + 4 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 0 + 1 = 1, plus carryover 1 = 2. Write 2, no carryover.\n\nHundreds: 9 + 4 =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nThousands:9 +1 =10, plus 1 =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:0 +3 =3, plus 1=4. Write 4.\n\nHundred thousands:9 +8=17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nMillions:9 +8=17, plus 1=18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nTen millions:3 +9=12, plus 1=13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:7 +1=8, plus 1=9. Write 9.\n\nWait, let me go step by step again carefully:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):.\n\n1st number: 8, 2nd number:4. 8+4=12. So digit is 2, carryover 1.\n\nNext digit (tens place):.\n\n1st number:0, 2nd:1. 0+1=1 + carryover 1 =2. So digit 2, carryover 0.\n\nHundreds place:.\n\n1st:9, 2nd:4. 9+4=13. Digit 3, carryover1.\n\nThousands:.\n\n1st:9, 2nd:1. 9+1=10 +1 (carryover)=11. Digit 1, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:.\n\n1st:0, 2nd:3. 0+3=3 +1=4. Digit4, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands:.\n\n1st:9, 2nd:8. 9+8=17. Digit7, carryover1.\n\nMillions place:.\n\n1st:9, 2nd:8. 9+8=17 +1=18. Digit8, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:.\n\n1st:3, 2nd:9. 3+9=12 +1=13. Digit3, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:.\n\n1st:7, 2nd:1. 7+1=8 +1=9. Digit9, no carryover.\n\nSo putting all digits together from right to left:.\n\nThe digits we got are:.\n\nOnes:2.\n\nTens:2.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nThousands:1.\n\nTen thousands:4.\n\nHundred thousands:7.\n\nMillions:8.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nHundred millions:9.\n\nSo the sum is 9 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 2 (ones).\n\nWait, let me arrange them properly:.\n\nStarting from the highest place:.\n\nHundred millions:9.\n\nTen m