 1 1.\n\nWait actually, let me check the exact digits. Let me count the digits in each number to ensure alignment.\n\nFirst number is 412,690,038. Breaking down:.\n\nDigits from left to right: 4 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 8 (ones). So that's 9 digits.\n\nSecond number is 352,532,811. Breaking down:.\n\n3 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 1 (ones). Also 9 digits. So when aligned, each digit position matches.\n\nLet me write them vertically:.\n\n      4 1 2 6 9 0 0 3 8.\n\n    + 3 5 2 5 3 2 8 1 1.\n\n    -----------------------.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 8 + 1 = 9.\n\nTens: 3 + 1 = 4.\n\nHundreds: 0 + 8 = 8.\n\nThousands: 0 + 2 = 2.\n\nTen thousands: 9 + 3 = 12. Write down 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands: 6 + 5 = 11, plus the carried 1 is 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nMillions: 2 + 2 = 4, plus 1 is 5. Write 5, no carryover.\n\nTen millions: 1 + 5 = 6.\n\nHundred millions: 4 + 3 = 7.\n\nPutting all together: 7 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 9 (ones). .\n\nWait, let me check again each digit:.\n\nStarting from the left (hundred millions place):.\n\n4 + 3 = 7. Correct.\n\nTen millions: 1 + 5 = 6. Correct.\n\nMillions: 2 + 2 = 4, but earlier I thought it was 5? Wait, where did that come from? Wait, let me recalculate step by step.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the millions place. Let's go through each digit again carefully.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 8 +1 =9.\n\nTens: 3 +1=4.\n\nHundreds:0+8=8.\n\nThousands:0 +2=2.\n\nTen thousands:9 (from first number) +3 (second number) =12. So write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands:6 (first) +5 (second) =11, plus 1 =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nMillions place: 2 (first) +2 (second) +1 (carryover) = 5. So 5, no carryover.\n\nTen millions place: 1 (first) +5 (second)=6.\n\nHundred millions:4+3=7.\n\nSo the result digits are:.\n\nHundred millions:7.\n\nTen millions:6.\n\nMillions:5.\n\nHundred thousands:2.\n\nTen thousands:2.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nHundreds:8.\n\nTens:4.\n\nOnes:9. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
