      "text": "935,123,066.\n\nWait, let me verify that step by step again because sometimes when adding, it's easy to make a mistake in the carryover.\n\nLet me do the addition column by column again, carefully:\n\nFirst, writing the numbers aligned:\n\n          2 7 5 7 6 9 7 0 5\n        + 6 5 9 3 5 3 3 6 1\n        ---------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1. Units place (position 1): 5 + 1 = 6. Write down 6. No carryover.\n\n2. Tens place (position 2): 0 + 6 = 6. Write down 6. No carryover.\n\n3. Hundreds place (position 3): 7 + 3 = 10. Write down 0, carryover 1.\n\n4. Thousands place (position 4): 9 + 3 = 12, plus the carried 1 = 13. Write down 3, carryover 1.\n\n5. Ten thousands place (position 5): 6 + 5 = 11, plus 1 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands place (position 6): 7 (from first number) + 3 (from second number)? Wait, hold on, maybe I messed up the positions here.\n\nWait, let me check the digits again for each position. Let's list each digit's place properly:\n\nFirst number: 275,769,705.\n\nBreaking down each digit with position (from right, starting at 1):\n\nPosition 1 (units): 5\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 0\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 7\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 9\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 6\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 7\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 5\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 7\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 2\n\nSecond number: 659,353,361\n\nPosition 1 (units): 1\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 6\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 3\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 3\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 5\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 9\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 3\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 5\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 6\n\nSo, when adding position 6 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst number's hundred thousands digit is 7, second number's is 9. So 7 + 9 = 16. Wait, but earlier I thought it was 7 + 3, which was wrong. Wait, hold on, this is where I must have messed up earlier.\n\nWait, in my previous calculation, I might have miscounted the positions. Let me redo this step-by-step:\n\nLet me make a table for each position:\n\nPosition 1 (units):\n\nFirst:5, Second:1 \u2192 5+1=6\n\nPosition 2 (tens):\n\nFirst:0, Second:6 \u21920+6=6\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\nFirst:7, Second:3 \u21927+3=10\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\nFirst:9, Second:3 \u21929+3=12\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\nFirst:6, Second:5 \u21926+5=11\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst:7, Second:9 \u21927+9=16\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst:5, Second:3 \u21925+3=8\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst:7, Second:5 \u21927+5=12\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst:2, Second:6 \u21922+6=8\n\nWait, so my mistake earlier was in position 6. I had thought the second number's hundred thousands place was 3, but actually it's 9. Let me recalculate with this correction.\n\nLet me go through each position again, keeping track of any carryovers:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Position1 (units):5 +1=6. Write 6. Carryover 0.\n\n2. Position2 (tens):0 +6=6. Write 6. Carryover 0.\n\n3. Position3 (hundreds):7 +3=10. Write 0. Carryover 1.\n\n4. Position4 (thousands):9 +3=12 +1 (carryover) =13. Write 3. Carryover 1.\n\n5. Position5 (ten thousands):6 +5=11 +1=12. Write 2. Carryover 1.\n\n6. Position6 (hundred thousands):7 +9=16 +1=17. Write 7. Carryover 1.\n\n7. Position7 (millions):5 +3=8 +1=9. Write 9. Carryover 0.\n\n8. Position8 (ten millions):7 +5=12. Write 2. Carryover 1.\n\n9. Position9 (hundred millions):2 +6=8 +1=9. Write 9. Carryover 0.\n\nNow, compiling all the written digits from right to left:\n\nPositions 1-9:\n\nPosition1:6\n\nPosition2:6\n\nPosition3:0\n\nPosition4:3\n\nPosition5:2\n\nPosition6:7\n\nPosition7:9\n\nPosition8:2\n\nPosition9:9\n\nWait, but when writing the number, the highest place is first. So the digits from position9 to position1:\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):9\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):2\n\nPosition7 (millions):9\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):7\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):2\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):0\n\nPosition2 (tens):6\n\nPosition1 (units):6\n\nSo putting them together: 9 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 6 (units).\n\nSo the number is 929,723,066?\n\nWait, let me check that again. Wait, Position8 (ten millions) was 2? Wait, let me see:\n\nWait, Position8 is ten millions place. Let's retrace:\n\nPosition8 is the ten millions place. The calculation for position8 was:\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 7, second number's is5. So 7+5=12 plus any carryover? Wait, let's go step by step.\n\nWait, Position8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 7 (from 275,769,705: the digits are 2 (hundred millions),7 (ten millions),5 (millions)...)\n\nSecond number's ten millions digit is 5 (from 659,353,361: 6 (hundred millions),5 (ten millions),9 (millions)...).\n\nSo 7+5=12. But before that, from the previous position (position7):\n\nWait, in step 7 (millions place):\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst number has 5 (millions digit), second number has 3 (millions digit). So 5+3=8, plus carryover from position6 (hundred thousands).\n\nWait, let me retrace step by step again with carryovers.\n\nStarting over with carryover tracking:\n\nPosition1: 5+1=6. No carryover.\n\nPosition2:0+6=6. No carryover.\n\nPosition3:7+3=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition4:9+3=12 +1(carryover)=13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nPosition5:6+5=11 +1(carryover)=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\nPosition6:7+9=16 +1(carryover)=17. Write7, carryover1.\n\nPosition7:5+3=8 +1(carryover)=9. Write9, carryover0.\n\nPosition8:7+5=12 +0(carryover)=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\nPosition9:2+6=8 +1(carryover)=9. Write9, carryover0.\n\nTherefore, the digits are:\n\nPosition9:9\n\nPosition8:2\n\nPosition7:9\n\nPosition6:7\n\nPosition5:2\n\nPosition4:3\n\nPosition3:0\n\nPosition2:6\n\nPosition1:6\n\nSo the number is 929,723,066.\n\nWait, let me write that out with