.\n\n5+1=6 \u2192 935,123,060 +6=935,123,066.\n\nAh! So here, the total is 935,123,066. Wait, but this conflicts with the previous result of 929,723,066. Which is correct?\n\nHmm, clearly there's a discrepancy here. Let me see where the mistake is.\n\nWait, when I did the column addition earlier, I got 929,723,066, but when breaking into components and adding, I get 935,123,066. There must be an error in one of the methods.\n\nLet me check the column addition again step by step carefully.\n\nWriting the numbers:\n\nFirst number:275,769,705\n\nSecond number:659,353,361\n\nAdding digit by digit from the right:\n\nLet me write them stacked:\n\n        2 7 5 7 6 9 7 0 5\n      + 6 5 9 3 5 3 3 6 1\n      ---------------------\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1. Units (position1):5 +1=6. Write 6. Carryover 0.\n\n2. Tens (position2):0 +6=6. Write6. Carryover0.\n\n3. Hundreds (position3):7 +3=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\n4. Thousands (position4):9 +3=12 +1=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\n5. Ten thousands (position5):6 +5=11 +1=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands (position6):7 (first number) +9 (second number) =16 +1=17. Write7, carryover1.\n\n7. Millions (position7):5 (first number) +3 (second number)=8 +1=9. Write9, carryover0.\n\n8. Ten millions (position8):7 (first) +5 (second)=12 +0=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\n9. Hundred millions (position9):2 (first) +6 (second)=8 +1=9. Write9, carryover0.\n\nSo the digits from position9 to position1:\n\n9 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions),9 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),2 (ten thousands),3 (thousands),0 (hundreds),6 (tens),6 (units).\n\nWait, but when written as a number, hundred millions is first, so:\n\n9 (hundred millions), then 2 (ten millions), 9 (millions), so that's 929 million, followed by 723 thousand and 066. So 929,723,066.\n\nBut in the expanded addition, I arrived at 935,123,066. There's a contradiction here. Let me check the expanded addition again.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the expanded terms:\n\nFirst number:275,769,705.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n200,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n70,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n5,000,000 (millions)\n\n700,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n60,000 (ten thousands)\n\n9,000 (thousands)\n\n700 (hundreds)\n\n0 (tens)\n\n5 (units).\n\nSecond number:659,353,361.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n600,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n50,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n9,000,000 (millions)\n\n300,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n50,000 (ten thousands)\n\n3,000 (thousands)\n\n300 (hundreds)\n\n60 (tens)\n\n1 (unit).\n\nAdding each category:\n\nHundred millions:200+600=800.\n\nTen millions:70+50=120 \u2192 total so far 800+120=920.\n\nMillions:5+9=14 \u2192 total 920+14=934.\n\nHundred thousands:700+300=1,000 \u2192 934,000,000 +1,000,000 =935,000,000.\n\nTen thousands:60+50=110,000 \u2192 total 935,110,000.\n\nThousands:9,000 +3,000=12,000 \u2192 935,110,000 +12,000 =935,122,000.\n\nHundreds:700+300=1,000 \u2192 935,122,000 +1,000 =935,123,000.\n\nTens:0+60=60 \u2192 +60 gives 935,123,060.\n\nUnits:5+1=6 \u2192 total 935,123,066.\n\nHmm, so according to this breakdown, the total is 935,123,066. But the column addition gave me 929,723,066. There's a clear inconsistency here. I must have made an error in the column addition.\n\nWait, let's look at the column addition again. Let's re-express the two numbers with their digits:\n\nFirst number (275,769,705):\n\nDigits from right to left (positions 1-9):\n\n5 (units), 0, 7, 9, 6, 7, 5, 7, 2.\n\nWait, maybe I have the digits reversed in the column addition. Let me write them as lists from right to left (units to hundred millions):\n\nFirst number's digits (right to left):\n\nPosition1:5, Position2:0, Position3:7, Position4:9, Position5:6, Position6:7, Position7:5, Position8:7, Position9:2.\n\nSecond number's digits (right to left):\n\nPosition1:1, Position2:6, Position3:3, Position4:3, Position5:5, Position6:9, Position7:3, Position8:5, Position9:6.\n\nSo when adding position by position:\n\nPosition1:5+1=6, no carryover.\n\