00 +1,200,000 =667,200,000. Correct.\n\nTen thousands:80k +90k=170k \u2192 add to 667,200k \u2192 667,370,000.\n\nThousands:7k +1k=8k \u2192 667,378,000.\n\nHundreds:700 +600=1,300 \u2192 667,379,300.\n\nTens:60 +80=140 \u2192 667,379,440.\n\nUnits:7 \u2192 total 667,379,447.\n\nHmm, so that gives a different result than the column addition. Which is concerning.\n\nBut when I added column by column earlier, I got 667,370,447. There's a discrepancy of 9,000. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, let me check the breakdown method again. Let's see:\n\nWait, when adding the ten thousands place: the first number has 8 (ten thousands) =80,000 and the second has 9 (ten thousands) =90,000. So 80k +90k =170k. So adding to previous total of 667,200,000, that becomes 667,370,000. That's correct.\n\nThen the thousands place: first number's thousands digit is 7 (thousands place?), wait, let me check the original numbers.\n\nWait, in the breakdown of the first number:\n\nFirst number breakdown:\n\nFirst number: 458,987,767.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n4 (hundred millions)\n\n5 (ten millions)\n\n8 (millions)\n\n9 (hundred thousands)\n\n8 (ten thousands)\n\n7 (thousands)\n\n7 (hundreds)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n7 (units).\n\nWait, so the thousands place is the 7 in the thousands place. So 7,000. Second number's thousands digit is 1 (as in 1,000). So 1,000. 7+1=8, so 8,000. So adding that to 667,370,000 gives 667,378,000. Correct.\n\nThen the hundreds place: first number has 7 (700), second has 6 (600). 1,300. Adding to 667,378,000 gives 667,379,300.\n\nTens place: first has 60, second has 80. 140. 667,379,440.\n\nUnits: +7 =667,379,447.\n\nOkay, so according to this breakdown, it's 667,379,447, which is different from the column addition. Hmm.\n\nWait, let me check the column addition again. Let me go through each column step by step again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\nFirst number: 4 5 8 9 8 7 7 6 7\n\nSecond number:2 0 8 3 9 1 6 8 0\n\nAdding starting from the right:\n\nPosition 1 (units):7+0=7.\n\nPosition2 (tens):6 +8=14. Write 4, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):7 +6=13 +1=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):8 (from first number) +1 (from second number)=9 +1 (carryover)=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7 (first number's digit here?) Wait, let's clarify the digits' places.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the positions in the column addition. Let me clarify the digits with their place values for each number.\n\nFirst number: 458,987,767.\n\nBreaking into places:\n\nDigit 1 (units):7\n\nDigit2 (tens):6\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):7\n\nDigit4 (thousands):8\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):7\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):9\n\nDigit7 (millions):8\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):5\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):4\n\nSecond number:208,391,680.\n\nDigits:\n\nDigit1 (units):0\n\nDigit2 (tens):8\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):6\n\nDigit4 (thousands):1\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):9\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):3\n\nDigit7 (millions):8\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):0\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):2\n\nNow, let's add digit by digit from 1 to9:\n\nPosition1 (units):\n\nFirst:7, second:0 \u21927.\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n6+8=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n7+6=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n8+1=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\nFirst digit5:7, second digit5:9 \u21927+9=16 +1=17 \u21927, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst digit6:9, second digit6:3 \u21929+3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst digit7:8, second digit7:8 \u21928+8=16 +1=17 \u21927, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst digit8:5, second digit8:0 \u21925+0=5 +1=6 \u21926, no carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst digit9:4, second digit9:2 \u21924+2=6.\n\nThus the result digits from position1 to9 are:\n\n7,4,4,0,7,3,7,6,6. So reversing the order (since we built from units to hundred millions):\n\n6 (hundred millions),6 (ten millions),7 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),0 (thousands),4 (hundreds),4 (tens),7 (units). So 667,370,447.\n\nWait, so this is conflicting with the breakdown method. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, in the breakdown method, the thousands place was 8,000 (from first number) +1,000 (second) =9,000, but according to the column addition, the thousands digit was 8 (from first number's thousands place?) Wait, no, let me check the breakdown again.\n\nWait in the first number, the thousands digit is the fourth digit from the right. Let me write the first number again:\n\nFirst number: 458,987,767.\n\nBreaking into digits:\n\nDigits from right (units to hundred millions):\n\nPosition1:7 (units)\n\nPosition2:6 (tens)\n\nPosition3:7 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:8 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:7 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:9 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:8 (millions)\n\nPosition8:5 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:4 (hundred millions)\n\nSo the thousands digit (position4) is 8. That's correct.\n\nSecond number:208,391,680.\n\nPosition4 (thousands digit) is 1 (since the number is ...680, so the thousands digit is 1 (since it's 391,680, so the thousands place is the 1 in 391,680? Wait let's parse it:\n\nSecond number: 208,391,680.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n208,391,680: the digits are:\n\n2 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions),8 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),1 (thousands),6 (hundreds),8 (tens),0 (units).\n\nWait, so thousands digit is 1, yes. So 1,000.\n\nThus, in the thousands place addition: first number has 8 (8,000), second has1 (1,000), so total 9,000, plus carryover1 from the hundreds place (which was 14, so carryover1). Wait, no: in the thousands place addition, the carryover comes from the hundreds place.\n\nWait in the column addition steps:\n\nPosition4 (thousands): 8 (first) +1 (second) =9 plus any carryover from previous (position3 which was hundreds place had a carryover1). Wait, in position4 (thousands):\n\nFirst digit (8) + second digit (1) + carryover1 (from position3's addition which was 14, so carryover1) \u2192 total 9+1=10. So write0, carryover1.\n\nThus the thousands digit is 0 with a carryover1 to the next (ten thousands place). So in the total sum, thousands digit is0, but in the breakdown method, adding the thousands components (8,000+1,000) gives 9,000, but in the column addition, it's 0 with a carried over1 to the next place. Hence the discrepancy.\n\nAh! Here's the problem. In the breakdown method, I added all the components without considering the carryover from the lower digits. For example, when adding the thousands place in the breakdown, the 8,000 +1,000 =9,000 is correct, but the carryover from the hundreds place (position3) had a carryover of1, which adds to the thousands place. So actually, the thousands place total is 9,000 +1,000 (carryover)? Wait no, the carryover is added to the thousands place sum.\n\nWait, in the column addition, the thousands place sum is 8+1=9 plus the carryover1 from hundreds place, totaling 10. So that 10 is 10,000, so it's written as 0 in thousands place and carryover1 to ten thousands. Thus, the thousands digit becomes 0, but the ten thousands place gets an extra 1.\n\nTherefore, the breakdown method is incorrect because it doesn't account for carryovers between the different place values. Hence, the correct total is the column addition result: 667,370,447.\n\nTherefore, the error in the breakdown method was treating each digit's place independently without considering the carryovers from the lower digits. The column addition is the accurate method here.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be 667,370,447.\n\nBut to make sure, perhaps I can perform a third method, like using approximate rounding to check.\n\nFirst number:458,987,767 is approximately 459 million.\n\nSecond number:208,391,680 is approximately 208 million.\n\nAdding them:459 +208 =667 million. The exact result should be around 667 million something. The column addition gave 667,370,447 which is 667.37 million, while the breakdown method had 667.379 million, which is only a difference in the lower digits, but the column addition is precise.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is 667,370,447.\n\nWait, let me also try adding using another method, perhaps splitting the numbers into smaller parts.\n\nFirst number: 458,987,767 = 458,000,000 + 987,767.\n\nSecond number:208,391,680 =208,000,000 +391,680.\n\nAdd the millions parts:458,000,000 +208,000,000 =666,000,000.\n\nAdd the remaining parts:987,767 +391,680.\n\nCompute 987,767 +391,680:\n\nLet's add 987,767 + 300,000 =1,287,767\n\nThen add 91,680: 1,287,767 +91,680 =1,379,447.\n\nTherefore total sum:666,000,000 +1,379,447 =667,379,447.\n\nWait, that's the same as the breakdown method. But according to column addition, it should be 667,370,447. Now this is confusing.\n\nWait, so here's the problem. When I split into 458,000,000 +987,767 and 208,000,000 +391,680, adding the smaller parts:\n\n987,767 +391,680.\n\nLet me compute that carefully:\n\n987,767\n\n+391,680\n\n--------\n\nAdding units:7+0=7\n\nTens:6+8=14, write4, carryover1\n\nHundreds:7+6=13 +1=14, write4, carryover1\n\nThousands:7+1=8 +1=9, carryover0\n\nTen thousands:8+9=17, write7, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands:9+3=12 +1=13, write3, carryover1\n\nMillions: (there are no millions in 391,680, so 0) + the 9 in 987,767's millions digit? Wait, 987,767 is 0.9 million? Wait, let's see:\n\nWait 987,767 is 9 hundred thousands, 8 ten thousands, 7 thousands, etc. So:\n\n987,767 is:\n\n9 (hundred thousands) =900,000\n\n8 (ten thousands)=80,000\n\n7 (thousands)=7,000\n\n7 (hundreds)=700\n\n6 (tens)=60\n\n7 (units)=7\n\n391,680 is:\n\n3 (hundred thousands)=300,000\n\n9 (ten thousands)=90,000\n\n1 (thousands)=1,000\n\n6 (hundreds)=600\n\n8 (tens)=80\n\n0 (units)=0\n\nAdding them digit by digit:\n\nUnits:7+0=7\n\nTens:6+8=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nHundreds:7+6=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nThousands:7+1=8 +1=9 \u21929, carryover0\n\nTen thousands:8+9=17 \u21927, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands:9+3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nMillions: the next place. Since both numbers have up to hundred thousands, the carryover1 from hundred thousands place becomes 1,000,000.\n\nSo the total of the smaller parts is:\n\n1,379,447.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nBreaking down the addition of 987,767 +391,680:\n\n987,767\n\n+391,680\n\n=?\n\nLet me add step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits:7+0=7\n\nTens:6+8=14, so 4 with carryover1\n\nHundreds:7+6=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nThousands:7 (from 987,767's thousands digit is 7?) Wait 987,767 has thousands digit as the fourth digit from the right: 7 (since it's 987,767). Yes, so 7 +1 (from 391,680's thousands digit is 1) =8, plus carryover1 =9. So thousands digit 9, no carryover?\n\nWait, let's do it step by step:\n\nThousands place:7 (from first number) +1 (from second number) + carryover1 from hundreds place (which was 14 \u2192 carried over 1). So 7+1+1=9. So thousands digit is 9, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands place:8 (from first number's ten thousands digit) +9 (second's ten thousands digit) =17. Write7, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands place:9 (first) +3 (second) =12 + carryover1=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\nMillions place: Both numbers don't have millions, but there's a carryover1 from hundred thousands place. So the result there is 1,000,000.\n\nHence total is 1,000,000 + 300,000 (from hundred thousands?) Wait:\n\nWait the millions place is 1 (carryover) so that's 1,000,000. Then the hundred thousands is 3 (so 300,000), ten thousands 7 (70,000), thousands 9 (9,000), hundreds 4 (400), tens4 (40), units7 (7). Thus total is 1,379,447. So the total of the smaller parts is indeed 1,379,447.\n\nAdding to 666,000,000 gives 667,379,447.\n\nBut this contradicts the column addition. Where is the error?\n\nWait in the column addition earlier, the total was 667,370,447, but here it's 667,379,447. The difference is in the ten thousands and hundreds of thousands place.\n\nWait, let me check the column addition again step by step:\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\nFirst number:4 5 8 9 8 7 7 6 7\n\nSecond number:2 0 8 3 9 1 6 8 0\n\nAdding column by column from right (units to hundred millions):\n\nPosition1:7+0=7\n\nPosition2:6+8=14 \u21924, c1\n\nPosition3:7+6=13 +1=14 \u21924, c1\n\nPosition4:8+1=9 +1=10 \u21920, c1\n\nPosition5:7 (first's ten thousands digit) +9 (second's ten thousands digit)=16 +1(carry)=17 \u21927, c1\n\nPosition6:9 (first's hundred thousands digit) +3 (second's)=12 +1=13 \u21923, c1\n\nPosition7:8 (first's millions digit) +8 (second's)=16 +1=17 \u21927, c1\n\nPosition8:5 (first's ten millions digit) +0 (second's)=5 +1=6, c0\n\nPosition9:4 +2=6.\n\nThus the digits from right to left are:\n\n7,4,4,0,7,3,7,6,6 \u2192 reversing gives 667,370,447.\n\nWait, but in the breakdown method, when adding the two numbers as 458,000,000+987,767 and 208,000,000+391,680, then adding 666,000,000 +1,379,447 gives 667,379,447.\n\nSo which one is correct? There must be an error in one of these.\n\nWait let me check the breakdown method again:\n\nFirst number:458,987,767.\n\nBreaking into 458,000,000 and 987,767. Correct.\n\nSecond number:208,391,680 \u2192208,000,000 +391,680. Correct.\n\nAdding the two parts:\n\n458,000,000 +208,000,000 =666,000,000.\n\nThen 987,767 +391,680.\n\nCalculating 987,767 +391,680:\n\nLet me add them digit by digit:\n\n987,767\n\n+391,680\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:7+0=7\n\nTens:6+8=14, so 4 carryover1.\n\nHundreds:7+6=13 +1=14 \u21924 carryover1.\n\nThousands:7 (from first number) +1 (from second number)=8 +1=9 \u21929, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands:8 (from first) +9 (from second)=17 \u21927 carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:9 (first) +3 (second)=12 +1=13 \u21923 carryover1.\n\nMillions place: The first number's millions digit is 9 (since 987,767 is part of the first number's millions place? Wait, 987,767 is the remaining part after 458,000,000. So 987,767 is less than a million, right? Wait 987,767 is 0.987 million. Wait no, 987,767 is actually 0.9877