en millions), 3 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 9 (ones).\n\nSo:.\n\nDigit 1: 5 (hundred millions).\n\nDigit 2: 5 (ten millions).\n\nDigit 3: 0 (millions).\n\nDigit 4: 9 (hundred thousands).\n\nDigit 5: 3 (ten thousands).\n\nDigit 6: 5 (thousands).\n\nDigit 7: 6 (hundreds).\n\nDigit 8: 3 (tens).\n\nDigit 9: 9 (ones).\n\nSimilarly, the second number is 230,781,763. Let's break that down:.\n\nDigit 1: 2 (hundred millions).\n\nDigit 2: 3 (ten millions).\n\nDigit 3: 0 (millions).\n\nDigit 4: 7 (hundred thousands).\n\nDigit 5: 8 (ten thousands).\n\nDigit 6: 1 (thousands).\n\nDigit 7: 7 (hundreds).\n\nDigit 8: 6 (tens).\n\nDigit 9: 3 (ones).\n\nOkay, so when adding, let me go step by step again, maybe I made a mistake earlier.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):.\n\nDigit 9: 9 + 3 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nDigit 8 (tens): 3 + 6 = 9, plus carryover 1 makes 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nDigit 7 (hundreds): 6 + 7 = 13, plus carryover 1 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nDigit 6 (thousands): 5 + 1 = 6, plus 1 = 7. Write 7, no carryover.\n\nDigit 5 (ten thousands): 3 + 8 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nDigit 4 (hundred thousands): 9 + 7 = 16, plus 1 = 17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nDigit 3 (millions): 0 + 0 = 0, plus 1 = 1. Write 1, no carryover.\n\nDigit 2 (ten millions): 5 + 3 = 8. Write 8.\n\nDigit 1 (hundred millions): 5 + 2 = 7. Write 7.\n\nPutting it all together, the sum should be:.\n\n7 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 2 (ones). Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
