ndred millions: 5.\n- Ten millions: 0.\n- Millions: 5.\n- Hundred thousands: 9.\n- Ten thousands: 1.\n- Thousands: 6.\n- Hundreds: 9.\n- Tens: 6.\n- Ones: 5.\n\nNow adding each place:.\n\nStarting from the ones:.\n\nOnes: 4 + 5 = 9.\n\nTens: 9 + 6 = 15 → 5 with carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 5 + 9 = 14 + 1 (carry) = 15 → 5, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 2 + 6 = 8 + 1 = 9 → 9, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands: 6 + 1 = 7 → 7.\n\nHundred thousands: 5 + 9 = 14 → 4, carryover 1.\n\nMillions: 9 (first) + 5 (second) = 14 + 1 (carry) = 15 → 5, carryover 1.\n\nTen millions: 3 + 0 = 3 + 1 (carry) = 4 → 4.\n\nHundred millions: 9 + 5 = 14 → 4, carryover 1.\n\nWait, but the hundred millions place addition: 9 + 5 = 14, so that would give us a digit of 4 in the hundred millions place and carryover 1 to the next (billions) place. Since both numbers are less than a billion, the total should be in the billions now.\n\nSo adding that carryover 1 to the billions place, which was 0 before, so the total becomes 1 in the billions place.\n\nPutting all together:.\n\nBillions place: 1.\n\nHundred millions: 4.\n\nTen millions: 4.\n\nMillions: 5.\n\nHundred thousands: 4.\n\nTen thousands: 7.\n\nThousands: 9.\n\nHundreds: 5.\n\nTens: 5.\n\nOnes: 9. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
