 9, carryover1.\n\nTen millions place: 8 +4=12 +1=13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions place: 1 +2=3 +1=4.\n\nSo putting all together the sum is 4 3 9 2 5 2 1 7 2?.\n\nWait, hold on, let me reconstruct the digits properly. Let me list each digit position:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits of the sum would be:.\n\nUnits: 2.\n\nTens:7.\n\nHundreds:1.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nTen thousands:5.\n\nHundred thousands:2.\n\nMillions:9.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nHundred millions:4.\n\nWait, so writing from right to left, but when we write the number it's left to right. So the final number should be 439,252,172?.\n\nWait, let me check again step by step, maybe I made a mistake in the places.\n\nWait the first number is 189,893,598. Let me count the digits:.\n\n1 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 8 (units).\n\nSecond number: 249,385,574.\n\nDigits: 2 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 4 (units).\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nUnits: 8 +4=12, so 2 carryover1.\n\nTens:9+7=16 +1=17, 7 carryover1.\n\nHundreds:5+5=10 +1=11, 1 carryover1.\n\nThousands:3+5=8? Wait, wait, hold on, the thousands place.\n\nWait the first number's thousands digit: Let me see:.\n\nFirst number breakdown:.\n\n1 (100,000,000).\n\n8 (80,000,000).\n\n9 (9,000,000).\n\n8 (800,000).\n\n9 (90,000).\n\n3 (3,000).\n\n5 (500).\n\n9 (90).\n\n8 (8).\n\nSecond number:.\n\n2 (200,000,000).\n\n4 (40,000,000).\n\n9 (9,000,000).\n\n3 (300,000).\n\n8 (80,000).\n\n5 (5,000).\n\n5 (500).\n\n7 (70).\n\n4 (4).\n\nSo the thousands place for first number is 3 (from 3,000), second number's thousands place is 5 (from 5,000). So 3 +5 =8 plus carryover 1 makes 9? Wait, but earlier I thought it was 3+8? Wait, maybe I messed up here.\n\nWait, let's go step by step again carefully:.\n\nLet me write down each digit position with labels to avoid confusion.\n\nStarting from the right (units):.\n\nPosition 1 (units): 8 + 4 =12. 2, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 9 +7 =16 +1=17. 7, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):5 +5=10 +1=11. 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3 (from first number's thousands) +5 (from second number's thousands)=8 +1 (carryover)=9. Wait, so 9? No carryover?.\n\nWait, 3+5=8, plus 1 is 9. So that's 9, no carryover?.\n\nWait, so in thousands place it's 9. Then, carryover is 0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands): 9 (from first number's ten thousands) +8 (from second number's ten thousands)=17. Wait, first number's ten thousands digit is 9, second's is 8. 9+8=17. Then plus any carryover? There was no carryover from thousands place. So 17. So write 7, carryover1.\n\nWait, but in my first calculation I had 15 here. Hmm, so I made a mistake earlier here.\n\nWait let me clarify:.\n\nFirst number: ten thousands place is 9 (since breakdown is ..90,000).\n\nSecond number: ten thousands place is 8 (from ..80,000). So 9+8=17. So that's 7 with carryover1. So previous step was wrong, I must have added the hundred thousands instead.\n\nWait, let's see:.\n\nPosition4 (thousands): 3+5=8 +1=9, no carryover.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):9 (first) +8 (second)=17. So 7, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands): first number's hundred thousands is 8 (from 800,000), second's is 3 (from 300,000). 8 +3=11 + carryover1=12. So 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions): first number's millions digit is 9, second's is9 (since first number has 9,000,000 and second has 9,000,000? Wait second number's millions digit is 9 (from 9,000,000). Wait first number's millions digit is 9, second's is 9. So 9+9=18 + carryover1=19. So 9, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions): first number's ten millions digit is 8 (from 80,000,000), second's is4 (from 40,000,000). 8+4=12 + carryover1=13. So 3, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions): first number's is1, second's is2. 1+2=3 + carryover1=4.\n\nSo putting all digits together from right to left:.\n\nPosition1:2.\n\nPosition2:7.\n\nPosition3:1.\n\nPosition4:9.\n\nPosition5:7.\n\nPosition6:2.\n\nPosition7:9.\n\nPosition8:3.\n\nPosition9:4.\n\nSo the number is 4 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions),9 (millions),2 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),9 (thousands),1 (hundreds),7 (tens),2 (units). Wait, let me arrange them properly.\n\nWait, the digits from highest to lowest place:.\n\nHundred millions:4.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nMillions:9.\n\nHundred thousands:2.\n\nTen thousands:7.\n\nThousands:9.\n\nHundreds:1.\n\nTens:7.\n\nUnits:2. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
