om right to left with their positions:.\n\nFirst number: 174,898,099.\n\nDigits (right to left, starting at position 0):.\n\nPosition 0 (ones): 9.\n\nPosition 1 (tens): 9.\n\nPosition 2 (hundreds): 0.\n\nPosition 3 (thousands): 8.\n\nPosition 4 (ten thousands): 9.\n\nPosition 5 (hundred thousands): 8.\n\nPosition 6 (millions): 4.\n\nPosition 7 (ten millions): 7.\n\nPosition 8 (hundred millions): 1.\n\nSecond number: 481,368,739.\n\nDigits:.\n\nPosition 0 (ones): 9.\n\nPosition 1 (tens): 3.\n\nPosition 2 (hundreds): 7.\n\nPosition 3 (thousands): 8.\n\nPosition 4 (ten thousands): 6.\n\nPosition 5 (hundred thousands): 3.\n\nPosition 6 (millions): 1.\n\nPosition 7 (ten millions): 8.\n\nPosition 8 (hundred millions): 4.\n\nPosition 9 (billions): 0 (since the first number doesn't have a billion place, but the second number's highest is 4 in hundred millions, so both are under a billion).\n\nWait, actually, let me confirm the digits properly:.\n\nWait, 481,368,739 is:.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\n4 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 9 (ones). Wait, that can't be. Wait, commas are placed every three digits. Let me parse it correctly.\n\nThe number 481,368,739:.\n\n- The first comma separates millions, so 481,368,739 is 481 (million) 368 (thousand) 739. So:.\n\nDigits from left to right:.\n\n4 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 9 (ones).\n\nTherefore, when aligned with the first number, which is 174,898,099:.\n\nFirst number:.\n\n1 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 9 (ones).\n\nSecond number:.\n\n4 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 9 (ones).\n\nSo when adding, let's line them up:.\n\nHundred millions: 1 + 4 = 5.\n\nTen millions: 7 + 8 = 15.\n\nMillions: 4 + 1 = 5.\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 3 = 11.\n\nTen thousands: 9 + 6 = 15.\n\nThousands: 8 + 8 = 16.\n\nHundreds: 0 +7 =7.\n\nTens:9 +3=12.\n\nOnes:9+9=18.\n\nWait, but when adding, we have to consider the carries from each digit. Maybe I should do it step by step starting from the right.\n\nStarting from the ones place:.\n\nOnes: 9 +9 = 18. Write down 8, carryover 1.\n\nTens:9 +3 =12, plus the carried 1 is 13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:0 +7=7 plus 1 is 8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nThousands:8 +8=16. Write 6, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:9 +6=15 plus 1=16. Write 6, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:8 +3=11 plus1=12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nMillions:4 +1=5 plus1=6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nTen millions:7 +8=15. Write 5, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:1 +4=5 plus1=6. Write 6.\n\nSo putting all together:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:.\n\nOnes:8.\n\nTens:3.\n\nHundreds:8.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nTen thousands:6.\n\nHundred thousands:2.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nTen millions:5.\n\nHundred millions:6.\n\nSo the total is 652,266,838?.\n\nWait, let me check the digits again step by step:.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the places. Let me write the addition vertically:.\n\nLet me write both numbers with commas for clarity:.\n\nFirst number: 174,898,099.\n\nSecond number:481,368,739.\n\nAdding:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes place: 9 +9 =18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nTens place:9 +3 =12 +1=13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nHundreds place:0 +7=7 +1=8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nThousands place:8 +8=16. Write 6, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands place:9 +6=15 +1=16. Write 6, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands place:8 +3=11 +1=12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nMillions place:4 +1=5 +1=6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nTen millions place:7 +8=15. Write 5, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions place:1 +4=5 +1(carryover)=6. So the digits are 6 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nWait, putting that together:.\n\nHundred millions:6.\n\nTen millions:5.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nHundred thousands:2.\n\nTen thousands:6.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nHundreds:8.\n\nTens:3.\n\nOnes:8.\n\nSo combining these places:.\n\n6 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 8 (ones). Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
