digit):.\n\n1st digit (units): 9 +4 =13 → write 3, carryover 1.\n\n2nd digit (tens): 2 +4 =6 +1 =7 →7.\n\n3rd digit (hundreds):1 +8 =9 →9.\n\n4th digit (thousands):8 +8 =16 →6, carryover1.\n\n5th digit (ten thousands):1 +0 =1 +1 (carryover)=2 →2.\n\n6th digit (hundred thousands):4 +1 =5 →5.\n\n7th digit (millions):0 +5 =5 →5.\n\n8th digit (ten millions):4 +4 =8 →8.\n\n9th digit (hundred millions):9 +2 =11 →1, carryover1.\n\nNow, since after the 9th digit, we still have a carryover of 1, so we add a new digit at the beginning (the billions place). So the total becomes 1 followed by the digits we have so far.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nThe digits from right to left after adding are:.\n\n3 (units),7,9,6,2,5,5,8,1 (from the 9th digit), and then the carryover 1.\n\nWait, let me list all the digits we got from each position:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost (units):.\n\n1st digit: 3.\n\n2nd:7.\n\n3rd:9.\n\n4th:6.\n\n5th:2.\n\n6th:5.\n\n7th:5.\n\n8th:8.\n\n9th:1 (with a carryover 1).\n\nThen the carryover adds a 10th digit at the front.\n\nSo the result is:.\n\n1 (from carryover) followed by the 9 digits:.\n\n1 (from 9th digit) 8 (8th) 5 (7th) 5 (6th) 2 (5th) 6 (4th) 9 (3rd) 7 (2nd) 3 (1st).\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to write them in order.\n\nWait, let's see:.\n\nAfter adding each digit, the digits from right to left (units to hundred millions) are:.\n\n3 (units).\n\n7 (tens).\n\n9 (hundreds).\n\n6 (thousands).\n\n2 (ten thousands).\n\n5 (hundred thousands).\n\n5 (millions).\n\n8 (ten millions).\n\n1 (hundred millions).\n\nplus the carryover 1 to the billions place.\n\nTherefore, the total number is:.\n\n1 (billions) 1 (hundred millions) 8 (ten millions) 5 (millions) 5 (hundred thousands) 2 (ten thousands) 6 (thousands) 9 (hundreds) 7 (tens) 3 (units). Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
