old on. Let me think again. The hundred millions place:.\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is 5 (since the number is 5 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 4 (millions)..). Second number's hundred millions digit is also 5. So 5 +5 =10. So we write 0 in the hundred millions place and carryover 1 to the billions place. But since the billions place in each number is also 5 (the first digit), so actually, wait, the first number is 5 6 4 8 4 6 0 1 8, which is 9 digits, so the first digit is the hundred millions place? Wait, hold on, maybe I messed up the digit places.\n\nWait, let's count the digits properly.\n\nThe first number is 564,846,018.\n\nBreaking it down with commas: 564,846,018.\n\nSo in digits: 5 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nSimilarly, the second number is 526,533,181.\n\nBreaking down: 5 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nSo when adding, the digits are:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones):.\n\nFirst number's digits:.\n\nPosition: Ones:8, Tens:1, Hundreds:0, Thousands:6, Ten thousands:4, Hundred thousands:8, Millions:4, Ten millions:6, Hundred millions:5.\n\nSecond number's digits:.\n\nOnes:1, Tens:8, Hundreds:1, Thousands:3, Ten thousands:3, Hundred thousands:5, Millions:6, Ten millions:2, Hundred millions:5.\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nLet me write them out digit by digit:.\n\nHundred millions: 5 +5 =10.\n\nTen millions:6 +2 =8, plus any carryover?.\n\nWait, let's do this step by step again.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (position 1: ones):.\n\nDigit 1 (Ones): 8 +1 =9.\n\nDigit 2 (Tens):1 +8 =9.\n\nDigit 3 (Hundreds):0 +1 =1.\n\nDigit 4 (Thousands):6 +3 =9.\n\nDigit 5 (Ten thousands):4 +3 =7.\n\nDigit 6 (Hundred thousands):8 +5 =13. So 3, carryover 1.\n\nDigit 7 (Millions):4 +6 =10, plus carryover 1 is 11. So 1, carryover 1.