 millions).\n\nSecond number: 198,444,005  .\nDigits (from right to left):  .\n5 (units), 0 (tens), 0 (hundreds), 4 (thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 4 (hundred thousands), 8 (millions), 9 (ten millions), 1 (hundred millions).\n\nHmm, maybe it's better to write them aligned vertically:.\n\n          1 1 5 6 2 0 9 6 4  .\n        + 1 9 8 4 4 4 0 0 5  .\n        --------------------.\n\nWait, actually, when written digit by digit, the alignment should be:.\n\nLet me write both numbers with their digits properly aligned:.\n\nFirst number: 1 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 4 (units).\n\nSecond number: 1 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 5 (units).\n\nSo aligning each digit:.\n\nHundred millions: 1 + 1 = 2  .\nTen millions: 1 + 9 = 10  .\nMillions: 5 + 8 = 13  .\nHundred thousands: 6 + 4 = 10  .\nTen thousands: 2 + 4 = 6  .\nThousands: 0 + 4 = 4  .\nHundreds: 9 + 0 = 9  .\nTens: 6 + 0 = 6  .\nUnits: 4 + 5 = 9  .\n\nWait, but when adding, we have to consider the carries over from each column. Let me start from the rightmost digit (units) and move left, keeping track of the carry-over.\n\nStarting over step by step:.\n\nUnits place:  .\n4 + 5 = 9. Write down 9. Carryover 0.\n\nTens place:  .\n6 + 0 = 6. Write down 6. Carryover 0.\n\nHundreds place:  .\n9 + 0 = 9. Write down 9. Carryover 0.\n\nThousands place:  .\n0 + 4 = 4. Write down 4. Carryover 0.\n\nTen thousands place:  .\n2 + 4 = 6. Write down 6. Carryover 0.\n\nHundred thousands place:  .\n6 + 4 = 10. Write down 0 and carryover 1.\n\nMillions place:  .\n5 + 8 = 13, plus the carryover 1 makes 14. Write down 4 and carryover 1.\n\nTen millions place:  .\n1 + 9 = 10, plus the carryover 1 makes 11. Write down 1 and carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions place:  .\n1 + 1 = 2, plus the carryover 1 makes 3. Write down 3.\n\nPutting it all together from right to left, the digits are:.\n\nUnits: 9  .\nTens: 6  .\nHundreds: 9  .\nThousands: 4  .\nTen thousands: 6  .\nHundred thousands: 0  .\nMillions: 4  .\nTen millions: 1  .\nHundred millions: 3  .\n\nWait, that seems off. Let me check again. Wait, when writing the digits, the order from right to left is units to hundred millions. But when we write the number, it should be from highest to lowest place.\n\nWait, let me write the digits in order:.\n\nStarting from the highest place (hundred millions):.\n\n3 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 9 (units).\n\nWait, that doesn't seem right. Let me reconstruct the number step by step:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):.\n\nFirst digit (units): 9  .\nSecond digit (tens): 6  .\nThird digit (hundreds):9  .\nFourth digit (thousands):4  .\nFifth digit (ten thousands):6  .\nSixth digit (hundred thousands):0 (but with a carryover?) Wait, let's retrace the steps properly.\n\nWait, let me go through each digit again carefully with carries.\n\nStarting from the right (units):.\n\n1. Units place:  .\nFirst number: 4  .\nSecond number:5  .\nTotal:4 +5 =9. Write 9. Carryover 0.\n\n2. Tens place:  .\n6 +0=6. Write 6. Carryover 0.\n\n3. Hundreds place:  .\n9 +0=9. Write 9. Carryover 0.\n\n4. Thousands place:  .\n0 (from first number) +4 (second)=4. Write 4. Carryover 0.\n\n5. Ten thousands place:  .\n2 (first) +4 (second)=6. Write 6. Carryover 0.\n\n6. Hundred thousands place:  .\n6 (first) +4 (second)=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\n7. Millions place:  .\n5 (first) +8 (second)=13, plus carryover 1=14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\n8. Ten millions place:  .\n1 (first) +9 (second)=10, plus carryover 1=11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n9. Hundred millions place:  .\n1 (first) +1 (second)=2, plus carryover 1=3. Write 3, no more digits.\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to hundred millions) are: 9,6,9,4,6,0,4,1,3.\n\nBut