d thousands): 0.\nPosition 7 (millions): 0.\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 4.\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 2.\n\nSimilarly, the second number is 109,105,608. Let's break that down:.\n\n109,105,608:.\n- 1 (hundred millions).\n- 0 (ten millions).\n- 9 (millions).\n- 1 (hundred thousands).\n- 0 (ten thousands).\n- 5 (thousands).\n- 6 (hundreds).\n- 0 (tens).\n- 8 (units).\n\nBreaking down each digit from right to left:.\n\nPosition 1 (units): 8.\nPosition 2 (tens): 0.\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 6.\nPosition 4 (thousands): 5.\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 0.\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 1.\nPosition 7 (millions): 9.\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 0.\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 1.\n\nNow, to add them, I need to align each digit correctly. Let me write both numbers vertically:.\n\n        2 4 0 0 0 6 4 0 8.\n      + 1 0 9 1 0 5 6 0 8.\n      --------------------.\n\nWait, actually, maybe it's better to write each digit in columns. Let me set it up properly:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units place):.\n\nUnits column: 8 + 8 = 16. Write down 6, carry over 1.\n\nTens column: 0 + 0 = 0, plus the carried 1 makes 1. So, 1.\n\nHundreds column: 4 + 6 = 10. Write down 0, carry over 1.\n\nThousands column: 6 + 5 = 11, plus the carried 1 makes 12. Write down 2, carry over 1.\n\nTen thousands column: 0 + 0 = 0, plus the carried 1 makes 1.\n\nHundred thousands column: 0 + 1 = 1.\n\nMillions column: 0 + 9 = 9.\n\nTen millions column: 4 + 0 = 4.\n\nHundred millions column: 2 + 1 = 3.\n\nWait, let me go through this step by step again to make sure I didn't skip any columns or miscalculate.\n\nLet me list each digit position by position:.\n\nStarting from the right (units):.\n\n1. Units place: 8 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) = 16. So, write 6, carryover 1.\n\n2. Tens place: 0 + 0 = 0, plus the carried over 1 = 1. So, write 1, no carryover.\n\n3. Hundreds place: 4 + 6 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\n4. Thousands place: 6 + 5 = 11, plus the carried over 1 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\n5. Ten thousands place: 0 + 0 = 0, plus the carried over 1 = 1. Write 1, no carryover.\n\n6. Hundred thousands place: 0 + 1 = 1. Write 1, no carryover.\n\n7. Millions place: 0 + 9 = 9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\n8. Ten millions place: 4 + 0 = 4. Write 4, no carryover.\n\n9. Hundred millions place: 2 + 1 = 3. Write 3, no carryover.\n\nPutting all the digits together from right to left, we have:.\n\nUnits:6, Tens:1, Hundreds:0, Thousands:2, Ten thousands:1, Hundred thousands:1, Millions:9, Ten millions:4, Hundred millions:3.\n\nSo the number is 3 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 6 (units).\n\nWait, let me structure this properly. Starting from the highest place value:.\n\nHundred millions: 3.\n\nTen millions: 4.\n\nMillions: 9.\n\nHundred thousands:1.\n\nTen thousands:1.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nHundreds:0.\n\nTens:1.\n\nUnits:6.\n\nTherefore, the number is 349,102,116?.\n\nWait, let me verify that:.\n\nBreaking it down:.\n\n- 3 hundred million is 300,000,000.\n\n- 4 ten millions is 40,000,000 → total so far 340,000,000.\n\n- 9 millions is 9,000,000 → total 349,000,000.\n\n- 1 hundred thousand is 100,000 → 349,100,000.\n\n- 1 ten thousand is 10,000 → 349,110,000.\n\n- 2 thousand is 2,000 → 349,112,000.\n\n- 0 hundreds → stays.\n\n- 1 ten → 10 → 349,112,010. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
