nHundred millions:2.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nSo writing in order from highest to lowest place:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:2.\n\nTen millions:5.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nHundred thousands:8.\n\nTen thousands:2.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nHundreds:1.\n\nTens:3.\n\nOnes:9.\n\nSo putting commas for clarity:.\n\n1,256,822,319.\n\nWait, let me confirm this with another method. Maybe adding the numbers in parts.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can add the numbers digit by digit again to verify.\n\nFirst number: 954,916,472.\n\nSecond number: 301,905,667.\n\nAdding them:.\n\nLet me write them vertically:.\n\n      954,916,472.\n\n    + 301,905,667.\n\n    ----------------.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 2 +7 =9.\n\nTens:7+6=13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:4 +6=10 +1=11. Write 1, carryover1.\n\nThousands:6 +5=11 +1=12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:1 +0=1 +1=2. Write2.\n\nHundred thousands:9 +9=18. Write8, carryover1.\n\nMillions:4 +1=5 +1=6.\n\nTen millions:5 +0=5.\n\nHundred millions:9 +3=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\nBillions:0 (since both are 9-digit numbers) + the carryover1. So 1.\n\nThus the total is 1,256,822,319. That seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can approximate to check if this is reasonable.\n\nThe first number is approximately 955 million, the second is approximately 302 million. Adding those gives roughly 1,257 million, which is about 1.257 billion. The exact answer is 1,256,822,319, which is 1.2568.. billion, so that seems close. The approximation matches, so that gives me more confidence.\n\nAlternatively, let me try adding using another method, like breaking the numbers into parts.\n\nFirst number: 954,916,472 = 900,000,000 + 54,000,000 + 916,000 + 472.\n\nSecond number: 301,905,667 = 300,000,000 + 1,905,667.\n\nWait, maybe breaking them into millions:.\n\nFirst: 954 million + 916,472.\n\nSecond: 301 million + 905,667.\n\nAdding the millions: 954 + 301 = 1,255 million.\n\nAdding the remaining parts: 916,472 + 905,667.\n\nCalculating 916,472 + 9