en millions (8):5.\n\nHundred millions (9):8.\n\nSecond number:.\n\nUnits (1):0.\n\nTens (2):6.\n\nHundreds (3):7.\n\nThousands (4):6.\n\nTen thousands (5):4.\n\nHundred thousands (6):7.\n\nMillions (7):5.\n\nTen millions (8):4.\n\nHundred millions (9):4.\n\nSo adding each position:.\n\nPosition 1:1+0=1.\n\nPosition2:4+6=10 → write 0, carryover1.\n\nPosition3:4+7=11 + carryover1=12 → write 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition4:8+6=14 +1=15 → write5, carryover1.\n\nPosition5:3+4=7 +1=8 → write8, no carryover.\n\nPosition6:4+7=11 → write1, carryover1.\n\nPosition7:4+5=9 +1=10 → write0, carryover1.\n\nPosition8:5+4=9 +1=10 → write0, carryover1.\n\nPosition9:8+4=12 +1=13 → write3, carryover1. But since there are no more digits, the carryover becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nWait, so after adding all positions, the carryover from position9 is 1, so the total would be 1 followed by the digits we wrote: 1 (carryover), 3, 0, 0, 8, 5, 2, 0, 1.\n\nWait, let me reconstruct the total step by step with the carries.\n\nLet me go through each step again carefully:.\n\nStarting with Position 1 (units):.\n\n1 +0 =1 → digit1 is 1, carryover 0.\n\nPosition2 (tens):.\n\n4 +6 =10 → digit2 is 0, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\n4 +7 =11 +1 (carryover) =12 → digit3 is 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\n8 +6 =14 +1 (carryover) =15 → digit4 is5, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\n3 +4 =7 +1 (carryover) =8 → digit5 is8, carryover0.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n4 +7 =11 +0 =11 → digit6 is1, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\n4 +5 =9 +1 (carryover) =10 → digit7 is0, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\n5 +4 =9 +1 (carryover) =10 → digit8 is0, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\n8 +4 =12 +1 (carryover) =13 → digit9 is3, carryover1.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, the carryover1 becomes a new digit at the next position (billions place). So the total is 1 3 0 0 8 5 2 0 1 1? Wait, no, let me see:.\n\nWait, the digits we have fr