Digit6:6 (hundred thousands).\n\nDigit7:3 (millions).\n\nDigit8:7 (ten millions).\n\nDigit9:1 (hundred millions).\n\nYes, so position4 (thousands) is 9. So adding position4: 9 +6 (since second number's thousands digit is 6) + the carryover 1 from position3 gives 9+6=15 +1=16. Wait, so that's 16. So we write 6 in the thousands place and carryover 1 to the next position.\n\nWait, so my previous calculation was wrong here. Earlier, I thought the first number had 0 in thousands place, but actually it's 9. That was the mistake! So that changes everything. Let me recalculate with this correction.\n\nStarting over with the column addition, correcting my earlier mistake:\n\nPosition1:7+2=9.\n\nPosition2:8+1=9.\n\nPosition3:4+9=13 \u2192 3, carryover1.\n\nPosition4:9 (first) +6 (second) +1 (carryover)=16 \u2192 write 6, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):0 (first) +0 (second) +1 (carryover)=1. So 1, no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):6 (first) +4 (second)=10 \u2192 write 0, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):3 (first) +0 (second) +1 (carryover)=4 \u2192 4, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):7 (first) +8 (second)=15 \u2192 write5, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):1 (first) +1 (second) +1 (carryover)=3 \u21923.\n\nNow compiling all digits from right to left:\n\nPosition1:9 (units).\n\nPosition2:9 (tens).\n\nPosition3:3 (hundreds).\n\nPosition4:6 (thousands).\n\nPosition5:1 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition6:0 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition7:4 (millions).\n\nPosition8:5 (ten millions).\n\nPosition9:3 (hundred millions).\n\nSo the number is 3 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 9 (units).\n\nPutting that together: 354,016,399. Which matches the second method's result. So my initial mistake was miscalculating the thousands place in the first column addition. I must have confused the digits positions. That's why cross-verifying is important.\n\nTherefo