ng down similarly:.\n\n7 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (ones). So:.\n\nDigit positions:.\n\n1 (ones): 0.\n\n2 (tens): 6.\n\n3 (hundreds): 1.\n\n4 (thousands): 6.\n\n5 (ten thousands): 4.\n\n6 (hundred thousands): 7.\n\n7 (millions): 4.\n\n8 (ten millions): 3.\n\n9 (hundred millions): 7.\n\nAlso 9 digits.\n\nSo when adding, each digit position can be added:.\n\nStarting from position 1 (ones):.\n\n4 + 0 = 4.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\n6 + 6 = 12 → write 2, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):.\n\n5 + 1 = 6, plus carryover 1 → 7.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):.\n\n1 + 6 = 7.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):.\n\n3 + 4 = 7.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n6 + 7 = 13 → write 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):.\n\n5 + 4 = 9, plus carryover 1 → 10 → write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):.\n\n4 + 3 = 7, plus carryover 1 → 8.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):.\n\n3 + 7 = 10 → write 0, carryover 1.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, the carryover 1 goes to the next position (billions place), so we have a 1 there.\n\nPutting it all together from the billions down:.\n\nBillions: 1.\n\nHundred millions: 0.\n\nTen millions: 8.\n\nMillions: 0.\n\nHundred thousands: 3.\n\nTen thousands:7.\n\nThousands:7.\n\nHundreds:7.\n\nTens:2.\n\nOnes:4.\n\nWait, let me structure it properly:.\n\nStarting from the highest digit (billions):.\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nWait, but when writing the number, the millions place after ten millions would be millions, right?.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to reconstruct the number step by step.\n\nLet me list each digit's place and the result:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 4.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 2.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):7.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):7.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):7.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):3.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):0.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):8.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):0.\n\nPosition 10 (billions):1.\n\nWait, but when we add, the positions after the carryover:.\n\nWait, let me write the digits from right to left with their results:.\n\nStarting from the ones digit:.\n\nPosition 1: 4.\n\nPosition 2: 2 (from 12, with carryover).\n\nPosition 3:7 (5+1 +1).\n\nPosition4:1+6=7.\n\nPosition5:3+4=7.\n\nPosition6:6+7=13, so 3 with carryover 1.\n\nPosition7:5+4 +1=10, so 0 with carryover1.\n\nPosition8:4+3 +1=8.\n\nPosition9:3+7=10, so 0 with carryover1.\n\nPosition10: the carryover 1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:.\n\n4 (ones), 2 (tens), 7 (hundreds),7 (thousands),7 (ten thousands),3 (hundred thousands),0 (millions),8 (ten millions),0 (hundred millions),1 (billions). Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
