d:.\n\nLet me write them vertically:.\n\n        8 1 5 0 6 6 4 5 0.\n\n      + 5 1 8 8 2 4 3 1 9.\n\n      -------------------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):.\n\nDigit 1 (ones): 0 + 9 = 9.\n\nDigit 2 (tens):5 +1 =6.\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):4 +3 =7.\n\nDigit4 (thousands):6 +4 =10 → write 0, carryover 1.\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):6 +2 =8, plus carryover 1 →9.\n\nWait, wait, let me check again:.\n\nWait the first number\'s ten thousands place (digit5 from left, which is the 5th digit: starting from left, first digit is 8, so digit5 is the 6 in the ten thousands place (since digit4 is 0, which is hundred thousands). Wait, perhaps I need to do this step by step carefully.\n\nLet me list each digit position with their values:.\n\nFirst number (815,066,450):.\n\nPositions (from right, starting at 1):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 0.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):5.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):4.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):6.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):6.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):0.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):5.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):1.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):8.\n\nSecond number (518,824,319):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):9.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):1.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):3.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):4.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):2.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):8.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):8.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):1.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):5.\n\nWait, actually, if we consider the second number as 518,824,319, breaking it down:.\n\nDigits from left:.\n\n5 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 9 (ones). So positions from the right:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):9.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):3.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):2.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8.\n\nPosition7 (millions):8.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5.\n\nSo now, let\'s add each column from right to left, keeping track of carries.\n\nStarting with Position 1 (ones):.\n\n0 (from first number) +9 (second)=9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nPosition2 (tens):.\n\n5 +1=6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\n4 +3=7. Write 7, no carryover.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\n6 (first number) +4 (second)=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\n6 (first) +2 (second) +1 (carryover)=9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n0 (first) +8 (second)=8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\n5 (first) +8 (second)=13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\n1 (first) +1 (second) +1 (carryover)=3. Write 3, no carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\n8 (first) +5 (second)=13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nNow, since we\'ve reached the leftmost digit and there\'s a carryover of 1, we need to add a new digit to the left, so the total becomes 1 3 3 .. etc.\n\nPutting it all together from right to left, the digits are:.\n\nPosition1:9.\n\nPosition2:6.\n\nPosition3:7.\n\nPosition4:0.\n\nPosition5:9.\n\nPosition6:8.\n\nPosition7:3.\n\nPosition8:3.\n\nPosition9:3.\n\nPlus the carryover 1 as the next digit (trillions place). Therefore, my final answer is: ']]
