ndred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nWait, let me make sure I have the order correct. Since when writing numbers, the leftmost digit is the highest place value. So the carryover 1 is the billions place. Then the next digits follow.\n\nSo the result should be:.\n\n1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nPutting that together: 1,363,113,033?.\n\nWait, let me verify the digits again step by step to make sure I didn't transpose any numbers.\n\nLet me list each digit position step by step:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):.\n\n1. Ones: 6 + 7 = 13 → 3, carryover 1.\n2. Tens: 3 + 9 = 12 + 1 =13 →3, carryover1.\n3. Hundreds:7 +2=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n4. Thousands:2 +4=6 +1=7 →7, no carryover.\n5. Ten thousands:8 +3=11 →1, carryover1.\n6. Hundred thousands:3 +7=10 +1=11 →1, carryover1.\n7. Millions:9 +3=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n8. Ten millions:6 +9=15 +1=16 →6, carryover1.\n9. Hundred millions:3 +9=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n10. Billions: carryover1 →1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:.\n\n3 (ones), 3 (tens), 0 (hundreds),7 (thousands),1 (ten thousands),1 (hundred thousands),3 (millions),6 (ten millions),3 (hundred millions),1 (billions).\n\nSo when written left to right, it's 1,3 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 3 (ones). So the number is 1,363,113,033.\n\nWait, let me check the commas for clarity:.\n\nBreaking down 1,363,113,033:.\n\n- Billions: 1.\n- Hundred millions: 3.\n- Ten millions:6.\n- Millions:3.\n- Hundred thousands:1.\n- Ten thousands:1.\n- Thousands:3.\n- Hundreds:0.\n- Tens:3.\n- Ones:3.\n\nSo written out, that's 1,363,113,033.\n\nBut let me cross-verify this with another method to be sure. Maybe adding the numbers in parts.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can add the n