its).\n\nWait, hold on, commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. So the number 831,100,114 is structured as:\n\n8 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 1 (millions), comma, 1 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), comma, 1 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 4 (units).\n\nWait, actually, the first comma after the first three digits from the left. Wait, no, in standard notation, commas separate every three digits starting from the right. So:\n\n831,100,114 is:\n\n8 3 1, 1 0 0, 1 1 4.\n\nSo breaking down:\n\n- The first part (831) is hundreds of millions: 8 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 1 (millions).\n\n- The second part (100) is thousands: 1 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands).\n\n- The third part (114) is units: 1 (hundred), 1 (ten), 4 (unit).\n\nTherefore, the digits from left to right are:\n\nPosition1 (hundred millions):8,\n\nPosition2 (ten millions):3,\n\nPosition3 (millions):1,\n\nPosition4 (hundred thousands):1,\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):0,\n\nPosition6 (thousands):0,\n\nPosition7 (hundreds):1,\n\nPosition8 (tens):1,\n\nPosition9 (units):4.\n\nWait, so the previous digit-by-digit analysis was incorrect because I misassigned the digit positions.\n\nOh no! So my earlier mistake was in assigning the digit positions. I incorrectly considered the first number's ten millions place as 1 instead of 3. Let me correct this.\n\nLet me re-express both numbers with proper digit positions:\n\nFirst number: 831,100,114.\n\nDigits from right to left (units to hundred millions):\n\nPosition1 (units):4,\n\nPosition2 (tens):1,\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):1,\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0,\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):0,\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1,\n\nPosition7 (millions):1,\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3,\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8.\n\nSecond number: 359,223,061.\n\nDigits from right to left:\n\nPosition1 (units):1,\n\nPosition2 (tens):6,\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):0,\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3,\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):