d thousands place is 0 with carryover 1. So that's correct.\n\nPosition5:7+1 + carryover? Wait no, position5 is ten thousands place. The digits were 7 and1, plus no carryover except from position6 which had a carryover already handled. Wait, actually the carryover from position6 (hundred thousands) was the 1 that was added to position7. So position5 had 7+1=8, plus no carryover, so it's 9? Wait, in my earlier step-by-step, position5 had a carryover from position4.\n\nWait, let me recount each position carefully:.\n\nStarting over with columns:.\n\nColumn 1 (units):.\n\n3 + 8 =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nColumn 2 (tens):.\n\n3 (from first number) + 6 (second number) +1 (carryover) = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nColumn3 (hundreds):.\n\n9 +1 +1 (carryover) = 11. Write 1, carryover1.\n\nColumn4 (thousands):.\n\n4 +9 +1 =14. Write 4, carryover1.\n\nColumn5 (ten thousands):.\n\n7 +1 +1 (carryover?) Wait, the carryover from column4 is 1. So 7+1=8, plus 1 carryover gives 9. So write 9, no carryover.\n\nColumn6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n8 +2 =10. Write 0, carryover1.\n\nColumn7 (millions):.\n\n0 +5 +1 (carryover from column6) =6. Write6, no carryover.\n\nColumn8 (ten millions):.\n\n1 +7 =8. Write8.\n\nColumn9 (hundred millions):.\n\n1 +4 =5. Write5.\n\nSo combining all the digits from highest to lowest:.\n\n5 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions),6 (millions),9 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),1 (hundreds),0 (tens),1 (units).\n\nSo the number is 586,994,101?.\n\nWait, let me check the digits again step by step:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nUnits place: 1.\n\nTens: 0.\n\nHundreds:1.\n\nThousands:4.\n\nTen thousands:9.\n\nHundred thousands:0.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nTen millions:8.\n\nHundred millions:5.\n\nSo written out: 5 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 6 (millions), then comma, 9 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),4 (thousands), comma, 1 (hundreds),0 (tens),1 (units). So that's 586,994,101.\n\nHmm. Let me verify with another method. Maybe adding th