+1=5. Write5, carryover0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\n7 +6=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\n8 +9=17 +1=18. So write 8 and carryover1. But since there are no more digits, the carryover 1 becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nSo the total is 1 (from carryover) followed by 8 (from 18) in the hundred millions place, then the rest of the digits.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me clarify:.\n\nWhen we add the hundred millions place (position9):.\n\n8 (from first number) +9 (from second) =17, plus the carryover 1 from position8's addition (which was 13, so carryover1). Wait, no, position8's addition was 7+6=13, so carryover1 to the next position (position9). So:.\n\nPosition9: 8 +9 =17, plus the carryover1 from position8: total 18. So 18 here. So 18 in the hundred millions place. Since it's two digits, the '1' is a carryover to a new digit (billions place), and '8' remains in the hundred millions. So the total sum would have a 1 in the billions place, followed by 8 in the hundred millions, then the other digits.\n\nTherefore, the digits from highest to lowest (billions, hundred millions, .., units):.\n\nBillions: 1.\n\nHundred millions:8.\n\nTen millions:3 (from position8's result).\n\nMillions:5.\n\nHundred thousands:7.\n\nTen thousands:0.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nHundreds:2.\n\nTens:2.\n\nUnits:0.\n\nWait, let me list all digits in order from the highest to the lowest:.\n\nStarting with the carryover 1, then the 8 from the hundred millions place, then the 3 from ten millions, then 5 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 2 (tens), and 0 (units). Wait, but let me check each position again step by step.\n\nWait, perhaps I should list all the results digit by digit:.\n\nAfter adding each position, the digits from right to left (units to hundred millions and beyond) are as follows:.\n\nPosition1 (units): 0.\n\nPosition2 (tens):2.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):2.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands)